FlashProve: Hash-Based Proofs of LLM Inference at
400 Billion Parameters
This paper is based in part on a collaboration with Alessandro Chiesa, Giacomo Fenzi, and Guy
Weissenberg, who provided ideas and direction towards the current protocol.

Abstract
FlashProve is a hash-based commit-and-prove argument for language-model inference: a provider
commits to a model’s weights, then proves its greedy outputs correct. Three properties of the protocol are
machine-checked in Lean, at exact match, in the random-oracle model, for a configuration fixed before the
oracle is sampled, each under the conditions stated in Section 1.3: index-binding knowledge soundness
(error about q/2124 + 2−101 after q queries) and, for one request per proof, completeness on served requests
(error at most 2−40 ) and zero knowledge (error below 2−154 , up to 688,127 replies and 264 hash queries);
one bit per request, which depends on the weights, is revealed. C++ and CUDA programs produce proofs
for a 400-billion-parameter mixture-of-experts integer model on public weights, on one H100 at a steady
rate of about 110 prefill tokens per second, in batches of 24 prompts, with the masking of the weights on;
each proof has 18.74 to 18.81 MB, checks one output token, and has been checked by the C++ verifier
alone. The theorems concern the protocol and its prover in Lean, not these programs; that the programs
likely hide the weights, at the default configuration, in honest runs, under conditions that the release’s
documentation lists, is our judgement, not a proof, and does not cover a real model’s weights.

