Introduction

1.1

The problem

A client of a hosted language model has difficulty checking which model produced its text [CSZS25]: the
weights are secret.
In FlashProve, a hash-based commit-and-prove argument [CFQ19], the provider publishes a commitment
to its weights M and proves, for a prompt x and an output y, that y is the greedy output of committed weights
on x (at exact match; Section 1.3 says which weights). The task resembles that of a function-hiding functional
commitment [BNO21]. Since floating-point inference differs between machines [DiFR], the proofs concern an
integer model with the shape of Llama 4 Maverick (Definition 3.1). Exact match requires the greedy token at
every token position; a clipped-gap policy, which builds on the logit margin of Token-DiFR [DiFR], bounds
the clipped logit gaps on average (Definition 3.4).
The goals are soundness and zero knowledge, formally index privacy (Definition 2.14); what is proved,
after compilation, is zero knowledge with request leakage (Definition 6.34; Remark 6.40). By design, every
published value that depends on the weights is masked or enters only through a salted hash, apart from a
declared list (the release’s docs/what-is-not-hidden.md; Definition H.1) and from what a client learns by
design; in Lean zero knowledge is proved for the protocol with one request per proof, under exact match
(Section 6.5, with its bounds), and nothing for the programs: that the programs reveal nothing more is the
design’s aim, not an established property.

1.2

Overview of the protocol

Informally, the protocol (Section 4) is as follows.
• Masked encoding. The weights, written as a matrix of digits, are encoded row by row, over a 64-bit
prime field, by a Reed–Solomon code with t random lowest coefficients ([CFWpc]; [CY24, Section 32.5];
[CFW26]). Any t columns of this committed table are independent of the weights; t is the opening budget
(Definition 4.2).
• Copies. Every proof opens columns, hence the provider makes copies with fresh randomness ([CFWpc];
[CFW26]). An offline proof shows that the first table decodes to a matrix in range; a link proof, that
a copy decodes to the same matrix; an online proof, that the output meets the policy at that matrix
(Definition 4.6 and Definition 4.7).
• Out-of-domain samples. The proximity tests [CFW26] run beyond unique decoding, strictly below the
Johnson radius, where fewer opened columns suffice but a word can be close to several codewords.
Masked linear forms in each table’s rows, published at a point derived from its root, determine one of
these up to a small error (Definition 4.5; [CFWpc]; the masks: [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026,
Section 9.2]; the technique: [BGKS20], as in [ACFY24b, Section 4.4]).
• Compilation. Rounds that sample positions have proof of work (Construction 4.13; [CFWpc], with
published uses in [Sta21, Section 3.11.3], [ACFY24a, Section 6.2] and [ACFY24b, Section 6.2]). Merkle
trees and the Fiat–Shamir transformation make the proofs non-interactive [BCS16, COS20].
• Cost. The weights, not the tokens, dominate the cost [LY26]: the table is committed once, and a batch
opens one copy once for all its requests, in at most νon columns (Table 2), as in Brakedown [GLSTW23]
and TensorSwitch [BFRW25] (see also [CFW26]). The model’s run is proved segment by segment, so
that one GPU’s memory can suffice (Appendix E.2).

1.3

Results

Statements here are informal; Table 1 lists the theorems by object, and Section 1.4 gives their chief limits. The
theorems, machine-checked in Lean, concern the protocol as Lean states it; the C++ and CUDA programs are
tested and measured, not proved. Under human direction, AI systems wrote this paper and almost all of the
programs and the Lean library, and made every test, calculation, reading and review that it reports; as far as
we know, no person has checked these tests, calculations, readings and reviews (Who wrote and checked this).

