The protocol. FlashProveZ consists of three interactive oracle proofs (IOPs; Construction 4.13), with
honest provers written in Lean as definitions, the Lean provers (Definition 6.14); compilation turns them into
the compiled Lean argument Πlp
Z : a committer, a prover and the verifier VZ , which is also the verifier of ΠZ
(Construction 4.15 and Definition 6.15). ΠZ is the argument whose committer forms several copies and whose
prover serves batches; in the Lean statements of Section 5 its committer and prover are constant functions
(Remark 6.16).
The configuration Z, three byte strings called its files, states all parameters and the policy; an admissible
configuration equals the default one, Z0 , up to its scales and eight numbers, within stated bounds (Definition 3.8).
Its files are in use if, moreover, the policy is exact match, which fixes three of the eight numbers, and the
parameter checks pass, without which VZ rejects (Definition 6.11); those of Z0 , as written out in Lean, are in
use (Proposition 6.17).
Three properties are machine-checked, in the random-oracle model: soundness, a property of the verifier
alone, and completeness and zero knowledge, for the protocol whose verifier is proved sound: their provers are
those of the IOPs from which VZ is compiled, and VZ itself judges the compiled prover, all of one compiled
system, Πlp
Z (Remark 6.46).
Main theorem (informal; Theorem 5.1, machine-checked). Let Z be an admissible configuration
with exact match, fixed before the oracle is sampled. In the random-oracle model with a second
hash function H ′ fixed (Assumption 4.11), there is an extractor that reads weights M ∗ off the
commitment and the committer’s hash queries, before any proof is made, such that for all committers
and provers whose bounds on queries add up to at most q
