Pr VZ accepts (x, y), and y is not a greedy output of M ∗ on x ≤ ε(q) ≈ q/2124 + 2−101 .
The property is index-binding knowledge soundness (Definition 2.7; Remark 2.8 (Names of the properties)).
The weights M ∗ need not be the committer’s own, no witness is extracted, and the extractor is a function, not
shown computable (Remark 5.3, Reading the theorem). For the committer compiled from the Lean provers
(Definition 6.15), at files in use (Definition 6.11), within its budget of 248 hash queries (Proposition 6.33), the
extractor gives back the committed weights except with probability about 2−155 (Theorem 6.31).
Completeness (informal; Theorem 6.29, machine-checked). Let the files of Z be in use. At every
served request, that is, a well-formed request whose claimed output is the model’s result on it
(Definition 6.12), VZ rejects the Lean prover’s proof with probability at most 2−40 , over the hash
function and the committer’s and the prover’s coins.
Zero knowledge (informal; Theorem 6.35 and Theorem 6.37, machine-checked). Let the files of Z
be in use. Against any verifier with at most 264 hash queries, none of them before the commitment,
and up to 688,127 answered requests (replies) on one copy of the committed table: if every request
is served, one simulator, fixed before the weights, that gets each request and the largest number of
replies, and beside these nothing of the weights, makes a view within 2−154 of the real one; it may
depend on the files of Z, and whether a request is served is not hidden. At all requests the same
holds of a simulator that gets besides one bit for each request: served or not.
Both are in the model of the main theorem, with the files of Z and the weights fixed before the hash
function is sampled, and are stated for one request per proof, one commitment, one copy of the committed
table and fresh random masks (Remark 6.42). The bit says whether the claimed output is the model’s result
(Remark 6.44). The property is zero knowledge with request leakage (Definition 6.34); neither it nor the form
before compilation is stated to be index privacy (Remark 6.40). On served requests completeness and zero
knowledge are stated through the texts of two definitions shaped with a person, each with one condition more,
that the request can be served (Definition 3.11); with the condition that the output is the model’s, these are
the served requests; the forms at every request are the Lean library’s own (Remark 6.47 and Limitation G.7).
Further machine-checked statements, and the check. Table 1 gives the properties before compilation
and on the commitment; Section 5.5 and Section 6 have results on other objects, each with its limits.

