• Batches and policies. A proof made in a batch, which VZ verifies singly, is covered at ε(q), and N proofs
sent at once at N ε(q) (Corollary 5.10). At every admissible Z fixed before the oracle is sampled, with
the error ε(q), an accepted output meets the policy of Z under M ∗ (Theorem 5.11 and Remark 5.12).
For a clipped-gap policy (Definition 3.4), under which a stock engine’s replies would be checked, this
says only that an accepted output’s token costs meet the policy’s limits at the extracted weights; it does
not prove that such an output is the model’s own.
• A verifier that runs. An executable form, a Lean term that can be evaluated, equals VZ at every hash
function with 32-byte values (Theorem 5.5), so the bound ε(q) holds for its own decision when its hash
function is a random oracle (Corollary B.11). It runs as the command lean-verify; its file parser,
and Lean’s evaluator, compiler and runtime that execute it, are trusted, not proved (Remark 5.9 and
Remark B.10).
• The main theorem’s proof. Steps 4 to 9 of VZ are the output of the Lean library’s compiler at three
interactive oracle proofs (Remark B.7, Which compiler); the arithmetization of the whole integer model,
the lemma on that compiler (Lemma A.15) and the coding theory below the Johnson radius (Lemma 5.13
and Lemma A.23; the first through files adapted from ArkLib and Mathlib, Appendix E.4) all lie inside
the proof. As printed, the theorem joins two Lean theorems (Section 5, head).
• The check. Lean’s kernel accepts the proofs, with the provisos of Section 1.4 and Remark B.17, and Lean
prints no axiom beyond its three standard ones for the theorems that lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md names.
An independent proof checker has accepted the soundness theorem on verify, sound_of_verify_at
(the form of Theorem 5.1 that leaves the extractor open: Section 5, head), with every declaration
beneath it, and nothing that does not lie beneath it, in a pass on that theorem’s export alone: the pass
was made over a copy of the compiled files of the released Lean files’ build (that the compiled files it
read were that build’s is known from a report alone) (Remark B.17, Checkers).
The programs. The released programs are cp_commit, cp_prove and cp_verify (the committer, the
prover and the C++ verifier) as the release’s sources build them; the timed runs are among the runs that are
told as runs on them: the programs that made these runs were built from sources that differ from the released
ones in comments, in the names of identifiers and files, in quoted texts that hold such a name and in the

4

wording of messages, and in nothing else (Appendix H.1, “Which programs ran”). A dagger († ) marks a result
not on the released programs, of any other programs, which is not shown to hold for the released programs
(Section 7.1). Most of these other programs are built from other sources: source text of this project’s programs
that differs from the released text in more than the ways that Appendix H.1, “Which programs ran”, lists.
In the timed runs (Appendix H.1) the C++ verifier (Section 7.1) accepted every proof (Section 7.2). In no
reported test did the C++ verifier program accept an output that violates the run’s own policy (Table 7 and
Table 4); most of these tests ran not on the released programs (Section 7.1).
• Honest proofs. The executable form accepted (by lean-verify; four by a separate build of the form
alone) every honest proof of Section 7.3, at the size of Z0 , on synthetic weights: 41 of the timed runs’
own as reported, at exact match; and, by other provers† , 363 and 18 at exact match and, where an
acceptance says less, 1 and 4 under a clipped-gap policy: the first of each pair by one prover built from
other sources, the second by other provers than that one (Section 7.3). One proof made at the default
configuration’s size, on synthetic weights, by the programs that the release’s sources build, as reported,
was accepted by cp_verify and, through lean-verify, by the executable form (Appendix H.1).
• False claims. The released verifier rejected all 57 proof files that malicious provers made at the default
configuration’s size, at exact match and under one commitment, on synthetic weights, and that were
put to it, 24 of them claiming a false output (Section 7.6); in the timed runs, on the public weights, the
programs rejected 240 of 240 altered copies of honest files and 9 of 9 altered calls. lean-verify and a
separate build of the executable form together rejected all 26 proofs† of claims false by the run’s own
policy, all from one test program, each rejected where the proof file is read (Section 7.3).
• Weight hiding, the programs’ goal (Definition H.1). We judge that weight hiding likely holds at the
default configuration, in honest runs, if the provider keeps its operating conditions and the judgement’s
own conditions (listed in docs/limits.md, not in this paper) hold. That judgement rests on the design,
what is proved in Lean for the protocol, code review and tests; it is not a proof, and it does not cover a
real model’s weights (Section 7.5).
Measurements. The figures are of those timed runs (Appendix H.1) that used a third party’s public,
mostly 4-bit weights: on one H100, at the default configuration as fitted to the weights (Section 3.3), at
exact match, on windows of 1,024 tokens, with masking on (read off the runs’ own summaries, not tested:
Appendix H.2); each proof checks one output token. Rates count prefill tokens; ± separates the mean and the
sample standard deviation (Section 7.2).
• In batches of 24 requests the steady rate is 111.23 ± 0.92 tokens/second (n = 3), from 110.20 to 111.99;
counted from the program’s start, over five batches, it is 103.60 ± 0.84. The ± 0.92 is the standard
deviation of three runs made side by side: three runs of the same kind on other programs, on another
day on other machines, had a standard deviation of 3.9 (Limitation G.8). With one request in a batch
the steady rate is 17.41 ± 0.15 tokens/second (n = 3). These rates are counted up to the moment at
which the prover hands out its proofs; it runs no verifier, and a serving script’s check comes on top
(Section 7.2).
• A proof file has 18.74 to 18.81 MB; a client also downloads, once, the key file and the one copy’s file,
6.24 MB together; the C++ verifier checks a proof in 21.14 ± 0.03 seconds called with its option for one
thread, on the prover’s machine (n = 3).
• Per GPU, proving alone takes 93.0 times (92.2 to 94.1 over all pairs of runs) the GPU time of stock
vLLM for prefill at the highest load tried (n = 3 prover runs, 8 engine runs; calculated; FP8 checkpoint,
eight H100s). The multiple omits committing and serving and could be higher (Section 7.8).
For its own integer model of Llama 4 Maverick, on other hardware and for a different statement, VerInf’s
README [VerInf] reports 14.3 h for one proof on 1,000 tokens and a proof of 93.6 GB (Table 8); each proof
of the timed runs on the public weights checks one output token.
Credit. The definition of the primitive, the plan of the protocol (an offline phase, a phase for each batch,
and link proofs between committed tables for the same weights), its zero-knowledge proximity tests [CFW26],
and the step beyond unique decoding with out-of-domain samples, including the sample taken when a table is
