1.4

Limits and open problems

The release lists the gaps that we know of, and no attack on the protocol is known to us at any gap while its
conditions are kept, the opening budget among them (Limitation 8.4, Opening budget). Almost no attack
was sought: that none is described is not a finding of safety.
At least four things stand between the theorems and the measured runs. Exact match requires that the
integer model itself be served: of the 96 honest replies scored from each of two stock engines none would
pass it† (Section 7.4). The runs prove batches, with masks derived from a seed, which completeness and
zero knowledge do not cover (Remark 6.42 and Remark G.15). Of the timed runs’ proofs only 41, all from
the runs on synthetic weights, have been put to the Lean verifier, over the released Lean files; it accepted
each (Section 7.3). That the programs run Lean’s protocol is tested, not proved, and differences are known
(Limitation 8.2).
What the theorems do not say.
• The programs. The theorems concern Lean terms: VZ , its extractor, and the Lean provers, which
are definitions, not programs that run (Remark 6.43); the paper does not print the provers, and no
statement compares two of the three provers with the honest provers that it describes (Definition 6.14
and Remark 6.16). Nothing is proved about the released programs, and no security level is claimed for
them (Section 8.1).
• The model. That a second hash function is fixed, and the oracle is taken as independent of it, is an
assumption that is neither tested nor proved (Assumption 4.11 and Remark 5.3), nothing is claimed
against a quantum adversary (Limitation G.6), and Fiat–Shamir attacks on protocols of this kind have
not been assessed (Limitation G.5).
• The configuration. Exact match is a hypothesis that VZ does not test, and the provider’s scale file, with
five numbers of the parameter file (Definition 3.7), fixes which integer model is meant, and the scale file
can disable any chosen blocks of it: the client must check these, and that y is as long as expected, since
any prefix of the model’s output is a greedy output (Remark 5.3, Limitation 8.6, and Limitation G.4).
The provider, not the verifier, chooses the configuration, and a commitment does not say whose weights
it holds (Limitation 8.5 and Limitation 8.7). A choice of the configuration after querying the oracle,
which is open to every provider, is not covered (Remark 5.3 and Limitation 8.5). For proofs sent one
after another no bound is stated (Remark B.13).
• Trained weights. No theorem says that a run on a trained model’s weights meets its hypotheses, and no
proof for such weights has been put to the executable form. The measured runs, on the public weights,
are at a configuration fitted to those weights (Section 3.3); the parameter checks were not evaluated at
it (Remark D.5). Nor are such weights shown to meet the model’s range conditions, which limits what
can be proved of such a model, not soundness (Remark 3.3 and Remark D.5).
• The integer model. How far the integer model and the floating-point model differ is stated by no theorem:
on the public weights its greedy token differs from that of the 16-bit weights, evaluated in float32,
at 6.81 % of token positions on 216 texts and at 9.41 % on the 24 natural passages among them (a
Python implementation† , no proofs), and its task quality has not been measured (Section 7.7 and
Limitation G.9). All texts are built on WikiText-2 [MXBS17], on which the scales were fitted.
• Completeness. It covers served requests alone, not batches, and that some request is served is shown at
constructed weights. Where the model has no result, or another result than the claimed output, nothing
is stated: the Lean prover returns the empty proof, which proves nothing and which a prover can also
return at a request that it could serve (Remark 6.30; Limitation 8.9). A gap in completeness costs a
rejection, with the provisos of Limitation 8.9.
• Zero knowledge. Batches, a further commitment, after compilation a second copy of the committed table,
clipped-gap policies, masks derived from a seed, a verifier’s hash queries before the commitment and
6

the C++ programs are not covered, and one bit per request, which depends on the weights, is revealed
(Section 6.5). Over many requests the bits tell what the model answers and where it has no result, and
no statement bounds what many bits reveal together (Remark 6.44). The simulators’ running time is
not modelled, and nothing is said of timing, or of what the files of Z tell of the weights (Remark 6.45),
or of what the requests tell: each served request holds the model’s own output (Remark 6.36); the
released tools compute the scale file and lines of the parameter file from the weights, and no statement
bounds what they reveal (Remark 6.1).
• Clipped-gap policies. A clipped-gap policy bounds a sum and a count, not each token (Limitation 8.8).
As in every commit-and-prove argument, the committer chooses the weights. By itself, Theorem 5.11
therefore excludes nothing against a committer who commits to weights under which every output meets
the policy (Remark 5.12). Some policies of admissible configurations exclude no token (Proposition B.1).
VZ does not reject a configuration with such a policy, and the C++ verifier rejects some such policies
but not every one: no verifier sees the model’s logits (Remark B.2).
• Paper and Lean. This paper gives VZ in outline, and that its words match the Lean terms is checked by
AI reading, not by Lean, except for the policy’s three numbers (Remark D.10 and Remark 5.3).
• How the proofs are checked. Over a build of the released Lean files the proofs of completeness, zero
knowledge and the theorem on the commitment have been checked by Lean’s kernel alone (Remark 6.48);
a whole run of the library’s full check over all but one of the released Lean files (Remark B.17) passed
at every step; its last line says that no theorem got a verdict on binders or on Classical.choice (the
whole line, and what the check does not cover: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md; Remark B.17). The pinned
version of Lean, 4.29.1, is older than the published fixes (from version 4.32.1 on) of soundness bugs in
Lean’s kernel and runtime and in the big-number library (GMP) that its Linux builds link (Remark B.17).
A re-checker, leanchecker, which is not the independent checker, stopped on 23 modules of a separate
Lean library (counted over Lean files that differ from the released ones), which Lean’s kernel accepts
at a thread stack larger than the default; by import lines at least one lies beneath each theorem of
Section 6.5 on completeness, zero knowledge and the commitment and beneath Proposition 6.17, part 1,
none beneath the soundness theorems of Section 5 (Remark 5.7; the finer finding, by a walk over the
constants: Remark 6.48).
What is tested, not proved.
• That the C++ programs run Lean’s protocol (Limitation 8.2). In tests the two verifiers, from whichever
programs (Table 4 says which), returned the same decision on every proof file that both judged. Of
four matched alterations of one honest proof† , each of which left its claim true, they agreed on one and
disagreed on three (these four are not common files: the C++ verifier read the altered raw proof, the
form the parsed file altered at the matching place), where the C++ verifier was the stricter. VZ has
sixteen tests that the C++ verifier, which clients run by default, lacks (lean/GAPS.md, gap 25), and the
one comparison of hash calls matched 881,280 of 1,166,983, a gap that the release rates as heavy.
• That VZ accepts the programs’ proofs. None of the 363, 18, 1 and 4 is shown to be the released programs’
own, and the 363 are of little variety; the 41 are the timed runs’ own, all on synthetic weights. All but
those put to it in the reference run and the 41 of the timed runs (Appendix H.3) were judged over Lean
files that differ from the released ones or are not shown to be these (lean/GAPS.md, gap 26).
• That the C++ verifier is sound. The tests are few. Test programs that bypass the verifier program and
call the library beneath it directly, against the library’s stated contract, did have false claims accepted,
by at least two ways; that the verifier program itself never calls it so rests on AI readings of source
text that differs in places from the released one, not on a test (Section 7.6). None of these ways went
through the verifier program, so a client should check proofs with that program, not through the library.
• That the programs hide the weights. Of the judgement’s five tests two have run on the released programs.
The tests ran at the reduced configuration (Section 3.3), on synthetic weights; only one of the five has
also run on a real model’s weights, once on the released programs and three times on other programs† .
None searches for a leak: they test that the masks are there and that a loss is noticed (Table 6).

7

Of the injected losses† (Table 6), 8 of 8 (none a loss of a single pad) stopped the committer, 130 of 130
in GPU memory stopped, and 263 of 263 riskier losses stopped. Losses were unnoticed in three places:
30 of 165 injected losses of random values in CPU memory; 9 of 9 at samples that no self-check covers;
and, with one self-check disabled, at a parameter file that counted no proof of work, a commitment
without masks, which passed every other check. Neither the programs nor the judgement has had an
independent review.
What is not covered. Openings beyond the budget, or a lost mask, give away exact linear equations,
unnoticed (Limitation 8.4, Limitation G.13, and Limitation G.14). Weight hiding rests on operating conditions
of the provider, most of which no program can check, and the shipped vLLM integration breaks at least one
(Limitation G.16). Section 8.1 and Appendix G state further limits, Section 8.2 the open problems.

2

Preliminaries and definitions

2.1

The random-oracle model

Definition 2.1 (Random oracle, query bound, trace). A hash function is a function H : {0, 1}∗ → {0, 1}λ ,
with λ = 256. The random oracle is the hash function with independent uniform values at distinct inputs.
An oracle algorithm is a finite rooted tree whose leaves are labelled by outputs. An inner node is a query
node, labelled by a string bs ∈ {0, 1}∗ , with one child for each element of {0, 1}λ , or a sampling node. A run
under H descends from the root, at a query node to the child H(bs) and at a sampling node to a uniformly
random child, and outputs the leaf’s label. Its query–answer trace tr lists the pairs (bs, H(bs)) of its query
nodes, in order.
A tree is deterministic if it has no sampling node, and a q-query algorithm if no path has more than q
query nodes. An algorithm with inputs is a family of trees, and has either property if every tree has.

2.2

Codes and mutual correlated agreement

Definition 2.2 (Mutual correlated agreement). Let K be a finite field, D a finite set of positions, C ⊆ KD
a linear code, k ≥ 2 an integer, and G a distribution on Kk , the generator. A word g ∈ KD agrees with C on a
set S ⊆ D if g coincides on S with some codeword. For δ ∈ [0, 1), the mutual correlated agreement (MCA)
error εmca (C, G, δ) is the maximum, over all words g1 , . . . , gk ∈ KD , of
h
i
P
Pr
∃ S ⊆ D, |S| ≥ (1 − δ) |D| :
j coj gj agrees with C on S and some gj does not .
(co1 ,...,cok )←G

The code C has mutual correlated agreement at the proximity parameter δ with error
ε, under G, if
√
εmca (C, G, δ) ≤ ε. The Johnson radius of a code of relative minimum distance ∆ is 1 − 1 − ∆.
Mutual correlated agreement was introduced in [ACFY24b, Definition 4.9], for proximity generators; it
strengthens the correlated agreement of [BCIKS20, Section 1.3]. Definition 2.2 follows [CFW26, version of
February 25, 2026, Definition 3.14], which defines the MCA error as a maximum over the words. In [BCGM25,
Definitions 3.10 and 3.14] a generator is a function of a random seed; here it is the distribution of the
coefficients. The Johnson radius is as in [ABF26, Section 1].

2.3

Interactive oracle proofs, compilation, proof of work

Definition 2.3 (Interactive oracle proof, batch). An interactive oracle proof (IOP) with external oracle
strings has an input, the IOP instance, and a fixed number of rounds. Its external oracle strings are arbitrary,
fixed before the first round, and not sent. In each round the prover sends an oracle part (a string) and a plain
part; the verifier answers with a uniform challenge from a finite set. Finally the verifier accepts or rejects;
it reads the IOP instance, the plain parts, the challenges, and the positions of the oracle strings that these
determine.
A batch IOP for at most R IOP instances marks each round as shared or own: a shared round has one
challenge for all of them, an own round one for each. Each prover message has a shared component and one
8

own component per instance. The verifier decides on each from its local transcript: the shared components,
its own components, and its challenges.
Interactive oracle proofs were introduced in [BCS16, Section 4], which states that [RRR16] independently
introduces the same notion, as probabilistically checkable interactive proofs.
Definition 2.4 (Compilation (BCS transformation)). The compilation Compile(Q, H) of an IOP Q
(Definition 2.3) under a hash function H is the following non-interactive argument; its queries to H are of
three kinds (leaf, inner node, Fiat–Shamir), coded injectively with disjoint ranges.
1. Each oracle string is committed by the root of a Merkle tree whose leaves hash a symbol and a random
salt. The external roots are an input of the verifier.
2. The first Fiat–Shamir query holds the external roots, a salt and the IOP instance. The query of round
m holds a chaining value from the preceding answer and the round’s roots and plain part; its answer
gives the round’s challenge and the next chaining value.
3. The proof string consists of that salt, the other roots, the plain parts and, for each position read, symbol,
salt and authentication path.
4. The verifier recomputes the challenges, checks the paths, and decides as the verifier of Q does.
Definition 2.5 (Proof of work (grinding)). Round m of an IOP (Definition 2.3) has κm bits of proof of
work if its plain part contains a nonce, a component that the verifier does not read, and the verifier rejects
unless κm designated bits of its challenge are 0. The compiled prover (Definition 2.4) tries values of the nonce,
up to a fixed number, until these bits are 0.

2.4

Commit-and-prove arguments and their properties

Definition 2.6 (Indexed relation, commit-and-prove argument). A token is a natural number. An
instance is a pair (x, y) of finite lists of tokens, the prompt and the output. An indexed relation consists of a
set I of indices, a set Rel of triples (M, x, y) with M ∈ I (there is no witness), and a function pp on I, the
public parameters of the index. The language of M is Lang(M ) = {(x, y) : (M, x, y) ∈ Rel}, and Lang(⊥) = ∅.
A commit-and-prove argument for this relation is a tuple Π = (Rnd, Commit, P, V ) of oracle algorithms
(Definition 2.1):
1. RndH outputs commitment randomness r;
2. CommitH (M, r) is deterministic and outputs a commitment c ∈ {0, 1}λ ;
3. P H (M, r, x, y) outputs a proof π;
4. V H (c, x, y, π) is deterministic and outputs 1 (accept) or 0 (reject).
The honest committer on M samples r ← RndH , publishes c = CommitH (M, r) and keeps (M, r) for the honest
prover P . In the general form, instances and commitments range over arbitrary sets, and P takes a list of
instances.
Indexed relations are as in [CHMMVW19, Section 3.1], where a triple holds an index, an instance and a
witness.
Definition 2.7 (Index-binding knowledge soundness). Let Π be a commit-and-prove argument with
verifier V for an indexed relation with index set I and languages Lang(·) (Definition 2.6), and let ε : N → [0, ∞].
An extractor is a function Ext from pairs of a commitment and a query–answer trace to I ∪ {⊥}. For oracle
algorithms AC , the adversarial committer, and AP , the adversarial prover, the experiment is:
1. sample the random oracle H;
2. run (c, st) ← AH
C , with query–answer trace trC ;
3. set M ∗ = Ext(c, trC );
4. run (x, y, π) ← AH
P (st);

9

5. set acc = V H (c, x, y, π).
The argument Π is index-binding knowledge sound for the relation, with error ε, if there is an extractor Ext
such that, for all qC , qP ∈ N, every qC -query committer AC and every qP -query prover AP ,


Pr acc = 1 and (x, y) ∈
/ Lang(M ∗ ) ≤ ε(qC + qP ).
Remark 2.8 (Names of the properties). The terms index-binding knowledge soundness, correctness of extraction
and index privacy are due to [CFWpc], and Definition 2.6, Definition 2.7, Definition 2.12, Definition 2.13,
and Definition 2.14 are adapted from that work; the name and the requirement of Definition 2.9 are taken
from it too, and its experiment and error are this paper’s. Here the word knowledge refers to the index: the
relation has no witness, and the extractor returns an index or ⊥. The names of the roles, index committer,
index verification key and index extractor, are from the same work. Table 12 lists uses of the same or like
names in the literature, of other objects. From here on an index committer is called a committer, and an
index extractor an extractor. The commitment is the 256-bit value that has the role of the index verification
key. Such a key may be all that a committer publishes; the commitment is narrower: in the theorem it is
these 256 bits alone.
Definition 2.9 (Correctness of extraction). Let Π = (Rnd, Commit, P, V ) be a commit-and-prove
argument with index set I (Definition 2.6), Ext an extractor (Definition 2.7), εext ∈ [0, 1], and H the random
oracle (Definition 2.1). Π has correctness of extraction for Ext with error εext if, for every M ∈ I fixed before
H is sampled,
