It is complete with error α if it is complete on the set of all triples.
In what follows SD is the statistical distance.
Definition 2.13 (Real and ideal experiment). Let Π be an argument in the general form (Definition 2.6),
with arbitrary indices and one or several independent random oracles H. A batch is a list of instances, each
marked revealed or hidden; P maps it to a proof or ⊥ for each instance. For an oracle algorithm V ∗ , a function
Env, the environment, and N ∈ N, the real experiment RealΠ (M, Env, V ∗ , N ) is:
10

1. sample H; the honest committer, on M , outputs c and keeps sk = (M, r) (Definition 2.6);
2. run V ∗ H (c), which asks for a batch at most N times;
3. each time, set bt = Env(view); if bt = ⊥, stop;
4. run P H (sk, bt) and give V ∗ the revealed instances with their proofs;
5. output view: c, what V ∗ was given, its query–answer trace and its coins.
In the ideal experiment IdealSim (M, Env, V ∗ , N ) a stateful Sim replaces H, committer and prover (so the
random oracle is programmable). Besides the queries of V ∗ , which it answers, it is given only the leakage:
pp(M ), N , and each batch’s revealed instances.
Definition 2.14 (Index privacy). Let S assign to each index M of a set J a set SM of instances, let R ≥ 1
and ζ : N2 → [0, ∞]. An environment is valid at M if each of its batches has at most R instances, all in SM .
An argument Π has index privacy on S for batches of at most R, with error ζ, if a simulator Sim exists such
that, for all M ∈ J , all environments Env valid at M , all q, N ∈ N and every q-query V ∗ ,

SD RealΠ (M, Env, V ∗ , N ), IdealSim (M, Env, V ∗ , N ) ≤ ζ(q, N )
(Definition 2.13), and in the ideal experiment equal queries receive equal answers.
What Section 6.5 proves of zero knowledge after compilation is stated for another notion, zero knowledge
with request leakage (Definition 6.34), and there in two statements: on the served instances with a constant
leakage (Theorem 6.35), and on all instances, those that are not served among them (Definition 6.12): at such
an instance the prover returns the empty proof, and the simulator is told, for each instance, whether it is
served, which depends on the weights (Theorem 6.37, with its bounds; Remark 6.40). Completeness says
nothing at such an instance (Remark 6.30).

3

The relation: model, policies, configuration

The complete definitions of the integer model, the placement rule, the arithmetization and the parameter checks
are terms of the Lean development, which Appendix F names. This paper outlines them (Construction D.3
and Construction D.9, Definition 4.1 and Definition 4.14); Remark D.10 (Given in outline) lists what it does
not print. Every admissible configuration is Z0 with its scale file, five of its model parameters and the three
numbers of its policy replaced (Definition 3.7 and Definition 3.8); the layout file and all sizes of Table 2 are
those of Z0 .

3.1

The integer model

The integer model is the function that one term of the Lean development computes. The release’s
docs/model.md describes it step by step, and Construction D.3 outlines it, without defining it.
Definition 3.1 (Integer model). The integer model (Construction D.3) is a transformer in integer arithmetic
with window length L = 210 and vocabulary V = {0, . . . , 202047}. Its weights M are integer matrices of fixed
shapes whose entries lie in fixed intervals that contain 0; their set is I. It rescales by its model parameters µ
(Definition 3.6). A context is a list x̄ of at least one and at most L tokens of V. At valid µ and M ∈ I, the
model is defined on x̄ if no intermediate value leaves its prescribed interval at any token position (the range
conditions); it then maps x̄ to a vector of logits logitM (x̄) ∈ ZV .
That Lean’s model function has a value exactly on such contexts is argued by hand; no Lean theorem states it.
Definition 3.2 (Greedy token, greedy output). Let µ be valid model parameters and M ∈ I (Definition 3.1). The greedy token after a context x̄ on which the model is defined is the least token of largest
logit,

greedyM (x̄) = min v ∈ V : logitM (x̄)[v] = maxv′ ∈V logitM (x̄)[v′ ] .
An output y = (y0 , y1 , . . . ) is a greedy output of M on a prompt x if, for every i < |y|, the list x ∥ (y0 , . . . , yi−1 )
is a context on which the model is defined, and yi is the greedy token after it. The relation of greedy outputs at
11

µ is the indexed relation (Definition 2.6) that consists of these triples (M, x, y), with index set I and constant
pp.
Remark 3.3 (Trained weights). The theorems’ weights are arbitrary elements of I, all-zero matrices among
them, and the theorems name no public model. No trained model’s weights are shown to meet the range
conditions, which limits what can be proved of such a model, not soundness. Remark D.5 (No real weights
shown) states what was counted and run.

3.2

Acceptance policies

The clipped-gap policies are modelled on Token-DiFR [DiFR], at temperature 0 (Appendix E.2).
Definition 3.4 (Logit gap, acceptance policy, exact match, clipped gap). Let µ, M , logitM and
greedyM be as in Definition 3.2. The logit gap of a token v ∈ V after a context x̄ on which the model is
defined is

gapM (x̄, v) = max
logitM (x̄)[v′ ] − logitM (x̄)[v] + [v′ < v] .
′
v ∈V

An acceptance policy is a triple pol = (C, θ, cap) of natural numbers: clip, threshold, cap.
Let x and y = (y0 , y1 , . . . ) be non-empty lists of tokens of V with |x|+|y| ≤ L+1. Put x̄i = x ∥ (y0 , . . . , yi−1 )
and nct = min(|y|, L − |x|); the counted tokens are y0 , . . . , ynct −1 . The output y is eligible on x under M if the
model is defined on every x̄i and, in case nct < |y|, yi = greedyM (x̄i ) at i = |y| − 1. Put gapi = gapM (x̄i , yi );
the cost of a counted token yi is costi = gapi + 28 . The output meets pol if it is eligible,
X
min(costi , C) ≤ θ nct ,
and
#{ i < nct : costi > C } ≤ cap.
i<nct

No other pair (x, y) meets a policy. Exact match is the policy (28 , 28 , 0); every other policy is a clipped-gap
policy.
Exact match forces every logit gap to 0, and the token after a full window has no cost.
Proposition 3.5 (Exact match and greedy outputs). [machine-checked] Let µ be valid model parameters,
M ∈ I, and x, y lists of tokens. The output y meets exact match on x under M (Definition 3.4) if and only
if y is non-empty and a greedy output of M on x (Definition 3.2). In that case y meets every acceptance
policy with θ ≥ 28 and C ≥ 28 .
Each of the two sentences is a single Lean theorem.
Outline. A logit gap is at least 0, and it is 0 exactly at the greedy token. At exact match, cap = 0 forces
costi ≤ 28 , hence gapi = 0, at every counted token, and an eligible output’s token after a full window is greedy.
Conversely, costs of 28 satisfy both bounds whenever θ, C ≥ 28 .

