Definition 3.7 (Configuration, default configuration). A configuration is a triple Z of byte strings: the
parameter file, the layout file and the scale file (their encoding: Definition D.1). The default configuration Z0
is one fixed configuration (Definition D.1). Its parameter file holds its policy (C, θ, cap), which is exact match
(Definition 3.4), and ϵ1 , ϵ2 , ϵF , MF , KF of its model parameters µ0 (Definition 3.6); its scale file holds the
rest of µ0 ; its layout file says where each cell (Definition 4.8) lies; a parameter check tests that each lies where
a Lookup (Construction D.6) reads it (Definition 4.14, item 3). For model parameters µ with the same ϵ1 and
ϵ2 in every layer and an acceptance policy pol, Z0 [µ, pol] denotes Z0 with these eight numbers and the scale
file replaced by those of µ and pol. Put Z1 = Z0 [µ0 , (25856, 512, 1)].
The release’s pages call the three byte strings of a configuration its public files.
Definition 3.8 (Admissible configuration). A configuration Z is an admissible configuration, written
Adm(Z) = 1, if Z = Z0 [µ, pol] (Definition 3.7) for valid model parameters µ (Definition 3.6) with one ϵ1 and
one ϵ2 for all layers and an acceptance policy pol = (C, θ, cap) with
256 ≤ θ ≤ C < 232

and

cap ≤ 1,024.

Then µZ = µ are the model parameters of Z, and polZ = pol is the policy of Z.
The configuration Z1 is admissible, and Proposition B.3 describes its policy. No theorem ties the shipped
files of Z1 to the Lean term (Remark D.2); five of the honest proofs of Section 7.3 are at these files. Section 7
uses two further kinds of configuration.
• A configuration is fitted to weights if its scale file and lines of its parameter file are computed from them
(Remark D.5, No real weights shown).
• A reduced configuration is one for a synthetic model far smaller than the integer model, for instance
with 8 layers and a window of 64 tokens; its parameter file states other sizes than that of Z0 , so it is not
an admissible configuration.

3.4

Policies that exclude nothing

Proposition 3.9 (All-zero weights). [machine-checked] Let µ be valid model parameters (Definition 3.6)
and pol = (C, θ, cap) an acceptance policy (Definition 3.4) with θ, C ≥ 257. Under the all-zero weights,
every non-empty output y meets pol on every non-empty prompt x, provided that all tokens lie in V, that
|x| + |y| ≤ L + 1, and that the last token of y is 0 if |x| + |y| = L + 1.
Outline. The model is defined on every context. With an all-zero output matrix all logits are 0, so token 0
is the greedy token, every logit gap is 0 or 1, and every cost is at most 257.
Remark 3.10 (What a policy bounds).
1. A clipped-gap policy bounds a sum and a count over the counted tokens of one output, which in a
soundness statement the prover chooses; each token is scored after the output’s own earlier tokens.
2. A logit gap is measured in the unit of the logits of the weights at hand, so what a policy excludes
depends on the weights (Proposition 3.9). The parameter file states that unit, as 2−15 nats at every
admissible configuration; the model does not read this number, and nothing in the proof ties it to the
weights.
3. The bound on the sum has the form of the test of Token-DiFR [DiFR], whose checker runs a model that
it knows, pools many outputs’ tokens and fits the clip to honest runs. Here each output is bounded
alone, and the provider writes the clip, so an output that meets the policy has not thereby passed that
test.

3.5

Instances that can be served

Definition 3.11 (Instances that can be served). Let µ be valid model parameters and M ∈ I
(Definition 3.1). An instance (x, y) with y non-empty and all tokens in V can be served at M if the integer
model at µ is defined, for M , on the first L = 1,024 tokens of x ∥ y ∥ 0 0 · · · . The set Servµ consists of all
(M, x, y) such that (x, y) can be served at M and y is a greedy output of M on x (Definition 3.2).
