The parameter file states the sizes of the tables and the bits of proof of work; every other value is calculated from the
parameter file and taken from no run; the rate ρ is rounded up, proximity parameters down (Remark D.14, How the
parameters are obtained).

Proposition 3.12 (Non-vacuity of the instances that can be served). [machine-checked]
(a) Valid model parameters exist, and for all valid µ the set Servµ (Definition 3.11) is not empty.
(b) There are valid model parameters µ and weights M ∈ I at which no instance can be served.
Remark 3.13 (Which instances can be served). Servµ leaves out the empty output, which is always a greedy
output; whether each of the other conditions of Definition 3.11 is needed for the honest proof to be accepted is
not known. At some valid model parameters and some weights no instance can be served (Proposition 3.12(b)).
Instances are proved to lie in Servµ at constructed weights and, at the model parameters of Z0 , at all valid
weights whose layers’ matrices have every entry in −800 .. 800: there an instance can be served exactly if its
tokens are in the vocabulary and its output is not empty. No statement says whether a real model’s weights
lie within that bound, and no instance is proved to lie in Servµ at a real model’s weights.

4

The protocol

Section 1.2 gives an overview of the protocol. This section gives its form, and Appendix D its rounds; what
of VZ the paper does not print is listed in Remark D.10. Sources are named where each idea is introduced;
Table 11 lists the parts of the protocol and their sources.

4.1

Parameters

Table 2 introduces the symbols for the sizes of the protocol. The parameter file states or determines them;
every admissible configuration has the values of Z0 .

4.2

Encoding and commitment

The weights are encoded once, with a masked code, a zero-knowledge Reed–Solomon encoding ([CFWpc];
[CY24, Section 32.5]; [CFW26]). In the literature’s terms, a committed table (Definition 4.2) is a codeword of a
14

2v -fold interleaved Reed–Solomon code, with the columns as its symbols. The masks are the pads and random
coefficients of the tables and the mask rows of the provers’ oracle messages (Construction D.6); Section 7.1
defines the mode masking on of a run. Any t columns of a table sampled with fresh randomness, together
with its OOD answers at an OOD seed chosen before the table is sampled, are distributed independently of
the matrix (Theorem 6.3; Remark 6.4); t + 1 columns are not (Proposition 6.5). This is the reason for the
opening budget t (Definition 4.2), a limit on the number of columns of one table that all proofs on it open in
total (Definition C.6; Remark C.7).
Definition 4.1 (Digit matrix). Each weight of M (Definition 3.1) is written in signed digits to the base
28 : four digits for an entry of the embedding matrix, two for every other weight. A public placement rule
(Remark D.10, Given in outline), fixed by the parameter file of Z, assigns to each digit of each weight one
entry of a matrix with d matrix rows and 2n matrix columns. An integer matrix A of this shape is in range if
every entry lies in [−27 , 27 ) and
b0 + 28 b1 ∈ [−212 , 212 )
for the entries b0 , b1 in each matrix column of each pair of matrix rows that the rule assigns to the low and
the high digits of two-digit weights. For A in range, wt(A) ∈ I denotes the weights that the rule reads off A.
A digit matrix of M is a matrix A in range with wt(A) = M ; it need not be unique.
Definition 4.2 (Masked encoding, committed table, opening budget). Let D ⊆ F with |D| ≥ ℓ + t.
The masked encoding of a message f ∈ Fℓ with random coefficients τ ∈ Ft is the Reed–Solomon codeword
