4.3

Copies and the link proof

Every proof opens columns, hence the provider makes copies of the table with fresh masks, each with a link
proof ([CFWpc]; [CFW26]).
Each committed table has an OOD sample ([CFWpc]; the technique: [BGKS20], as in [ACFY24b, Section
4.4]). The mask of an answer, a pad times a sampled nonzero factor (Definition 4.3), follows the private
zero-evaders of [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Section 9.2]; that only list members with different
messages count as a collision (Definition 4.5) follows its Section 9.1.
The OOD samples allow a radius e beyond half the code’s minimum distance, where the list of a word
can have several members; the sample selects one, up to a small error (Lemma A.25). Definition 4.5 takes
list members over E; those of a word over F are over F, since e is below the minimum distance (proof of
Lemma A.25). A word at relative distance δ passes ν sampled positions with probability (1 − δ)ν , hence a
larger δ requires fewer opened columns, which gives shorter proofs and allows more proofs within one opening
budget.
Definition 4.6 (First table, copy, link proof). Let A be in range. The first table T0 and the copies
T1 , . . . , TK are committed tables for A (Definition 4.2) whose pads and random coefficients are mutually
independent; σj is an OOD sample of Tj . The relation of the link proof is the set of all (T∗0 , σ0 , T∗j , σj ), two
words with a sample each, such that
Dec(T∗0 , σ0 ) = ⊥ or Coll(T∗j , ςj ) or Dec(T∗j , σj ) = Dec(T∗0 , σ0 ),
with Dec and Coll as in Definition 4.5. A link proof for the copy Tj is a proof string (Construction D.11) for
IOPlink
(Construction 4.13), whose IOP instance is (σ0 , σj ) and whose external oracle strings are T0 and Tj .
