4.5

The interactive protocol and the argument

A batch opens its copy once for all its requests, as in [GLSTW23] and [BFRW25] (see also [CFW26]); the
requests of a batch are the leaves of a Merkle tree, the batch tree (Construction D.11). The proximity tests
are the zero-knowledge tests of [CFW26], with changes, of which this paper describes one (Table 11). A
claim on the table (Construction 4.13, item 2), one linear claim with a linear form in committed masks,
has the form of its committed sumcheck relation [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Section 2.4 and
Definition 5.8]; the statements that are reduced to such a claim are this protocol’s, and the sources of the
reductions are in Table 11. The rounds that sample positions have proof of work ([CFWpc], with published
uses in [Sta21, Section 3.11.3], [ACFY24a, Section 6.2] and [ACFY24b, Section 6.2]). Compilation follows the
BCS transformation [BCS16]; the committed tables are compiled as external oracle strings, as in [COS20,
Section 10] and [CY24, Section 32.7], following [CFWpc].
Assumption 4.10 (Random oracle). The hash function H that the algorithms of ΠZ (Construction 4.15)
and all adversaries query is a random oracle (Definition 2.1) with outputs of λ = 256 bits. It models the
format’s hash function O∗ , the function from byte strings to 32-byte strings, built on BLAKE2b, by which the
release instantiates H. Every probability in a statement that cites this assumption is taken over the choice of
H and over the random choices of the algorithms.
That the Lean definition of BLAKE2b, beneath the Lean term for O∗ , is BLAKE2b [RFC7693] rests on
known answers alone.
Assumption 4.11 (Separate second hash). The second hash function H ′ that the algorithms of ΠZ
(Construction 4.15) evaluate is one particular function, the second hash function of the release’s format. It is
not sampled, and its values do not depend on H. It is assumed that O∗ (Assumption 4.10) can be modelled
by an oracle sampled independently of H ′ .
Remark 4.12 (On the second hash function). Like O∗ , H ′ is built on the compression function of BLAKE2b.
The statements that cite Assumption 4.11 use no further property of H ′ . They are proved for this one function,
on which Chk(Z) depends, and are not stated for another function in its place. The programs, which check the

17

lookup tables against their four digests under H ′ in the parameter file, also assume that H ′ resists collisions.
Remark D.13 (How the two hash functions differ) says how the two hash functions differ, and why a collision
of H ′ merges no two of VZ ’s queries.
Construction 4.13 (The interactive protocol FlashProveZ ). FlashProveZ consists of the encoder, which
maps a matrix A, pads and random coefficients to a committed table (Definition 4.2); the map from a table
and an OOD seed to its OOD answers (Definition 4.3); Coll and Dec (Definition 4.5); the largest size R of a
batch; and three IOPs with external oracle strings (Definition 2.3). After a table is sent, V samples its OOD
seed and P answers. The OOD sample belongs to no round of the three IOPs; it is part of their IOP instances.
