Definition 4.14 (Parameter checks). Chk(Z) ∈ {0, 1} is a function of Z and H ′ alone. Chk is a conjunction
of tests, in four groups; oracle messages, Lookups and blocks are those of Construction D.6.
1. Errors. A list of the IOPs’ rounds, each with an error bound, is derived from Z; every bound is at most
the parameter file’s target.
2. Rounds. The derived rounds, sizes and oracle messages are constants of VZ .
3. Layout. An oracle message is opened only after it is sent; the layout file places each cell where a Lookup
reads it.
4. Sizes. A challenge’s blocks, the bits of proof of work, the Merkle trees’ depths and the compiled verifiers’
queries are within fixed bounds.
The closed list is the Lean term that Appendix F names for this definition (Remark D.10, Given in outline).
In Construction 4.15 the committer and prover of ΠZ are those of Construction D.12. In a machine-checked
statement ΠZ is the Lean term with the same verifier and extractor, whose committer and prover are constant
functions (Remark 4.16). The statements of Section 5 on ΠZ depend on neither: Definition 2.7 names the
verifier and an extractor alone.
Construction 4.15 (The argument ΠZ ). Fix Z; H ′ is the function of Assumption 4.11. H is an oracle
of all algorithms; in every experiment it is sampled after Z is fixed. ΠZ = (RndZ , CommitZ , PZ , VZ ) is a
commit-and-prove argument (Definition 2.6) for the relation of greedy outputs at the model parameters µZ
(Definition 3.2 and Definition 3.8). Construction D.11 defines Merkle roots rt, the map Seed, which derives an
OOD seed from a root by two queries, and the compiled verifier of an IOP. code denotes fixed injective maps
18

to bit strings, one on configurations and one on pairs of a key and a digest; dgZ = H(code(Z)) is the digest of
Z. A proof π is a list of tokens and five byte strings.
Verifier. VZH (c, x, y, π) is deterministic. It evaluates H ′ on public data alone: on parts of Z, and on x and
y.
1. Scope test. Reject if Adm(Z) = 0 (Definition 3.8).
2. Unpacking. Unpack π, at dgZ and by one query to H (Construction D.11), into (fill, k, j, rtj , σj , πkey ,
πlink , πreq ), where k = (rt0 , σ0 ) is the key and each of the last three may be ⊥; if this fails, continue with
the fixed tuple of Construction D.11, at which step 4 rejects.
3. Public readings. Compute the public readings (dgZ , par) (Construction D.11); they enter every string
that steps 7 and 8 hash.
4. Reject unless ξ = build(x, y, fill) is defined (Definition 4.7).
5. Reject unless H(code(k, dgZ )) = c.
6. Reject if one of πkey , πlink , πreq is ⊥.
7. Reject unless ς0 = Seed(rt0 ) and ςj = Seed(rtj ): the OOD seeds that V samples in Construction 4.13
are derived from the roots.
8. Run the three compiled verifiers, each on a list of digests (roots, and OOD answers written as blocks of
λ bits), an IOP instance and a proof string:
