9. Reject unless the plain parts in πreq hold the number that π names for its copy. Otherwise accept.
Steps 4 to 9 are the compiled steps of VZ .
Extractor. ExtZ (c, tr), for a query–answer trace tr, makes no query; its value is ⊥ if a step fails.
1. Find the first entry of tr whose answer is c and whose query is code(k, dg) for a key k = (rt0 , σ0 ) and a
digest dg.
2. Translate the queries of tr, at the digest dg, into queries for leaves and inner nodes of Merkle trees
(Construction D.11).
3. From these, rebuild the word T∗0 under the root rt0 .
4. Return wt(Dec(T∗0 , σ0 )).
Committer and prover are those of Construction D.12. PZ serves up to R instances as one batch; VZ
checks each proof by itself.
Remark 4.16 (Honest parties). The machine-checked statements on ΠZ concern VZ , ExtZ and the model, which
are written in Lean, as above, up to what Remark D.10 (Given in outline) lists. Index-binding knowledge
soundness (Definition 2.7) depends on nothing else: a commitment is whatever λ = 256 bits the adversary
presents.
In the Lean term for ΠZ , RndZ , CommitZ and PZ are constants, and the provers of the three IOPs send
fixed messages. Hence completeness, index privacy (and with it anything on hiding the weights), correctness
of extraction, extractability of the commitment and no two openings cannot be stated with any content of
that term; a second Lean term, with the same verifier and with the committer and prover that the Lean
function compile yields (Remark B.7 (Which compiler); its output: Definition 6.15) from the Lean provers, is
the object of Section 6.5. Honest provers of the three IOPs, and the committer and prover compiled from
them, are written in Lean as definitions, for one request to a proof; completeness and zero knowledge are
machine-checked for these, with VZ and with their limits (Section 6.5). Of the committer compiled from
19

them, with VZ and its extractor, soundness with extractability of the commitment, no two openings and
correctness of extraction are proved, at the files in use, with their limits, in Section 6.5; these read no prover.
Of the released programs none of these properties is proved. The results of Theorem 6.3, Corollary 6.7, and
Theorem 6.9 concern other objects: the table experiment, the whole-key argument and IOPlink
Z . The extractor
need not return the weights that a committer used. The results on the explicit committer (Definition 5.16)
are statements on ΠΓZ , not on ΠZ .
The number that a proof names for its copy (step 9 of Construction 4.15) is tied to rtj by no step, and an
acceptance does not certify it.

4.6

True requests

Definition 4.17 (True request, constraints, honest bound). Let µ be valid model parameters
(Definition 3.6), for a configuration Z those that Z states. Let A be in range (Definition 4.1) and ξ =
(win, i0 , i1 , y + ) an encoded request (Definition 4.7), so that y + = 0 if i1 < L. Under exact match, the encoded
request is true at A if, for the integer model at µ and the weights wt(A) (Definition 3.1 and Definition 3.2),
1. the model is defined on win,
2. the token win[i + 1] is the greedy token after (win[0], . . . , win[i]) for all i0 ≤ i < min(i1 , L − 1), and
3. if i1 = L, then y + is the greedy token after win.
The constraints of an arithmetization (Definition 4.8) are its identities and lookups. Cells satisfy the constraints
modulo p, or over the integers, if every identity is 0 and every lookup key is an entry of its table, modulo p or
as integers. Cells are within the honest bound if each has absolute value at most 27 .
Lemma 4.18 (True requests and greedy outputs). [machine-checked] Let µ be valid model parameters, A
a digit matrix of M ∈ I, and x, y, fill lists of tokens.
(a) If build(x, y, fill) is defined and true at A (Definition 4.17), then y is a greedy output of M on x.
(b) If (M, x, y) ∈ Servµ (Definition 3.11), then build(x, y, fill) with empty fill is defined and true at A.

5

Soundness

All theorems, propositions, lemmas and corollaries of this section are machine-checked, nine of them (Theorems 5.1 and 5.19 among them) by joining Lean theorems on paper, as the text beside each says. For
each machine-checked statement, Appendix F names the Lean declarations that it renders. Lean proves
Theorem 5.1 in two forms, at the same error: with the extractor left open, as in Definition 2.7, and with ExtZ
of Construction 4.15 named (Remark 5.2, What the theorem gives). The Lean form of Theorem 5.1 has one
further hypothesis, that the three byte strings of Z decode without error; admissibility implies it by a further
Lean theorem; Theorem 5.1 as printed joins the two, and so does every statement of this section whose Lean
form carries that hypothesis, Corollary 5.10 and Theorem 5.11 for this reason alone.

5.1

The theorem

Theorem 5.1 (Soundness). [machine-checked] Let Z be an admissible configuration (Definition 3.8) with the
exact-match policy (Definition 3.4), fixed before H is sampled. Then the argument ΠZ (Construction 4.15) is
index-binding knowledge sound (Definition 2.7) for the relation of greedy outputs at µZ (Definition 3.2), in
the random-oracle model (Assumption 4.10 and Assumption 4.11), with error
