In T the 1 is the query of step 2 of VZ (Construction 4.15 and Construction D.11), and the summand 223
covers the verifier’s queries in steps 3 and 6 to 9 (as the compiler counts them, not its calls to H) and one
query that the proof adds; the query of step 5 is in neither, and the term (T + 2)2 /2256 accounts for it.
Section 5.6 accounts for that term and for the factor 16.
20

Remark 5.2 (What the theorem gives). Except with probability ε(q), an output that VZ accepts is a greedy
output of the weights that the straight-line extractor, ExtZ of Construction 4.15, reads off the commitment
and the committer’s queries; these weights are determined before any proof is made. The theorem holds for
every proof string: a proof that is made in a batch, which VZ verifies singly, with its path in the batch tree, is
covered at ε(q), and N proofs sent at once are covered at N · ε(q), q being the committer’s bound and the
whole list’s prover bound added up (Corollary 5.10).
Here ε(q) ≈ q/2124 + 2−101 : 124 bits per query (Remark B.5, Bits per query). The bounds on Reed–
Solomon codes strictly below the Johnson radius that the proof uses (Lemma 5.13 and Lemma A.23) are
machine-checked, resting in part on files adapted from ArkLib and Mathlib (Remark B.18, Typed statement).
On how the Lean proof is checked, see Remark B.17. Where Chk(Z) = 0, VZ rejects every input; Chk(Z0 ) = 1
(Corollary 5.8).
Remark 5.3 (Reading the theorem).
1. The configuration is admissible and fixed before the oracle. A choice of it after querying H, which is
open to every provider, is not covered (Open problem 8.16; Limitation 8.5).
2. A second hash function is fixed (Assumption 4.11), an assumption that is neither tested nor proved and
on which no run bears. The proofs hold for this function, not for every function in its place.
3. No witness is extracted: the relation has none, and no party is asserted to know the weights. The
extractor need not be efficient: it is a function defined with the axiom of choice, not a program.
4. The extracted weights need not be the committer’s: that they are is correctness of extraction (Section 5.5;
Theorem 6.31). ExtZ takes Z as an argument: no statement says that ExtZ and ExtZ ′ agree, or ties a
commitment to one configuration.
5. The client checks three things. Exact match is a hypothesis that VZ does not test: the client checks
that polZ = (256, 256, 0); at other policies Theorem 5.11 applies. The conclusion holds whatever the
other two are: the model parameters, which fix the integer model and lie in the scale file and, for ϵ1 , ϵ2 ,
ϵF , MF and KF , in the parameter file (Definition 3.7; Limitation 8.6, Scales can disable blocks), and the
length of y (Limitation G.4, Prefixes count). The verifier is VZ , not the C++ verifier (Limitation 8.1,
Lean verifier only); on what a run trusts, see Remark B.10.
6. That the parameter checks pass, which is necessary for acceptance and not sufficient, is proved only at
Z0 and, for Theorem 5.11, at the configurations that differ from it only in the policy (Proposition 5.6).
Besides the reduction of Remark 6.49, the one theorem on acceptance by VZ is completeness with the
Lean prover, on served requests and over a random oracle; that some request is served is stated by a
lemma beside it, at constructed weights (Section 6.5). A verifier that rejects everything also meets
Definition 2.7, and likewise round-by-round soundness (Proposition 5.14).
At Z0 , and at the configurations that differ from it only in the policy, the parameter checks are ruled out
as the cause, and in runs at Z0 and Z1 the executable form, which equals VZ by Theorem 5.5, accepted
honest proofs on the parsed proof files (Section 7.3); so there VZ is seen not to reject everything, at the
format’s hash function and as far as a run is trusted (Remark B.10, Claim from files). These runs were
made over Lean files that differ from the released ones, but for the calls of the reference run and of the
second set of 41, which were made over the released Lean files (Appendix H.3). Elsewhere, and at any
other hash function, the theorem may hold only because the verifier rejects everything (Limitation 8.9,
Completeness on served requests only); over a random oracle, Section 6.5 excludes this where the files of
Z are in use (defined there), and some request is served.
7. That Definition 3.8 and Construction 4.15 and the relation of greedy outputs are the Lean terms
of Theorem 5.1 is checked by reading, not by Lean, except for the policy’s three numbers; so is
Construction 4.13, its item 5 by a count by programs.
8. A verifier that treated the programs’ hash function specially would also meet the theorem, whose bound
is over a random oracle (Limitation G.5, Fiat–Shamir instantiation).
Remark B.4 adds particulars to items 3 to 5.

21

5.2

The executable form and the default configuration

As a Lean term, VZ cannot be evaluated, because parts of its definition use the axiom of choice. A second
term, the executable form, can, and it computes the same decision.
Definition 5.4 (Executable form). A function O from byte strings to byte strings induces the hash
function HO , where HO (bs) consists of the first λ = 256 bits of O(byt(bs)); the injective coding byt of bit
strings as byte strings and the order of bits are those of Construction B.8. The executable form of VZ at O is
the function EZO that the algorithm of Construction B.8 computes. It maps a commitment c, a prompt x, an
output y and a proof π (Construction 4.15) to 1 (accept), 0 (reject) or ⊥.
Theorem 5.5 (The executable form computes the verifier). [machine-checked] For all configurations Z,
all functions O from byte strings to 32-byte strings, and all commitments c, prompts x, outputs y and proofs
π (Definition 5.4),
EZO (c, x, y, π) = VZHO (c, x, y, π).
In particular, EZO (c, x, y, π) ̸= ⊥.
Proposition 5.6 (The parameter checks pass at the default configuration). [machine-checked]
The default configuration Z0 (Definition 3.7) is admissible (Definition 3.8), its policy is exact match, and
Chk(Z0 ) = 1 (Definition 4.14). Every configuration Z that differs from Z0 only in the values (C, θ, cap) of its
policy, where 28 ≤ θ ≤ C < 232 and cap ≤ 1024, is admissible, and Chk(Z) = 1.
Remark 5.7 (How the proposition is checked). Proposition 5.6, a conjunction made on paper of Lean theorems,
concerns Z0 as written out in Lean, not the release’s files (Remark D.2, Written copy). The proofs of its
statements on Chk lie in a separate Lean library, which nothing beneath Theorem 5.1 or Proposition 5.14
imports, so neither of these rests on it. One constant, on which that library rests, equates the layout file as one
string with its pieces. At the default thread stack Lean’s kernel stops on it at too deep a recursion; at a larger
one the kernel accepts it, both when the file is compiled and when the toolchain checks it again. The re-checker
leanchecker stops on it in the same way at each stack limit that was tried, and no independent program has
accepted it. It is not the only one. In passes over every other module of the main and the separate library,
over Lean files that differ from the released ones, in code too, the re-checker stopped with the same message
on 22 further modules of that library, and on no module of the main library. Replayed at the larger stack
over the build of those files, by Lean’s own replay, Lean’s kernel accepted each of the 22. The check script
replays all 1,438 modules of two libraries (lean/AtFullSide/, 914 modules; lean/ShapeLines/, 524), not
counting the file added later (Remark B.17), by Lean’s own replay at a larger stack and not by leanchecker;
23 of them are shown to need it, all of them modules of lean/AtFullSide/; on lean/ShapeLines/ nobody
has tried leanchecker. By import lines at least one of the 23 lies beneath the module of each theorem of
Section 6.5 on completeness and zero knowledge, and beneath those of Theorem 6.31, of Proposition 6.17,
part 1, and of Proposition 6.33, parts 1 and 4; and beneath none of the soundness theorems of Section 5
(Theorem 5.1; Theorem 5.17 and Theorem 5.19), nor beneath Theorem 5.5, Corollary B.11 or Theorem 6.20:
no module of that library lies beneath any of them (lean/GAPS.md, gap 73; lean/LIMITS.md, L69).
Corollary 5.8 (At the default configuration). [machine-checked] Let Z0 be the default configuration
(Definition 3.7). In the random-oracle model (Assumption 4.10 and Assumption 4.11), the argument ΠZ0 is
index-binding knowledge sound (Definition 2.7) for the relation of greedy outputs at µZ0 , with the extractor
ExtZ0 and the error ε(q) of Theorem 5.1. Moreover, Chk(Z0 ) = 1, and EZO0 = VZH0O for every function O from
byte strings to 32-byte strings.
Lean has these three parts as separate theorems, joined here on paper; on the part Chk(Z0 ) = 1, see
Remark 5.7.
Remark 5.9 (What a client gets). At Z0 , comparing three digests (Definition D.1; Remark D.2) settles the
policy and the model parameters, and of the three checks of Remark 5.3 (item 5) the length of y remains.
Corollary 5.8 is the conjunction of Theorem 5.1, Theorem 5.5, and Proposition 5.6 at Z0 . The bound ε(q)
also holds for the executable form’s own decision (Corollary B.11), and the executable form has accepted, in
