Remark 5.15 (Single rounds).
1. These are errors of single rounds; the probability of a forgery is bounded by Theorem 5.1, at 124 bits per
query. The proposition joins on paper three Lean theorems, one for each IOP. Where Z is admissible and
Chk(Z) = 1 (Section 6.5), Lean proves these round errors for the three IOPs together, as one statement,
and bounds the error of the whole run before compilation by 67,391 · 2−96 < 2−79 ; after compilation the
bound stays that of Theorem 5.1.
2. In the rounds that sample positions, the bound holds only with the round’s 20, 28 or 32 bits of proof of
work; without them the errors are about 2−96 to 2−110 (a program’s arithmetic, not machine-checked;
where Lean joins the three, as in item 1, it is machine-checked that no round is worse than 2−96 ). At Z0
the 28 bits for the 64 positions of a table are needed: with 27 the bound exceeds 2−128 (Proposition D.8).
3. The figures that the C++ verifier prints follow another accounting of round errors. Under stricter counts
than that accounting (all columns of the matrix of cells that is tested; a factor for the list size), some
of its rounds would miss, by a planning calculation that is not machine-checked, its target of 2−128
(Remark G.2, Stricter counts). Proposition 5.14 does not depend on it: its bound is proved for the IOPs
of Construction 4.13.

5.5

The commitment

A client needs soundness for the weights that were committed, not for weights that an extractor returns.
Theorem 5.19 gives it, for an explicit committer: except with a small probability, an accepted output meets
the policy under the committed weights themselves. It rests on Theorem 5.11 and on Theorem 5.17: where the
parameter checks pass, ExtZ returns the committed weights, except with probability about 2−89 . Theorem 5.17
states its errors in the form of the error of Theorem 5.1; Proposition 5.18 gives sharper ones for its parts 1
and 2, and Remark B.23 the form of part 1 of Theorem 5.19 with them.
