Remark 5.21 (Scope of the statements on the commitment).
• Object. The explicit committer models one part of the release’s committing program. It was compared,
in values only, with the committing program of other programs† , at the reduced configuration (Table 4),
where none of these theorems is stated: what ran was a faster Lean definition of the committer, proved
equal to it, with two further Lean definitions, for the table and for the coins, which no theorem on
the committer mentions. Nothing is proved about the release’s committing program (Limitation G.1,
Implementation unverified).
• Checks. Chk(Z) = 1 is machine-checked only at the configurations of Proposition 5.6. There the
hypothesis of Theorem 5.17 is met. Where Chk(Z) = 0, Theorem 5.19 is vacuous (Remark B.22, Proof
outlines for the commitment).
• Acceptance. The explicit committer outputs no key file and has no honest prover, so no proof is shown
to be accepted under its commitment. Who made a given commitment is outside the statements
(Limitation 8.7, Unattributed commitment). No test covers the three properties of Theorem 5.17.
That the properties of Theorem 5.17 fail for a committer that replaces every digit matrix by the all-zero
matrix, and that a committer whose commitment is a constant also meets the bound of Theorem 5.19, which
constrains a committer only on the event that VZ accepts, is Proposition B.24.

25

5.6

Proof outline

The proof of Theorem 5.1 has four parts (Appendix A).
Compilation. Steps 4 to 9 of VZ are the output of the Lean library’s compiler (Construction D.11) at the
three IOPs (Remark B.7, Which compiler). The lemma on this compiler (Lemma A.15) rests on an argument
over separate oracles (Lemma A.11), which fixes an event Bad on traces (Definition A.5): a new answer
repeats a value of the trace, changes the state of an IOP from 0 to 1, or gives an OOD seed with a collision.
A Q-query algorithm meets Bad with probability at most Q εrd + 2 (Q + 1)2 /2256 (Lemma A.8). Outside Bad,
an accepting run of a compiled verifier gives the relation of its IOP at the words under its roots (Lemma A.9).
Hence the first table decodes, the copy decodes to the same matrix A, and (A, ξ) ∈ ReqZ (Lemma A.10).
Rounds. Proposition 5.14 gives εrd = 2−128 where the parameter checks pass; elsewhere the three verifiers
reject (Lemma A.22).
Meaning. If (A, build(x, y, fill)) ∈ ReqZ , then y meets polZ on x under wt(A) (Lemma A.33). This proves
Theorem 5.11; at exact match, y is a greedy output (Proposition 3.5).
Error. The compiled verifiers make qV ≤ 220 queries to the separate oracles of Construction D.11, which is
not a bound on their queries to H (Remark B.13, Lists sent at once). The public readings make eight queries
to H, and step 2 one, for dgZ (Construction D.11; Lemma A.35); the proof adds one query that announces rt0 ,
and to each query four queries for OOD seeds (Lemma A.11; Definition A.4). Hence Q = 5 (q + qV + 10) ≤ 5T ,
since the 223 in T covers qV + 9. Then Q εrd ≤ 5 T /2128 , and where ε(q) < 1 the quadratic term is at most
6 T /2128 . The factor 16 is not tight: the arithmetic lemma is stated for round errors up to 2 εrd . The term
(T + 2)2 /2256 accounts for replacing the key by its hash value c (Lemma A.14 and Lemma A.36).

6

Zero knowledge and completeness

This section is on zero knowledge, formally index privacy (Definition 2.14), and on completeness; what is proved
is exact honest-verifier simulation up to private aborts before compilation (Definition 6.24; Theorem 6.27) and
zero knowledge with request leakage after it (Definition 6.34; Theorem 6.35 and Theorem 6.37; Remark 6.40).
For the Lean provers, honest provers of the three IOPs written in Lean as definitions, completeness and zero
knowledge are machine-checked in the forms that Section 6.5 states, before and after compilation, with the
limits stated there (Limitation 8.3 (Zero knowledge of definitions in Lean); Limitation 8.9 (Completeness on
served requests only)); that the forms of zero knowledge are index privacy is not stated. On zero knowledge
three further objects have machine-checked results. On completeness, further: Remark 6.49.
• The committed tables of FlashProveZ , before compilation. With fresh randomness and within the opening
budget, their OOD answers and opened columns are independent of the weights (Theorem 6.3).
• The whole-key argument (Section 6.3). Its index privacy reduces to five conditions on its provers, five
of twenty (Theorem 6.6). In Lean the compiled zero-knowledge statement of Section 6.5 is obtained
through the theorem on all twenty conditions (Theorem C.25): inside its proof all twenty conditions are
met by the Lean provers of Section 6.5, at one copy of the table. No Lean theorem states that by name;
we have it from a reading of the proof’s calls. Every limit of the results before compilation holds for
that statement as well (Remark 6.41). With constant-message provers, fresh random masks, one request
to a proof and the whole key, not a 256-bit hash, as the commitment, and against any verifier, what it
shows of the tables is simulated from the leakage alone, up to a stated error (Corollary 6.7).
• The IOP of the link proof, before compilation. At admissible Z its honest prover has an exact honestverifier simulator, given the two tables’ OOD samples and opened columns, which is not a statement
that the weights are hidden. At admissible Z with Chk(Z) = 1, the link’s verifier accepts the same
prover at every challenge that passes proof of work, for all weights, at one fixed public-files digest, at
which VZ is not proved ever to be called (Theorem 6.9).

6.1

Leakage

The leakage (Definition 2.13) is what a simulator is given: pp(M ), N and each batch’s revealed instances.
What a client of the released programs sees by design, including timing and failures, is listed in Remark G.10.

26

Remark 6.1 (What the leakage contains). The simulator may depend on Z, which is quantified before M in
every statement. In the statements on index privacy the simulator is given the leakage (Definition 2.13); what
the simulators of Section 6.5 are given is listed there. In the machine-checked statements on index privacy
pp is constant, and each concerns one commitment. Every revealed instance lies in SM (Limitation G.11,
Outcome leakage), and the released tools compute the scale file of Z and lines of its parameter file from the
weights (Section 3.3); these two files encode µZ (Definition 3.7). No statement bounds what the revealed
instances or these files reveal. The environment is quantified after M and may depend on it. So its instances
can carry information about the weights into both views, and no statement bounds that.

6.2

Committed tables

The protocol has copies because a table is shown to hide the weights for at most t opened columns. In the
table experiment an unbounded A names an OOD seed for each table before the table is sampled, and receives
OOD answers, chosen columns, and runs of the three IOPs with constant-message provers. It is within the
opening budget if at most t columns of each table are opened, where each run is charged 62 or 64 columns.
Definition 6.2 (Constant-message prover). A prover for an IOP (Definition 2.3) is a constant-message
prover if in every round it sends one fixed message, the same for all inputs, coins and challenges, and never
aborts. Its external oracle strings are the committed tables of its input.
Theorem 6.3 (Committed tables before compilation). [machine-checked] Let Z be admissible (Definition 3.8), with opening budget t = 44,040,191. For all matrices A, A′ over F and every algorithm A within the
opening budget (Definition C.6), the outputs of the table experiments with fresh randomness for each table and
constant-message provers (Definition C.5),
TabZ (A, A)

and

TabZ (A′ , A),

are identically distributed.
Remark 6.4 (No prover message is covered). By Theorem 6.3, what an unbounded, adaptive A within the
opening budget sees of the committed tables is perfectly independent of the weights. The theorem is not
index privacy.
• No message that an honest prover computes from A is covered, alone or jointly with the tables.
• Each table has one OOD sample, and its randomness is used at one configuration. Randomness derived
by a pseudorandom function, as in the released programs, is not covered. An OOD seed is chosen before
its table is sampled. A seed computed from the table, such as a hash of its root, as in the released
programs, is not covered.
For the OOD answers Theorem 6.3 rests on Lemma C.9, which follows [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026,
Section 9.2].
Proposition 6.5 (The opening budget is tight for one table). [machine-checked] Let t = 44,040,191 and
s = 1, at the table sizes of Z0 (Definition 3.7). Let A = 0 and let A′ have the single nonzero entry A′ [0][0] = 1,
both in range (Definition 4.1); let ς ◦ be the OOD seed with point (1, . . . , 1) and factor 1. For every set Q ⊆ D
of t + 1 positions, the distribution of the pair of the OOD answers at ς ◦ and the columns (T[ϕ])ϕ∈Q of a
committed table T (Definition 4.2) differs between A and A′ .

6.3

After compilation

The whole-key argument Πwk
Z [P] (Definition C.20) differs from ΠZ as follows.
• Matrices A and encoded requests ξ take the place of indices and instances.
• The commitment is the whole key: the roots and OOD samples of T0 , . . . , TK (each sample at a seed
that is a hash of its table’s root), an offline proof and K link proofs.
• A proof is for one request, on a uniformly chosen copy; Nc bounds the requests simulated on one copy.

27

• The proofs are compiled runs (Definition C.18) of provers P = (Pkey , Plink , Preq ), which are parameters.
• The verifier runs steps 6 to 9 of VZ , at fixed values (dg, par) of its public readings.
Theorem 6.6 requires five conditions of each prover P∗ (in full: Definition C.21):
1. its external oracle strings are the committed tables;
2. a simulator Sim∗ , given the IOP instance, the challenges and the opened columns, reproduces what P∗
discloses, except with probability ζ∗sim ;
3. P∗ aborts for a reason that depends on its input with probability at most ζ∗ab ;
4. the prefixes of its leaf salts are public;
5. all coins are uniform.
