Remark 6.8 (What holds after compilation). By Corollary 6.7, with constant-message provers, fresh random
masks, one request to a proof and the whole key, not a 256-bit hash, as the commitment, what the commitment
and the proofs show of the committed tables (roots, OOD samples, opened columns) is simulated up to ζmax .
This holds against any verifier within the corollary’s bounds. Besides the verifier’s queries the simulator is
given the leakage alone, not the weights.
28

1. Conditional. Of 20 conditions (Theorem C.25) fifteen are proved (a count of conditions, not of work):
those that mention no prover, coin or simulator; five of the fifteen hold by type or default. The other
five concern the three honest provers, their simulators and coins; the Lean provers meet them inside
the proof of the compiled zero-knowledge statement of Section 6.5, at one copy of the table; no Lean
theorem states that by name, and we have it from a reading of the proof’s calls (Open problem 8.13).
2. Constant messages. Fixed messages are trivially independent of the weights. Hence, for the provers’
own messages, a result on index privacy is meaningful only together with completeness of the same
provers (for the Lean provers: Section 6.5). The corollary covers nothing that the three provers (in
Corollary C.29: the other two) would compute from the weights in their rounds.
3. The whole key. The commitment of ΠZ has 256 bits, not the whole key. No statement transfers either
result to ΠZ .
4. Single requests. Both results are for one request to a proof. Lean’s statements on completeness and zero
knowledge do not cover batches, which the released programs use (Open problem 8.14).
5. Proofs are produced. A constant-message prover never aborts, so its compiled run is ⊥ only if a proof-ofwork search exhausts its limit (Definition C.18). Part 2 bounds the probability of that; without it, part
1 could be a statement on views that hold no proof.

6.4

The link proof

Theorem 6.9 (Link proof before compilation). [machine-checked] Let Z be admissible (Definition 3.8),
