Hypotheses and objects.
Definition 6.11 (Configuration in use). Three conditions on a configuration Z (Definition 3.7) are used
as hypotheses.
(Admissible configuration) Adm(Z) = 1 (Definition 3.8).
(Checks pass) Chk(Z) = 1 (Definition 4.14).
(Exact match) The policy polZ of an admissible Z is exact match (Definition 3.4).
The files of Z are in use if Z is admissible, Chk(Z) = 1 and its policy is exact match; Z itself is then also
called in use. At files in use the policy is exact match, which fixes the policy’s three numbers; the five numbers
ϵ1 , ϵ2 , ϵF , MF , KF stay free.
Definition 6.12 (Served instance, label, bit). Let µ be valid model parameters and M ∈ I (Definition 3.1).
An instance (x, y) is well-formed if x and y are non-empty lists of tokens of V with |x| + |y| ≤ L + 1, that is,
if build(x, y, ∅) is defined (Definition 4.7). It is served at M if (M, x, y) ∈ Servµ (Definition 3.11), and refused
otherwise. The bit bitµ (M, x, y) ∈ {served, refused} says which. The label of a well-formed instance is


if (M, x, y) ∈ Servµ ,
served
labµ (M, x, y) = failed
if (x, y) cannot be served at M ,


other tokens if (x, y) can be served at M and y is not a greedy output of M on x.
An instance that is not well-formed has the label other tokens.
Remark 6.13 (Served and can be served).
1. Two conditions. “Can be served” (Definition 3.11) requires only that the model be defined on the
window of (x, y), the first L tokens of x ∥ y ∥ 0 0 · · · . “Served” requires in addition that y be a greedy
output of M on x. The bit is membership in Servµ , not the weaker condition. Every served instance is
well-formed.
2. The Lean form. In Lean the label is defined through the model’s result on (x, y): the tokens that its run
on the window chooses for the positions of y, or no result if the model is not defined on the window. A
well-formed instance is served if the model has a result on it and y is that result; its label is failed if
there is no result, and other tokens if the result differs from y. Lean theorems, at valid µ, give the form
of Definition 6.12 by propositional steps.
3. Zeros after the output. The window includes the zeros that follow y. Hence a greedy output y of M on
x has the label failed if the model is not defined on the whole window.
4. No token. The label names no token: other tokens says only that the result differs from y.
