Remark 6.21 (Reading the bound on a whole run). Let Z be admissible with Chk(Z) = 1, as in Theorem 6.20.
1. Round by round. It is machine-checked, as one Lean statement, that against every prover the protocol
before compilation is round-by-round sound (Definition A.1) for ReqZ , the relation under the policy of
Z. Each round’s error, that of the round in which an OOD seed is sampled included, is at most 2−128 of
all that round’s challenges, proof-of-work bits among them (Proposition 5.14).
2. The factors. Among the challenges that pass, a round’s error is therefore at most 2−96 , since no round has
more than 32 bits of proof of work. A whole run has 67,391 rounds. Lean’s bound is for a deterministic
prover; for a randomized one it follows by averaging over the prover’s coins, a step on paper.
3. Not a figure for the compiled argument. 2−96 is the bound for the rounds with the most proof of work,
applied to every round. It is not a bound for the compiled argument, whose error is that of Theorem 5.1.
4. The relation. ReqZ is taken at the policy of Z, with the relaxed bound of Definition 4.8; at exact match
it implies that each output token is the model’s.
5. The extractor. The round-by-round statement of item 1 has an extractor for the weights. It is straightline: the decoding function Dec, applied to the first table and its OOD sample. It extracts the weights
alone, and no running time is claimed for it.
Definition 6.22 (Completeness of provers for the three IOPs). Let P = (Pkey , Plink , Preq ) be provers
with abort sets (Definition C.2) for the three IOPs of FlashProveZ , Y a set of pairs (A, ξ), and α ∈ [0, 1]. A
run of P∗ fails at given coins and challenges ch if ch lies in the abort set, or if the verifier of the IOP rejects the
transcript for some values of its nonces. P is complete on Y with error α if there are αkey + αlink + αreq ≤ α
such that, for every (A, ξ) ∈ Y, all randomness and all OOD seeds of committed tables T0 , Tj for A, and
every copy number:
1. the run of Pkey on T0 , of Plink on (T0 , Tj ), and of Preq on Tj and ξ, each with the tables’ OOD samples,
fails with probability at most αkey , αlink , αreq , over the prover’s uniform coins and a uniform ch that
passes;
2. at all coins, challenges and nonces, the plain parts of Preq hold the copy number.
