Remark 6.30 (Reading completeness).
1. In words. Where the files of Z are in use, at every well-formed request whose claimed output is the
model’s own result, over the hash function and the committer’s and the prover’s coins, VZ rejects the
Lean prover’s proof with probability at most 2−40 .
33

2. Two bounds. 2−41 (Theorem 6.23) and 2−40 are bounds over two distributions. The first is over the
provers’ coins and the challenges that pass, at each encoding randomness, each OOD seed and each
copy’s number, not on average. The second is over H and all coins, and includes the proof-of-work
searches that exhaust their limit.
3. Served instances only. At a request that is not served, that is, one that is ill-formed or on which the
model has no result or another result than the claimed output, Theorem 6.23 and Theorem 6.29 state
nothing. In particular, no statement of completeness covers the well-formed requests on which the model
has no result (Open problem 8.12). At a real failure the prover returns the empty proof, the same as for
a claimed output that is not the model’s. That a request is refused is unchecked, as the label is: an
empty proof proves nothing, and a prover can return it at a request that it could serve. The compiled
verifier rejects it, under every hash function and at all public files (Proposition 6.33). A service that
chooses to say “failed” does not prove it.
