Remark 6.32 (Reading the theorem on the commitment).
1. Which parts are single Lean theorems. Two statements are single Lean theorems to the letter: part 1
for some extractor, and part 3. Part 1 for ExtZ by name, and part 2, are not. They follow a fortiori
from one Lean theorem, which states, for ExtZ , index-binding knowledge soundness with the error ε of
Theorem 5.1, extractability of the commitment with error γ lp (q) = 3 (q + 249 + 10)2 /2256 , and no two
openings with error 2γ lp . Parts 1 and 2 as printed follow on paper: ε ≤ εb and min(γ lp , 1) ≤ β ≤ εb ,
each by a Lean lemma, and a probability is at most 1.
2. In words. Nobody opens a commitment of the compiled committer to other weights than the extractor
gives, except with probability about q/2124 + 2−100 , where q counts the hash queries of whoever made the
commitment and of the opener together, and no commitment is opened to two different weights except
with twice that. For the honest committer, within its budget of 248 hash queries (Proposition 6.33), the
extractor gives back the committed weights except with probability about 2−155 .
3. Openings. To open (Definition 2.10) is to name the weights and one number, r.
4. What the commitment covers. The 256-bit commitment covers the first table’s root, its OOD sample
and the digest dgZ . It does not cover πkey , the copy or πlink .
5. No prover. Parts 1 and 2 depend on no prover. They hold for every argument that the same compilation
yields from any three provers, provided that all leaf salts of its tables have one length, the same whatever
the public readings return. Part 3 holds there with error 3(N + 249 + 10)2 /2256 , for any bound N on
the committer’s own queries.
6. The extractor. ExtZ is a function, not shown computable.
7. The programs. Nothing is stated about the committer of the released programs (Open problem 8.11).
8. Figures. The error of Theorem 5.1 is about q/2124 + 2−101 , a different figure from εb . No figure here is
128 bits.

