Remark 6.36 (Reading zero knowledge on served instances).
1. In words. At all files in use, for one commitment, up to 688,127 answered requests and 264 hash queries,
and against any verifier within these two bounds: if every request is served, a simulator that gets each
request and the largest number of answered requests, and beside these nothing of the weights, makes a
view within 2−154 of the real one.
2. The files are not counted. The simulator is chosen after the files of Z and may depend on them, so
what these files reveal about the weights is not counted. The released tools compute the scale file of
Z and lines of its parameter file from the weights (Remark 6.1, What the leakage contains). Nor are
the requests: each served request holds the model’s own output on its prompt, and the simulator gets
the request, so what the requests reveal about the weights is not counted. “Nothing of the weights”
concerns the experiment alone: there pp and the request leakage are constant.
3. Membership is not hidden. Whether an instance is served depends on the weights, and the theorem
does not hide it. The verifier does not name its requests: the environment does, as a function of the
verifier’s view. The environment is chosen after the weights, may depend on them, and is the same in
both experiments. That each of its instances is served at M is a hypothesis on the environment; nothing
in either experiment checks it. Every such environment is covered, so a verifier can pass a choice of
request to the environment through its coins and queries. A verifier that gets a reply to a request that
is not served is outside this theorem and inside Theorem 6.37.
4. One simulator. One simulator serves all weights, all valid environments and all verifiers, and with
probability 1 it gives no two answers to one query. Its running time is not modelled.
35

5. The error. The theorem states that an error function with this bound exists and names none; beyond
the two bounds nothing is stated. N counts the answered requests (Remark 6.39); at a served request
the answer is a proof or, where a proof-of-work search exhausts its limit, the empty proof.
6. Scope. The theorem is for fresh random masks and coins, one commitment, one copy of the table, one
request per proof and exact match (Remark 6.42, Scope of the results). The weights are fixed before
the hash function is sampled, and the verifier has no hash query before the commitment (Remark 6.45,
The model). The prover and the committer are Lean definitions (Remark 6.43); the view is that of
Remark 6.39, item 4.
7. Which requests. At every valid µ some request is served (Proposition 3.12(a): at constructed weights;
more at Z0 : Remark 3.13). At the model parameters of Z0 there are valid weights, constructed too,
at which no instance can be served (Proposition 3.12(b)): at such weights the theorem concerns the
commitment alone. No instance at a real model’s weights is proved to be one that can be served
(Remark 3.13).
Theorem 6.37 (Zero knowledge at every instance). [machine-checked] Let Z be in use (Definition 6.11),
fixed before H is sampled. In the random-oracle model (Assumption 4.10 and Assumption 4.11), the compiled
Lean argument Πlp
Z (Definition 6.15) has zero knowledge with request leakage bitµZ (Definition 6.12) on the
set of all instances (Definition 6.34), with an error ζ such that
ζ(q, N ) ≤ 2−157 + 688,127 · 2−174 < 2−154

for all q ≤ 264 and N ≤ 688,127.

Corollary 6.38 (Zero knowledge with the label shown). [machine-checked] Under the hypotheses of
Theorem 6.37, the argument Πlp
Z [labµZ ] has zero knowledge with request leakage labµZ (Definition 6.12) on the
set of well-formed instances (Definition 6.34), with an error that satisfies the bound of Theorem 6.37.
Remark 6.39 (Reading zero knowledge after compilation).
1. In words. At all files in use and every request, well-formed or not,
• for one commitment, on one copy of the table, up to 688,127 answered requests and 264 hash
queries,
• and against any verifier within these two bounds that has no hash query before the commitment
(Remark 6.45),
• a simulator that gets each request, the largest number of answered requests and, of the weights,
only one bit for each request (served or refused)
• makes a view within 2−157 + 688,127 · 2−174 < 2−154 of the real one; each answer is a proof or the
empty proof.
As a corollary (Corollary 6.38), for a service that chooses to say “failed”, the same holds at every
well-formed request with one label (served, failed or other tokens) given to the simulator and shown
with each proof.
2. The simulator. The simulator is fixed before the weights, may depend on the files of Z, and answers the
hash queries in the hash function’s place. Its running time is not modelled. The public parameters of
the index, pp, are constant for this relation (Definition 3.2), so they carry nothing about the weights.
3. The leakage. The notion is only as strong as ℓ is small: if Π has zero knowledge with a constant request
leakage, then Π[ℓ] with ℓ(M, x, y) = M , whose prover outputs the weights, has zero knowledge with
request leakage ℓ.
4. The commitment and the view. The commitment in both experiments is the 256-bit commitment of
Definition 6.15, not the whole key. The view consists of this commitment, each answered request with
what the prover outputs for it, the query–answer trace and the coins of V ∗ .
5. The two bounds. N counts the requests that the prover answers, by a proof or by the empty proof.
Elsewhere in this paper an answered request is called a reply. Nothing in the prover counts the answered
requests, and beyond the two bounds nothing is stated.
36

6. The error. 2−157 bounds the error of the compilation at these numbers; 2−174 is the bound on private
aborts of Theorem 6.27, counted once for each answered request. A proof-of-work search that exhausts
its limit is part of what is simulated: it gives the empty proof at a served request.
7. Derivation. Theorem 6.37 follows from Theorem 6.35 and part 1 of Proposition 6.33 by a lemma on
every commit-and-prove argument. Suppose that every served instance lies in the relation and that the
prover outputs one fixed value at every refused instance. Then zero knowledge with constant request
leakage on the served triples gives zero knowledge with request leakage bit on all instances, with the
same error function: the simulator, given “refused”, outputs that value. With the ζ of Theorem 6.35
this gives the bound 2−154 . The sum in the display is the bound of a second Lean theorem, whose proof
takes two steps of the same form at that number. Taken at that number, the proof term of Theorem 6.35
gives the sum as its bound too; no named Lean theorem states this, and the step has not been compiled.
The converse, from Theorem 6.37 to Theorem 6.35, is not proved.
Remark 6.40 (Relation to index privacy). Zero knowledge with request leakage (Definition 6.34) differs from
index privacy (Definition 2.14) in three respects: each batch is one revealed instance; the set W need not lie
in the relation, and in Theorem 6.37 it contains instances that are not served; and the simulator is given the
request leakage. In Theorem 6.35 only the first respect is left: its environments are those valid at M for SM
the set of instances served at M , and a constant leakage carries nothing. It is not index privacy for batches of
at most one either: an empty batch and a hidden instance, which Definition 2.14 admits, are not covered; for
this form Lean has the other direction alone, and no Lean theorem derives index privacy from it. That zero
knowledge in the forms of Theorem 6.27 and Theorem 6.37 is index privacy is not stated: no Lean theorem
derives index privacy of Πlp
Z from Theorem 6.37.
Remark 6.41 (From the IOPs to the argument).
1. Limits carry over. Completeness and zero knowledge after compilation follow from the two results before
compilation by a conditional theorem on the compilation, so every limit of these two holds for them as
well.
2. Zero knowledge. The Lean proof of Theorem 6.37 passes through Theorem C.25 on the whole-key
argument: inside its proof all twenty conditions are met by the Lean provers, at one copy of the table. No
Lean theorem states that by name; we have it from a reading of the proof’s calls (Open problem 8.13).
3. From the whole key to 256 bits. The statement of Theorem 6.37 itself concerns the 256-bit commitment.
The step from the whole key to that commitment is made inside the proof, in two parts. The whole-key
argument is first carried from its separate oracles at fixed (dg, par) to the one oracle H, with the readings
computed from it; this leaves the bound unchanged, and it is used as a bound for all values of (dg, par).
Then a verifier against Πlp
Z is run by a verifier against this argument, which computes H(code(k, dgZ ))
from the key by one query of its own and forms each proof from the key and a proof of that argument.
So the bound of Theorem C.25 is used at q + 1 queries in place of q, and no term is added.
4. Completeness. Completeness does not pass through the reduction of Remark 6.49.
Limits common to these results.
Remark 6.42 (Scope of the results). Theorem 6.23, Theorem 6.27, Theorem 6.29, Theorem 6.35, and
Theorem 6.37 and part 3 of Theorem 6.31 are for fresh random masks and coins, one commitment, and one
request per run and per proof, not for batches. The theorems after compilation hold at the exact-match
policy, and Theorem 6.29, Theorem 6.35, and Theorem 6.37 for one copy of the table. Before compilation
exact match is not a hypothesis; an output that the policy of Z would accept and that is not the model’s
own is not served, and nothing is stated about it. Every admissible configuration has the layout file of Z0
and its parameter file except for eight numbers, with θ ≥ 28 (Definition 3.8), so every result that asks for an
admissible Z is at the default configuration’s size.
Remark 6.43 (Definitions in Lean). The results concern the Lean provers exactly as written, default values
included. By their definition in Lean, each prover’s coins are uniform and fresh in each run, and, if
lp
(A, ξ) ∈
/ Serviop
Z , the abort set of Preq at (A, ξ) contains every list of challenges. That the provers are not
37

trivial follows from the results together (Remark 6.46, The two together). These are definitions, not programs
that run: for instance, the model’s run on a request is obtained by a non-constructive choice. Nothing is
stated about the released programs (the C++ verifier and committer, the CUDA prover).
Remark 6.44 (Not hidden).
1. The bit before compilation. Before compilation one bit per request is not hidden: whether the request
is served, which depends on the weights. The simulator is given that bit, and where a request is not
served the prover shows nothing. A bit says whether the claimed output is the model’s result on that
request, so over many requests the bits tell what the model answers.
2. The bit after compilation. After compilation one bit per request is revealed (served or refused). The bit
depends on the weights, and it is declared, not hidden: for every request of its choice a verifier learns
whether the claimed output is the model’s result. (It reads the bit off the reply: a refused request gets
an empty proof; so, with small probability, does a served one, where a search for a nonce runs out.)
3. Many requests. So over many requests, chosen adaptively, the verifier learns what the model outputs on
inputs of its choice: whoever tries each possible token for a prompt learns the model’s next one from
this bit alone, unless all are refused.
4. What the bit keeps back. Against three labels the bit hides one thing: whether a refusal means that the
model has no result or that its result is other tokens. The bit hides that only from a verifier that asks
few requests. A request’s window (the 1,024 tokens that the model’s run reads: the prompt, the claimed
output, then zeros) can itself be sent as a prompt, with one more token as the claimed output, which
the run does not read: a verifier that tries every value of that token and is refused each time learns that
the model has no result on that window, and one that is served once learns that it has one (argued from
the definitions; no theorem states it). Each such search costs up to 202,048 of the 688,127 replies: one
for each token of the vocabulary (Definition 3.1).
5. Further. A true output is refused if the model has no value further on in the window. No statement
bounds what many bits reveal together. In the corollary one label per request is revealed (served, failed
or other tokens), and it depends on the weights.
Remark 6.45 (The model).
1. Order of choices. In all results after compilation the files of Z are fixed before the hash function is
sampled; in Theorem 6.29, Theorem 6.35, and Theorem 6.37 and in part 3 of Theorem 6.31 so are the
weights. In Theorem 6.35 and Theorem 6.37 the verifier has no hash query before the commitment.
2. Second hash function. A second, fixed hash function inside the format, H ′ , is not treated as random
(Assumption 4.11).
3. Running time. The simulators’ running time is not modelled.
4. The configuration and timing. Neither completeness nor zero knowledge says anything about what the
files of Z themselves reveal about the weights, or about timing.
5. Figures. The figures on zero knowledge are statistical distances (2−135 , 2−154 ) or bounds on a probability
(2−174 ), not a security level.
Remark 6.46 (The two together). A prover that sends fixed messages and aborts at every request that is not
served, or whose abort set has a public part that contains every list of challenges, also satisfies a statement
of the form of Theorem 6.27. Completeness of the same provers excludes this, so the two results are to
be read together. One Lean theorem, at every Z in use, states round-by-round soundness (Remark 6.21,
item 1), Theorem 6.23 and part 1 of Theorem 6.27 of one term for FlashProveZ and one triple of provers.
That term is the one from which VZ , the verifier of Theorem 5.1, is compiled. Likewise Theorem 6.29,
Theorem 6.31, Theorem 6.35, and Theorem 6.37 concern one committer, one prover and one verifier, those of
Πlp
Z ; in Corollary 6.38 the label is added to the prover’s output, and the verifier ignores it (Definition 6.34).
Remark 6.47 (Definitions of the properties). The definitions of this subsection restate definitions of the Lean
library. Before compilation, the definitions of completeness and of honest-verifier zero knowledge are this
38

library’s own and follow no published definition word for word; after compilation Theorem 6.29 and Theorem 6.35 are stated through CompleteOn and ZeroKnowledgeOn, the texts of Complete and ZeroKnowledge,
each with one condition more, that the request can be served (Definition 3.11); with the condition that the
output is the model’s, these are the served requests; the forms of Theorem 6.37 and Corollary 6.38 are the
library’s own. The definitions before compilation, and the Lean statements of Theorem 6.23, Theorem 6.27,
Theorem 6.37 and Corollary 6.38 other than the six top theorems and the final statement’s six fields, have not
been independently reviewed (“Who wrote and checked this”).
Remark 6.48 (How they are checked). Of the library’s six top theorems the independent checker has accepted
sound (Remark 6.16), repliesFit and refusedHandsOutNoProof (Proposition 6.33) by name, over a build
of the released Lean files; over such a build the three on binding, completeness and zero knowledge have
been checked by Lean’s kernel alone, and they rest on equations between closed terms that the kernel decides
by computation. For those of the Lean theorems behind them that are named in lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md,
Lean prints no axiom beyond its three standard ones. The reference run (the 109 calls of Appendix H.3)
has been made over the released Lean files, and the independent checker has been run, on the soundness
theorem’s export and on those of the theorems named at this remark’s head, as the exporter writes them
over the released Lean files’ build; what it accepted: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md; of the theorems of this section
only those named at this remark’s head are among what it accepted. A whole run of the library’s full
check over all but one of the released Lean files (Remark B.17) passed at every step; its last line says that
no theorem got a verdict on binders or on Classical.choice (the whole line, and what the check does
not cover: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md; Remark B.17). What these checks are: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md; the
limits of the independent checker’s pass, and the version of Lean: Remark B.17. The Lean modules of
Theorem 6.23, Theorem 6.27, Theorem 6.29, Theorem 6.31, Theorem 6.35, Theorem 6.37, and Corollary 6.38,
of Proposition 6.17, part 1, and of Proposition 6.33, parts 1 and 4, import the separate library of Remark 5.7; by
import lines at least one of the modules on which the re-checker stopped lies beneath each of them. By a walk
over the constants of a build of the released Lean files, proofs included (a program’s finding, no theorem; it covers
twenty-three modules), the Lean theorems finalSoundAndBinding_value, finalHandsOutNoProof_value
and finalRefusedHandsOutNoProof_value reach no constant of these modules; each of the theorems on
completeness and zero knowledge listed above reaches at least one, but for Theorem 6.35, whose one Lean
theorem was not among those walked; the modules of a third library, on which these theorems rest too, were
not put to the re-checker; of the other Lean theorems that the listed statements cite it is not known.

6.6

A reduction for completeness, and what is open

Remark 6.49 (Completeness in Lean). Besides the results of Section 6.5, on completeness the following is
machine-checked.
1. A reduction. Let µ be the model parameters (Definition 3.6) that the files of Z fix. If thirteen conditions
hold at (Z, α), then VZ , with the committer and prover built from a proving side given as a parameter,
is complete on Servµ with error α: an output that the model itself gives is rejected with probability at
most α. Three conditions are on the files: they parse, Z is admissible, and Chk(Z) = 1.
Ten are on the proving side. One, the largest, asks at every instance true under Z’s policy, whether it
can be served or not, that each of the three IOP verifiers accept its prover’s run, at OOD seeds that are
not excluded and at every challenge list that satisfies each proof-of-work check, outside an abort set
and an exceptional set; the probabilities of the three sets enter α. It also asks that each prover’s input
oracles encode the index, that a challenge list lie in the union of these two sets whenever zeroing its
proof-of-work bits gives a list in it, and that the request prover send the copy number handed to it.
Three fix how the messages and coins of the key and request provers fit the byte format, and five
constrain a salt length, a tree depth and the three search limits. The last asks that α dominate these
probabilities, the excluded seeds’ twice, and the failure probability of the searches, added up. The only
acceptance assumed is that of the three IOP verifiers.
2. Conditions that hold. The three on the files, at Z0 and at every Z that differs from it only in the policy,
in its three numbers, within the bounds of Proposition 5.6 (Remark 5.7, How the proposition is checked).
For admissible Z with Chk(Z) = 1: what the largest condition asks of the link proof, for Plink with

39

empty exceptional set and an abort set of probability 0 (Theorem 6.9); the three search limits, at 2b+6
attempts per b-bit proof of work, where the searches add at most 71 · 2−92 to α; and the salt length and
the depth, which are met by choice. For the same Z, in the whole-key argument at the fixed digest 0λ ,
wherever πlink ̸= ⊥, the verifier of IOPlink
Z , not the compiled one, also accepts Plink at the challenges
derived by hashing (Remark C.30; Remark 5.7).
3. Searches succeed. In the whole-key argument with constant-message provers and at least 240 attempts
per search, which is more than in item 2, Corollary 6.7 bounds Pr[π = ⊥] for each kind of proof π; on
acceptance it is silent.
Whether the Lean provers (Section 6.5) meet the ten conditions is not stated here. All thirteen are shown
together only for idle provers, which always abort; then α ≥ 3 and the bound is vacuous. In the reduction
the parts of the offline and the online prover are not shown, so that it gives no bound below 1. The proving
side is a parameter, not that of ΠZ ; the reduction does not bound its queries, and no statement relates it
to the released programs. Outside Servµ (Definition 3.11) nothing is stated. For no valid µ is Servµ empty
(Proposition 3.12(a)), but its members are exhibited at constructed weights (more at Z0 : Remark 3.13). For one
µ there are valid weights M , constructed too, for which Servµ contains no triple (M, x, y) (Proposition 3.12(b));
by a further Lean theorem that µ is the model parameters of Z0 .
Completeness and zero knowledge of the whole protocol, with batches, are open problems (Open problem 8.12 and Open problem 8.14).

7

Evaluation

7.1

Set-up

The implementation has a committer, a prover and a verifier in C++ and CUDA (the C++ verifier
(Limitation 8.1, Lean verifier only)), an integration with vLLM [KLZ23], and the command lean-verify,
which evaluates the executable form (Definition 5.4) on the prover’s proof files. Weights are synthetic, generated
by the implementation, or public: a third party’s mostly 4-bit quantization of Llama 4 Maverick [Uns25], not
Meta’s checkpoint, converted to the integer model (Construction D.3).
Timed runs use one H100 (80 GB) of an eight-GPU host with 96 CPU cores: in the committing and
serving runs the programs were bound to 24 of these cores, all of the GPU’s NUMA node, under a limit of
26 cores and 463.9 GB of memory for the container; as reported, when a machine was assigned the host’s
other seven GPUs were in use by other tenants; whether the cores were reserved to a run, and what ran
beside it later, the records do not tell; the prover’s process held at most 257.1 GB of host memory while
serving at 24 requests a batch on the public weight files (269.7 GB in its container, file cache not counted);
on synthetic weights both were higher (265.5 GB and 278.3 GB), and at 64 a batch on the public files the
container reached 278.3 GB; the container held more with the middleware’s check beside the prover, and both
held more in runs not on the released programs† (Appendix H.2); the committer’s process held at most 286.9
GB (299.6 GB). A largest size or peak is rounded up, the lower end of a range cut.
Each request is proved on a window of 1,024 tokens and receives its own proof. Lean’s statements on
completeness and zero knowledge do not cover batches. A run is one commitment and one or two executions
of the prover on one host; where there are two, its figure is their mean. Masking on means that the committer
samples the secret seed and no test option is set (Appendix H.2); fitted and reduced configurations are defined
in Section 3.3.
A result with no mark is of a run that Appendix H.1 names (the runs of lean-verify: the next paragraph):
a run on the released programs, which are the committer, the prover and the C++ verifier as the release’s
sources build them (the programs that made these runs were built from sources that differ from the released
ones in comments, in the names of identifiers and files, in quoted texts that hold such a name and in the
wording of messages, and in nothing else: Appendix H.1, “Which programs ran”); the timed runs are among
these, and have a part of their own there. A result with no mark that Appendix H.1 does not name is to be
taken as not on the released programs (Appendix H.1, “What this paper tells of other programs”). A dagger
(† ) marks a result not on the released programs, of any other programs, which is not shown to hold for the
released programs; a double dagger (‡ ) marks the test programs of the second and the third test of hiding

40

(Table 6), built from the sources of other programs (two of the fault injection’s three from a copy of these in
which four files differ: Section 7.5), which do not ship.
The runs of lean-verify told here were made over Lean files that differ in code from the released ones,
but for the calls of the reference run and of the second set of 41, which were made over the released Lean
files (Appendix H.3). Those marked † used scripts that differ from the released ones, in code too (one lacked
a bound on the length of a number that it reads), and a parser compiled from sources that differ from the
released ones, in code too (its hashing class among what differs; the rest not compared file by file). The
calls of a separate build of the executable form are another thing (Appendix H.3). Appendix H gives the
conditions, the provenance and the limits of each table (Limitation G.7, No independent review).

