Appendix H.2); its unmarked rows are of the timed runs. At exact match a proof covers the model’s run
over the whole prompt and checks one output token, the one that follows the prompt. Rates are steady-state
rates: they count every prefill token of a window, checked or not, and exclude start-up and committing. A
steady rate counts every interval between two releases. With start-up counted, in batches of 24 at exact
match, each of the three runs passes 100 tokens per second (102.65 to 104.24); of the three sessions with whole
outputs checked, and of their control, one each stays under it (99.33; 98.12). At the steady rate the slowest
two executions gave 110.07 and 110.33.
Three runs made side by side say little about other times and machines: three runs of the same kind on
other programs† , on another day on other machines, gave 105.3 ± 3.9 tokens/second (100.85 to 108.06); there
each service’s last interval is not counted; whether the programs, the machines, the day or that rule made the
difference is not shown (Appendix H.2). A batch is taken from the queue just before the responses of the
batch before it are released, so a request waits a little longer than the time between two releases: 220.7 to
225.9 seconds from a batch’s start to its release, from the second batch on, in each case 1.6 to 2.3 seconds
more than the interval that ends at that release. That wait ends at a proof on which no verifier has run. Of
what ships, the middleware of the vLLM integration hands on no response that has a proof before the C++
verifier has accepted every proof of its batch (Limitation G.16); that time is not in these figures. In one run of
its own on the released programs, none of the timed runs, on stored proofs of one batch on synthetic weights,
with no inference engine in front and no prover running beside it, on 24 CPUs of a 32-CPU machine, the
middleware’s check of the 24 proofs, at its default there of six checks at once with one thread each, took 94.0
seconds from the hand-over of the proofs to the batch’s release; no spread is known. Beside a serving prover
on the same 24 CPUs of another machine, in one more run on synthetic weights and on stored proofs, not that
prover’s own, at the middleware’s default, six checks at once with one thread each, each of five checks took
88.74 to 89.15 seconds (alone on that machine the check took 92.1 seconds before the services and 90.3 after

41

them; what else ran there was not held the same), and the prover’s steady rate was 109.64 tokens per second,
against 110.11 in that run’s second service with no check beside it; in the timed runs on synthetic weights two
such services part by 0.1 to 0.7 % with nothing beside them, so no slowing can be told from one run.
On other programs† , in the runs that answer to the three rows of Table 3 under a clipped gap (105.38,
37.9 and 105.26), the rates differed, row by row, by 8.0 to 9.0 % (the most over the least), and no cause is
shown. The difference between the means there and in the timed runs is matched by the control’s and is
smaller than the spread there; the timed runs had the lighter CPU load; no effect of the programs is shown.
The configuration of these runs is the default one (the clipped-gap rows and their control apart from
their policy) as fitted to the public weights; the three configuration files of one set of timed exact-match runs
on these weights pass the scope test for an admissible configuration, which Theorem 5.1 requires, by one
evaluation of a test that does not ship (no theorem); whether they are byte for byte the files behind the runs
reported here is not shown, and the parameter checks were not evaluated at them (Remark D.5, No real
weights shown). The jobs’ own calls of the C++ verifier, behind the timed stretches, accepted every proof; in
these jobs the executable form was given none. Under the clipped-gap policy the accepting last line is WITHIN
LIMITS, not YES.
Batches of 64 are not to be relied on with such a GPU: on synthetic weights, in the timed runs, one call of
the prover on 64 requests and one attempt at a service of five such batches, one run each, ended at a failed
CUDA call, the service in its first batch, with 0 of 64 and 0 of 320 requests proved; every attempt at 64 on
synthetic weights on record failed, eight in all, six of them on other programs† (Appendix H.2). The three
runs at 64 on the public weights completed, with all 960 proofs accepted and at most 80,629 MiB of 81,559
MiB in use. At exact match, in batches of 24, a proof file has 18.74 to 18.81 MB.

7.3

The executable form on the prover’s proofs

At the format’s hash function, at the default configuration’s size and on synthetic weights, the executable
form accepted (by lean-verify; four of them by a separate build of the executable form alone, Table 4), in
calls that ran to their end, every proof from an honest run of the prover counted here. At exact match 363
were made by one prover built from other sources† , the part handed over of the 723 that it made (not the 723
that the three timed runs on synthetic weights made, 241 a run): 360 with one output token (96 requests of
one shape from one list, each proved under the same three commitments, 24 of them twice under each) and
three of one stored instance.
Eighteen were made by other provers than that one: seven of that instance, nine of three others, and two
beside the malicious proofs of the true claim of Appendix H.3, one of them of one of those three.
Under the clipped gap of Z1 it accepted one by that prover† and four by others, as reported, all of one
further instance.
A second set of 41 honest proofs, from the timed runs’ own files on synthetic weights, counted apart
from the 109 calls of Appendix H.3, has been put to the Lean verifier: each of three runs’ files holds proofs
numbered 000 to 119 and one more; taken were that one and 000, 009 and so on, to 108 in two runs (13
each) and to 099 in the third (12): 3 + 13 + 13 + 12 = 41; it accepted each, at exact match, one call each.
These are other files than the 41 of the 363 that are among the 109 calls. Like the 109 calls, the calls on
them were made over the released Lean files; the command had accepted each of them before, over Lean files
that differ from the released ones; 7 of their 14 requests are among those of the 47 honest proofs of the 109.
Appendix H.3 gives their provenance.
Under the clipped gap lean-verify has met honest proofs alone; a separate build of the executable form
rejected the two proofs of false claims of Table 4.
lean-verify and a separate build of the executable form together rejected all 26 proofs of claims false by
the run’s own policy (Table 4) — 24 and two — all from one test program, claims fixed beforehand, each
rejected where the proof file is read.
With the form of the command marked † in Section 7.1, the library built in advance, three calls† on one
stored proof, made one at a time for the timing, one after another on one shared CPU machine (an Intel Xeon
Platinum 8375C, 64 CPUs), each accepted it, in 1,393 to 1,440 seconds, with at most 14.13 GB of memory in
a call’s largest process, in these three calls. What an evaluation trusts, how narrow these proofs are, and
what a reader can repeat: Appendix H.3.

