Section 6 concerns the protocol; for the programs, their goal, weight hiding (Definition H.1), is tested. We
judge that weight hiding likely holds at the default configuration, in honest runs, if the provider keeps its
operating conditions and the judgement’s own conditions (listed in docs/limits.md, not in this paper) hold.
This judgement is given of the released programs: the first and the fifth of its five tests (Table 6) have run
on them, under exact match, with the outcomes that Table 6 and Appendix H.5 tell, both at the reduced
configuration on synthetic weights and the first at the default configuration too, once on synthetic weights
and once, as fitted to them, on the public weights; the fifth ran once. The second and the third ran on test
programs and the fourth on other programs, all three built from sources that differ from the released ones.
The one reading of whole files for hiding was of a text from which the released one differs. It took in 384 files:
all that a production build of the committer and the prover from that text reads, but for the C++ verifier’s
own 36. The released sources differ in code in 68 of the 384, and in 136 more in comments, names and the
wording of messages alone; two more of the 384 are not in the released sources; in the 68 the differences in
code were read for hiding, by difference alone, apart from renamed names and reworded messages, and the
files were not read whole again (Appendix H.5). That judgement rests on the design, what is proved in Lean
for the protocol (Section 6; what that leaves out: Limitation 8.2 and Limitation 8.3), code review and tests; it
is not a proof, it does not cover a real model’s weights, and neither the programs nor the judgement has had
an independent review. Only one short search has looked for a leak: in two sets of honest published files,

44

three proofs among them, at the reduced configuration, not on the released programs† . It made several looks:
at the OOD answers, for guessable seeds, and over the opened columns of the stored tables; of the proofs it
studied nothing else by value. It found nothing, which is worth little: on the first of these sets the first look
that it made cannot see any fault of a pad. Beyond that search no proof has been searched for a leak, and no
file at the default configuration.
None of that judgement’s five tests searches for a leak: they test that the masks are there and that a loss
is noticed. The tests behind it ran at the reduced configuration, on synthetic weights; only one of the five has
also run on a real model’s weights, once on the released programs and three times on other programs† , at the
default configuration as fitted to those weights. That judgement reaches, on less evidence, a configuration
that differs from the default one in a clipped-gap policy’s three numbers alone. Its whole scope, its conditions,
and what is not shown or untested: Appendix H.5.
A self-check is a program’s recomputation of masked values; an audit is a shipped tool that recomputes
part of the published bytes from the provider’s secrets. Of the fault injection’s three test programs two were
made from a copy, in which four files differ, of the sources of other programs. In Table 6 losses were unnoticed
in three places: 30 of 165 injected losses of random values in CPU memory; 9 of 9 at samples that no self-check
covers; and, with one self-check disabled, at a parameter file that counted no proof of work, a commitment
without masks, which passed every other check.
In one trial, not on the released programs, on a weight file with a tensor of 16 bits, which that judgement
does not cover, a party that could write that file but not read it learned the magnitudes of two weights of
their choice (Limitation G.18, One trial with such a file).

