In no reported test did the C++ verifier program accept an output that violates the run’s own policy
(Table 7 and Table 4); most of these tests ran not on the released programs. On proof files the released
verifier’s record is chiefly this (the whole of it: Appendix H.1): it rejected all 57 that malicious provers made
at the default configuration’s size, at exact match and under one commitment, on synthetic weights, and that
were put to it, 24 of them claiming a false output and 33 the true output with a falsification inside; and, at
the small example’s setting, the 228 acceptances on 57 kept sets of files in which an edited committer had
altered one stored table (at 1 to 131,072 of its 8,388,608 positions; the four full checks on each set; six of the
sets a second making of six others) all carry a true claim, by a reading, not machine-checked (the release’s
docs/limits-all-rows.tsv, rows LIM-63.1 and SO-15.7; Appendix H.1). Few falsifications test whether
a false claim can pass: each was fixed beforehand, and a rejection shows that one is caught, not that none
passes.
The fourth row of Table 7 is not a test of that program: there, test programs call the library beneath it
directly, against the library’s stated contract (docs/limits.md): a list of rules for C++ code that calls it,
among them to name no private member of the library, to declare nothing at global scope before its headers,
and to keep operator new. They run on small inputs of their own, which are not configurations. At the
45

128-bit target, on these small inputs, they had false claims accepted by at least two ways: in one, at all 9 tries
that were carried through; in another, on synthetic data, at 8 of 48 tries against a control of 0 of 48. There
may be others.
That the verifier program cannot reach the library’s open ways rests on readings of that program’s source,
not on a test; the source read differs in places from the released one (Appendix H.1).
The altered tables that were accepted decode, by separate programs’ counts of the changed columns (no
decoder was run; Appendix H.1), to the unaltered weights, which the design allows: an accepted proof is
meant to show that the committed table decodes to the weights, not that its bytes are their honest encoding.
No malicious program was built from the released sources, and none searches for a false proof (Appendix H.6).

7.7

The integer model against floating point

On the public weights the integer model’s greedy token differs from that of the reference, public copies of the
16-bit weights (two, the same bit for bit) evaluated in float32, at 6.81 % of token positions on 216 texts and
at 9.41 % on the 24 natural passages among them (Python code† , no proofs; the same two shares, to the
digits shown, in each of three runs). The count behind the first share was not the same in each run, and the
cause is not known. All texts are built on WikiText-2 [MXBS17], on which the scales were fitted, and no task
benchmark was run (docs/limits.md) (Limitation G.9, Quality unmeasured).

7.8

Comparison

Per GPU, proving alone takes 71.4 times (65.2 to 76.4 over all pairs of runs), 93.0 times (92.2 to 94.1 over
all pairs of runs) and 20.1 times (19.7 to 20.5 over all pairs of runs) the GPU time of stock vLLM [KLZ23]
for prefill of 24 windows at once, prefill at the highest load tried, and decoding at 512 streams (n = 3; the
engine’s n = 30, 8, 5). The engine served the FP8 checkpoint on eight H100s, on other machines; the multiples
are calculated from the first rate of Table 3, omit committing and the serving that exact match requires
(Section 7.4), and could be higher, since the engine’s rates are lower bounds.
Table 8 sets our figures beside those of four earlier systems. By a simulation of 17 such machines [GHL26,
Table 6], DeepProve’s rate for GPT-2 rises to 1,855 tokens per minute.
Llama 4 Maverick is a mixture of experts: every second layer has 128 routed experts, of which a token
uses one, together with a shared expert. Of the 400.7 billion committed weights, one token’s forward pass
uses 16.15 billion (computed from the parameter file; the whole output head and the one embedding row that
a token reads are counted). Each request’s proof also does work over all 400.7 billion committed weights, once
per request and not once per token. The rate is therefore not comparable, parameter for parameter, with the
rates for dense models.

8

Limitations and open problems

8.1

Limitations

The release lists the gaps that we know of, and no attack on the protocol is known to us at any gap while its
conditions are kept, the opening budget among them (Limitation 8.4, Opening budget). Almost no attack
was sought: that none is described is not a finding of safety. Table 16 names the release’s pages.
Theorems and proofs concern the protocol, or Lean’s IOPs and VZ ; measurements and tests concern the
implementation. Nothing is proved about the released programs, and no security level is claimed for them.
Between the theorem and the command lean-verify the gap is smaller than between the theorem and the
C++ programs (Limitation 8.1 (Lean verifier only); Limitation 8.2 (Protocol match unproved)): lean-verify
evaluates the proved verifier’s executable form (Remark 5.9, What a client gets).
Limitation 8.1 (Lean verifier only). Theorem 5.1 concerns VZ , not the C++ verifier, which clients run by
default. Nothing is proved about that program, and no soundness is claimed for the programs as they run:
an acceptance has the theorem’s meaning only if VZ gives it, and neither 124 bits per query nor 2−128 per
round is stated of the C++ programs. No theorem states that the two decide alike on any file, or bounds a
program’s probability of accepting a false claim.

