Limitation 8.2 (Protocol match unproved). That Lean’s IOPs (Remark B.7, Which compiler) are the protocol
of the C++ programs is not proved, nor that they are the technical report’s [TP]: no theorem relates them,
and the only evidence is the runs. In the one comparison of hash calls, on the stored proof at Z0 , a logging
build of the C++ verifier† made 881,280 of the executable form’s 1,166,983 calls, with equal answers; the
rest are explained only in part, a gap that the release rates as heavy. On that proof both decide alike; on
three altered copies of an honest proof (one bit turned in a digest the form never reads) the executable form
accepted the parsed file where a C++ verifier and a parser, both of other programs, rejected the raw proof; the
claim was true (lean/GAPS.md, gap 66). These calls were made over Lean files that differ from the released
ones. Over the released Lean files the decision on the stored proof has been repeated (Appendix H.3); the
comparison of hash calls and the calls on the altered copies have no run over the released Lean files. VZ has
sixteen tests that the C++ verifier lacks (lean/GAPS.md, gap 25).

47

Limitation 8.3 (Zero knowledge of definitions in Lean). The provers for which zero knowledge is machinechecked (Section 6.5) are the Lean provers: definitions in Lean, exactly as written, default values included.
On the C++ programs nothing is stated. It is stated at the default configuration’s size, after compilation at
the exact-match policy, with fresh random masks, for one commitment and one request per proof, not for
batches; before compilation for an honest verifier; after compilation in the random-oracle model, on one copy
of the committed table, up to 688,127 replies and 264 hash queries. All of it holds where Z is admissible with
Chk(Z) = 1, after compilation in use (Definition 6.11). Not hidden is one bit per request, before and after
compilation: whether the request is served, which depends on the weights; in the corollary, one label per
request (served, failed or other tokens). A bit says whether the claimed output is the model’s result on that
request, so over many requests the bits tell what the model answers. Nothing is said of what the files of Z
themselves tell of the weights, or of timing. The simulators’ running time is not modelled (docs/limits.md).
Nor is derived randomness covered (Remark G.15, Pseudorandom masks). At a refused request the Lean
prover returns the empty proof. The programs’ service answers “failed” for every request that it does not prove,
whatever the cause; a client can partly tell the causes apart (docs/limits-all-rows.tsv, row HW-532).
That answer is neither the bit nor the corollary’s label “failed”, and no theorem ties them. That the released
programs hide the weights is the design’s aim, not an established property (Section 1.1); our judgement of it:
Section 7.5.
Limitation 8.4 (Opening budget). A committed table can open only t columns before its random coefficients
are used up, with no margin (Proposition 6.5). Beyond that, its proofs give whoever holds them exact linear
equations, permanently and unnoticed: t + k columns, 1 ≤ k ≤ d 2n0 , give k independent equations modulo p
in each row’s weight digits and pads and, for k > 3s, at least k − 3s in its digits alone (Proposition C.16). By
a reading of other sources, a row that the prover keeps from one segment of a request’s proof to the next is
encoded anew for each segment, and each encoding has 219 random coefficients, for the 219 positions that a
proof opens in it, without slack: a number calculated from the default configuration and taken from no run;
that it is enough is not proved, and it is enough only if each new encoding’s random coefficients are fresh,
which by the code’s own list of conditions no function there tests. A commitment serves boundedly many
batches. The released programs count batches on each copy, at most 688,127 at the default configuration
(calculated), and no program counts proofs (Limitation 8.2, Protocol match unproved). Behind the programs’
counter no Lean theorem stands: nothing in Lean keeps count of the columns that a table has opened. The
688,127 of the compiled zero-knowledge result (Section 6.5) counts another thing: replies with one request to
a proof, on one copy of the committed table. So a provider within the programs’ count is not thereby within
that result. No program detects a count copied, restored or rolled back (docs/limits.md).
Limitation 8.5 (Provider-chosen configuration). The provider, not the verifier, chooses the configuration:
all three files for the programs, and for Theorem 5.1 whatever may vary in an admissible configuration.
Theorem 5.1 needs it fixed in advance, before the oracle is sampled and independently of the adversary (Open
problem 8.16); one tuned to the concrete hash function is outside Theorem 5.1. The C++ verifier accepts
forms of configuration that no statement covers, some of the release’s examples among them.
Limitation 8.6 (Scales can disable blocks). The scale file of an admissible configuration can disable any chosen
subset of the model’s 96 attention and feed-forward blocks, up to all of them, whatever the weights, so the
client must read it. This was worked out by hand and is not a theorem.
Limitation 8.7 (Unattributed commitment). As in every commit-and-prove argument, a commitment does not
say whose weights, or what model’s, were committed: the provider chooses which weights, if any, it fixes, and
a client has only the word of whoever published it. A client needs the commitment before it sends a prompt.
Limitation 8.8 (Clipped gap is weak). Under a clipped-gap policy an acceptance states only “within limits”,
which is much less than exact match (unless θ = 28 < C, where an output meets the policy if and only if it
meets exact match). Otherwise, meeting the policy bounds the sum of the counted tokens’ clipped costs and
the number of them that cost more than the clip, not each token (Remark 3.10, What a policy bounds), and,
by design, does not show whether any given counted token is the greedy token. With C = θ the bound on
the sum says nothing; if cap is also at least the number of counted tokens, as at (256, 256, 1024), the count
says nothing either. VZ does not reject such a configuration, and there only the token after a full window is
certified (lean/THEOREM.md).
48

Limitation 8.9 (Completeness on served requests only). Lean proves soundness of VZ ; of its completeness it
proves, besides the reduction of Remark 6.49, only that, on served requests, it rejects the Lean prover’s proof
with probability at most 2−40 , in the random-oracle model, where the files of Z are in use (Z admissible,
Chk(Z) = 1, exact-match policy), for one request to a proof and one commitment (Section 6.5). Where the
model has no result, or another result than the claimed output, nothing is stated. No honest proof exists for
a window on which the model is not defined. The Lean prover is a definition: that VZ accepts the programs’
honest proofs is tested, on proofs made on other programs and on 41 of the timed runs’ own on synthetic
weights, at the two stored configurations Z0 and Z1 alone (Remark B.6, Acceptance), not proved. These calls
were made over Lean files that differ from the released ones, but for the calls of the reference run and of the
second set of 41, which were made over the released Lean files (Appendix H.3). Nor does Theorem 5.11 show
that VZ accepts any non-greedy output. A gap in completeness could only cost a visible rejection: it cannot
make VZ accept a false claim beyond the bound of Theorem 5.1, under its hypotheses, provided the functions
that form the claim are right (Remark B.10, Claim from files).
Limitation 8.10 (Released commitment). The released commitment has 32 bytes and hashes the three
configuration files, the first table’s Merkle root, its out-of-domain answers and the out-of-domain seed. That it
ties the provider to one set of weights is not proved for the released programs: what is proved of a commitment
is proved of the committer compiled from the Lean provers (Section 6.5) and of an explicit committer that
models part of CommitZ (Open problem 8.11). Of a commitment a proof can certify at most the matrix to
which the first table decodes, not that the table’s bytes are its honest encoding, and the commitment does
not fix the offline proof in the key file: one set of weights can have several commitments, one commitment
several key files, and a proof is tied to its copy.
Appendix G states the further limitations: the implementation is unverified; only greedy outputs in a fixed
window are covered, and prefixes count; the hash function is modelled as a random oracle, and adversaries
are classical; there is no independent review, there are few runs, and the model’s quality is unmeasured;
outcomes are visible by design; and eleven limitations concern how the released programs are operated.
Among the eleven: a further commitment to the same weights, which renewal or a second GPU requires, is
inside the judgement of Section 7.5 only on thin ground and on five conditions, and no test has searched two
commitments together for leaks (Limitation G.12, Further commitments).

8.2

Open problems

Open problem 8.11 (Extraction and binding for the released committer). [open] Prove correctness
of extraction and extractability of the commitment (Definition 2.9 and Definition 2.10) for CommitZ of the
released programs. For the committer compiled from the Lean provers both are proved (Section 6.5). The hash
function is drawn at random: these are statements in the random-oracle model. They hold at public files in
use; the extractor is a function, not shown computable. The committer is a Lean term that no theorem ties to
a program. For CommitZ of the released programs nothing is proved: the explicit committer (Definition 5.16)
models only part of it, and was compared, in values alone, with the committing program of other programs†
(Table 4).
Each property that names the honest parties requires its own proof (Remark 4.16, Honest parties).
Open problem 8.12 (Completeness beyond served requests). [open] State and prove completeness of
the compiled term, VZ with the Lean committer and prover (Section 6.5; Remark 4.16 (Honest parties)), at
well-formed requests on which the model has no result, and prove it for batches.
Open problem 8.13 (The twenty conditions as a theorem). [open] State, as a theorem of its own, that
the three Lean provers (Section 6.5) meet the twenty conditions of Theorem C.25.
Zero knowledge of the Lean provers is machine-checked (Section 6.5). For the results on the whole-key
argument the step to ΠZ is not proved; the compiled statement of Section 6.5, which is on Πlp
Z (Definition 6.15),
needs no such step for the commitment, which is the 256-bit one, or for the verifier; for the honest parties of
ΠZ the step is not proved (Open problem 8.14). Batches are not covered.

49

Open problem 8.14 (Zero knowledge of the whole protocol). [open] Prove index privacy of ΠZ with
its honest parties, for batches, on the instances that can be served.
Open problem 8.15 (Many proofs without the factor N ). [open] Find qH such that every run of VZ
makes at most qH queries to H. Bound the error for N proofs sent one after another, each after the decision
on the one before.
Given such a qH , which no theorem provides, the bound ε(q + N qH ), without the factor N , is machinechecked for lists sent at once (Remark B.13, Lists sent at once).
Open problem 8.16 (A configuration chosen after the oracle). [open] Prove index-binding knowledge
soundness (Definition 2.7) of ΠZ against a committer that chooses the admissible configuration Z after
querying H.

9

Related work

Proofs of language-model inference. zkLLM [SLZ24] is interactive, uses a Hyrax commitment
[WTSTW18] and hides the parameters from a semi-honest verifier. Our integer softmax and attention follow
zkLLM, zkGPT [QSL25] and ZKML [CWS24]. zkAgent [WLL26] checks the greedy token by range-checking
the logit differences over the whole vocabulary, as zkCNN [LXZ21, Section 4.3] does for max pooling; at exact
match our protocol’s last segment does the same, by looking up the digits of the differences.
DeepProve [GHL26] proves all generated tokens of an output, for GPT-2 and Gemma 3 models of 124M
and 270M parameters. ZKTorch [CTK25] compiles a model into basic blocks and accumulates their proofs.
ZK-DeepSeek [Wan25] turns DeepSeek-V3, with 671 billion parameters, into an integer model, composes
proofs recursively into one of constant size, without a trusted setup, and reports times for several components.
By its abstract, which is all that we have read of it, zkPyTorch [XLF25] compiles PyTorch models into circuits.
By the passages of its blog post that we have read, Attestable [Attestable] reports early results for a hash-only
zero-knowledge system over committed weights.
Proofs for the same model. VerInf [VerInf], which precedes this work, reports in its README an
interactive, hash-based zero-knowledge proof, on one DGX Spark and 1,000 tokens, for its own integer model
of Llama 4 Maverick, the model whose shape we use. It proves a different statement from ours, a bound on
the “unexplained information” of a continuation, and it hides both the tokens and the weights. Our choice of
public 4-bit weight files [Uns25] comes from VerInf, and our rule for converting the stored weights to integers
grew out of VerInf’s.
One committed model, many proofs. In the literature’s terms the task of ΠZ is close to that of a
function-hiding functional commitment [BNO21]: the committed function is the model, and Corollary B.14
has the form of evaluation binding. We state the likeness only; their definition has not been compared
with ours clause by clause. [GGQZ25] builds such a commitment over KZG for convolutional networks,
and zkCNN [LXZ21, Section 4.1] already has the prover commit to a network’s parameters before proving
predictions. By the passages that we have read, Artemis [LVAKH24] is a commit-and-prove SNARK for any
homomorphic polynomial commitment, and reports cutting the overhead of tying proofs of inference to the
model’s commitment.
Attacks on committed models. Nothing in FlashProve prevents the Hollow-LLM attack [GLL26], in
which weights of the declared size yield valid proofs but compute no more than a much smaller model
(Limitation 8.7, Unattributed commitment). [CPD24] recovers a model’s last layer, up to a linear map, from
many vectors of its logits; whether vectors cut off at the clip suffice is not known, and as far as we know this
has not been tried on these programs (Limitation G.21, Clipped gap reveals logits).
Alternatives to proofs. A trusted execution environment has a reported overhead below 7% on Hopper GPUs for most typical queries [ZYDAZ24], but the client must trust the hardware and its vendor.
TOPLOC [TOPLOC] hashes intermediate activations into 258 bytes per 32 new tokens, and its check is
50

reported to be much faster than the inference. Token-DiFR [DiFR] scores the tokens themselves, at no added
cost to the provider. In both, the verifier runs the model again, so it must hold the weights. Our clipped-gap
policy builds on Token-DiFR’s logit margin at temperature 0, but our verifier needs no weights (Remark 3.10,
What a policy bounds).
Reported numbers. Table 8 sets our figures beside those of four earlier systems, VerInf (as its README
reports them), zkLLM, DeepProve and Attestable. Appendix E.1 and Appendix E.2 give the sources of our
masks, segments, batches and policies.

Who wrote and checked this
Under human direction, AI systems wrote this paper. The software and the Lean code are also almost entirely
their work. The exceptions include code that they adapted from public sources, among them ArkLib and
Mathlib (Appendix E.4) and the BLAKE2 reference code [BLAKE2]; the release’s THIRD_PARTY.md, NOTICE
and lean/CREDITS.md name the sources and what was taken. AI systems also wrote nearly all of the technical
report [TP], except where it marks words as taken from a source.
For a machine-checked statement, Lean’s kernel accepts a proof of each Lean statement that Appendix F
names for it, with two provisos: that kernel is the pinned version’s, of which Remark B.17 tells what is publicly
known, and the re-checker stopped, for the depth of recursion, on constants in 23 modules of a separate library
(counted over Lean files that differ from the released ones) which the build’s kernel accepts at a thread stack
larger than the default (Remark 5.7, How the proposition is checked); Remark 6.48 names the statements
of Section 6.5 whose modules import that library. The kernel does not check what a statement means.
That a Lean statement says what this paper’s statement says is a reading; nothing machine-checks it. Six
definitions of properties in the library (Sound, SoundAndBinding, SoundOpened, Complete, ZeroKnowledge,
CompleteSoundZeroKnowledge; lean/VerifiableInference/Statements.lean) were shaped with a person.
The library’s six top theorems, which the release’s page lean/STATEMENTS.html shows first, are sound,
soundAndBinding, completeOnCanServe, zeroKnowledgeOnCanServe, refusedHandsOutNoProof and
repliesFit; Appendix F names them under Remark 6.16, Theorem 6.31, Theorem 6.29, Theorem 6.35,
and Proposition 6.33. The first two are stated through the first two of these definitions. The third and
the fourth are stated through this library’s CompleteOn and ZeroKnowledgeOn, the texts of Complete and
ZeroKnowledge, each with one condition more, that the request can be served (Definition 3.11); with the
condition that the output is the model’s, these are the served requests. The last two are this library’s
own, and so are the system, the bounds and the conditions on the files in all six. The library also has
the final statement, one Lean structure with six fields (lean/AUDIT.md), all proved by one Lean theorem;
Appendix F names it under Theorem 6.29, Proposition 6.33, and Corollary 6.38. completeOnCanServe and
zeroKnowledgeOnCanServe are no fields of it.
AI systems made every test, run, comparison, calculation, reading and review that this paper reports:
where it says “by a reading”, “by hand” or “as reported”, the reader, calculator or reporter is an AI system.
Such checking as there was, of the match between the English statements and the Lean ones and of the written
proofs, tests and judgements, was made by AI readers of this project; no written proof has been read whole
by one reader, and, as far as we know, no person has checked any of these (Limitation G.7, No independent
review).
Acknowledgments: We thank Alessandro Chiesa, Giacomo Fenzi, Guy Weissenberg, Miranda Christ, Rahul
Dalal, and Ben Mayer for their input and collaboration on FlashProve. We also thank James Petrie for VerInf
[VerInf].

