A.2

From the argument to three IOPs

Round-by-round soundness was introduced in [CCHLRR18, Definition 5.3]; [Hol19, Definition 2.5] restates it with a
doomed set of partial transcripts. Related work on doomed sets: [BGKTTZ23, BGTZ23]. Round-by-round knowledge
soundness is due to [CMS19, Definition 8.5], as round-by-round knowledge, in a form that differs from ours in detail;
[CMS19, Definition 8.4] states round-by-round soundness for IOPs. Compare [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026,
Definitions 3.6 and 3.7]; some of the Lean files that Definition A.1 and Definition A.2 follow are among those marked
as written from that work (Table 13). That work has the notion from [BCFW25], extended to relaxed relations.
Definition A.1 (Round-by-round soundness). Let L be a set of pairs (in, ext) of an IOP instance and external
oracle strings (Definition 2.3). An IOP is round-by-round sound for L with round errors (εm )m if there is a function
State from such pairs with a partial transcript to {0, 1} such that:
1. for (in, ext) ∈
/ L, the empty transcript has state 0;
2. no prover message changes the state from 0 to 1;
3. the challenge of round m does so with probability at most εm ;
4. the verifier rejects every complete transcript of state 0.
In a batch IOP each IOP instance has the state of its local transcript, and in a shared round εm bounds the probability
that the state of some instance of the batch changes from 0 to 1.
Definition A.2 (Early part, knowledge soundness). Let Q be a batch IOP and early a map on IOP instances,
the early part. Q is round-by-round sound for L with early part early and round errors (εm )m if there is a function
State that meets the conditions on prover messages and challenges (Definition A.1) and, for every (in, ext) ∈
/ L:
1. the empty transcript has state 0 at (early(in), ext);

56

2. the verifier rejects in on complete transcripts of state 0 at (early(in), ext).
Let Lwit be a set of such pairs with a witness, wit a witness map, and Q′ be Q with a verifier that also rejects each
complete transcript trs with (in, ext, wit(in, ext, trs)) ∈ Lwit . Q is round-by-round knowledge sound for Lwit under wit if
Q′ is sound in this sense for L = ∅.
Definition A.2 is used with the empty witness alone (Definition A.6). There, round-by-round knowledge soundness
is round-by-round soundness with an early part.

Terms of Construction D.11. The separate oracles are Merkle oracles for leaves and for inner nodes, of trees

over oracle strings and of the batch tree, and, for each of the three IOPs, a Fiat–Shamir oracle fs and an oracle g. An
answer of fs gives a round’s challenge and a chaining value, which the query of the next round holds. The first query
of a compiled verifier holds a list cms of digests. The early part of an encoded request ξ is ξ with y + replaced by 0.
Seed(rt) is the OOD seed that two queries to fs derive from a root. The oracles ann and spare occur in the analysis
alone (Definition A.3). The function H ′ enters the proof as one fixed function (Assumption 4.11; Remark 4.12).
Definition A.3 (The argument over separate oracles). Fix Z and a value pv of the public readings (dgZ , par) of
Construction 4.15. The argument Π◦Z,pv queries the separate oracles of Construction D.11, which are independent and
uniform. Further oracles exist, which its verifier never queries; among them are ann, with a single possible answer, and
a spare oracle on bit strings.
• A matrix A in range takes the place of the index, an encoded request ξ that of an instance; the relation is ReqZ .
• A commitment is a key k = (rt0 , σ0 ).
• A proof is π ◦ = (j, rtj , σj , πkey , πlink , πreq ).
• The verifier V ◦ performs steps 6 to 9 of VZ at the value pv.
• The extractor is Ext◦ (k, tr) = Dec(Wd(tr, rt0 ), σ0 ), with Wd as in Definition A.4.
The number qV bounds the queries of V ◦ to the separate oracles, on every input.
Definition A.4 (Word under a root, rooted query, seed-first trace). Let tr be a trace over the separate oracles.
1. The word under a digest rt, Wd(tr, rt), has at each position the symbol of the leaf query that tr determines under
rt, else a fixed symbol.
2. rt is announced in tr if tr contains the query ann(rt).
3. A start query is the first query to fs of step 1 of Construction D.11. A query to fs is rooted in tr if it is a start
query, or its chaining fields are chaining values of rooted queries of the preceding round; queries of Seed are not
rooted. A rooted query determines a partial transcript: challenges from answers, plain parts from queries, oracle
parts as the words under the queried roots.
4. tr is seed-first if each start query is preceded by the queries of Seed for the first two digests of its cms. Its
seed-first completion tr inserts these queries, at most four.
Definition A.5 (The event Bad). Fix state functions of the three IOPs (Definition A.1 and Definition A.2). A trace
lies in Bad if an entry that is not contained in the prefix pre before it causes one of four events.
1. Merkle event. The answer of a Merkle oracle is a digest that occurs in pre.
2. Chaining event. The chaining value of an answer of fs, or an answer of g, occurs in pre.
3. State event. A rooted query to fs has an answer that changes the state of its partial transcript from 0 to 1; in a
shared round, that of some encoded request under the queried root of the batch tree. External oracle strings are
the words in pre under the first digests of cms; OOD seeds are read off pre, OOD answers off cms.
4. Seed event. The seed of the second query of Seed(rt) is a collision of Wd(pre, rt).
By definition, Bad is closed under extension of traces.
Definition A.6 (Conditions on soundness). Let εrd ≥ 0. The sets are those of Construction 4.13.
(Sound-key). IOPkey
is round-by-round sound for its set, with round errors at most εrd (Definition A.1).
Z
(Sound-link proof). The same holds of IOPlink
Z .
(Sound-request). IOPreq
Z , as a batch IOP for at most R encoded requests, is round-by-round knowledge sound for
its set, with the empty witness, with the early part of ξ, and with round errors at most εrd (Definition A.2).
In the first two, a shared round’s error is counted once for each leaf of the tree of the call’s requests.

57

Definition A.7 (Conditions of the compiler). The fifteen conditions of the compiler, at εrd ≥ 0 and a value pv of
the public readings, are the three of Definition A.6 and twelve side conditions:
(No-decoding-at-a-collision). Coll(T∗ , ς) implies Dec(T∗ , (ς, a)) = ⊥.
(Local), for each IOP. The verifier reads oracle strings only at declared positions.
(Coherent), for each IOP. A plain part is the pair of its two components’ plain parts; each oracle position lies in one
component.
(Rare-collisions). For every word, the OOD seed read off uniform hash blocks is a collision with probability at most
εrd .
(Layout). Queries determine rounds and openings; uniform answers give uniform challenges.
(Answers-code), two conditions. The code of OOD answers is injective and of one length.
(Hashed-key). The implication of Lemma A.14, for Π◦Z,pv .
The conditions on soundness are the subject of Appendix A.3. (Layout) is stated informally: it summarizes the
laws of four Lean structures, which this paper does not print. (Hashed-key) holds by Lemma A.14.
Lemma A.8 (Bad is rare). [machine-checked] Assume the conditions of Definition A.7 other than (Hashed-key),
and form Bad (Definition A.5) with the state functions that (Sound-key), (Sound-link proof) and (Sound-request) of
Definition A.6 provide. Then the trace tr of every q-query algorithm over the separate oracles satisfies
Pr tr ∈ Bad





≤ q εrd +

2 (q + 1)2
.
2λ

Proof. We bound the probability that the answer to a new query causes an event, given pre, and take a union bound.
Step 1: Merkle and chaining events. The answer, or its chaining value, is uniform in {0, 1}λ and independent of
pre, in which each entry holds at most four values that count as occurring. The i-th query causes such an event with
probability at most 4i/2λ ; the sum over i < q is at most 2q 2 /2λ .
Step 2: state events. If a query to the oracle fs of one IOP is rooted, pre determines its partial transcript, the IOP
instance and the external oracle strings; among several preceding queries with one chaining value the first is taken. By
(Layout) the challenge is uniform, and by the condition on that IOP it changes a state from 0 to 1 with probability
at most εrd . In a shared round pre determines at most one encoded request for each leaf of the batch tree, and the
round’s error bounds the event for all of them jointly.
Step 3: seed events. A second query of Seed causes no state event. The word Wd(pre, rt) is determined before its
answer, and (Rare-collisions) applies.
Lemma A.9 (Outside Bad). [machine-checked] Assume the conditions of Definition A.7 other than (Hashed-key).
Let tr ∈
/ Bad.
1. Stable words. If rt is announced in a prefix tr0 of tr, then Wd(tr0 , rt) = Wd(tr, rt).
2. Seeds. If tr contains the queries of Seed(rt), with value ς, then Coll(Wd(tr, rt), ς) does not hold.
3. Accepting runs. Suppose that tr is seed-first and has equal answers at equal queries, and that it contains
an accepting run of the compiled verifier of one of the three IOPs and, for each root of an external oracle
string, the queries of Seed, with the OOD seed of the IOP instance as value. Then the relation of that IOP
(Construction 4.13) holds of the IOP instance and the words under these roots in tr.
Proof. Part 1. Without a Merkle event, no query after tr0 has as its answer a digest that occurs in tr0 ; the tree under
rt can neither change nor grow.
Part 2. Without a chaining event, the second query of Seed(rt) follows the first, in which rt occurs; from then on
the word under rt is stable. A collision at ς would be a seed event.
Part 3. Without chaining events, the queries to fs of the accepting run occur in tr in the order of the rounds, and
each is rooted. Because tr is seed-first, the OOD seeds are read off the prefix before the start query; by (Answers-code)
the OOD answers are read off their injective code. The partial transcripts therefore belong to one IOP instance and to
the stable words under the roots.
Suppose for contradiction that the relation fails. The state is 0 at the start and stays 0, since there is no state
event, and the IOP’s verifier rejects the complete transcript. By (Local), (Coherent) and the verified openings, the
compiled verifier decides as the IOP’s verifier does. For IOPreq
Z the start query holds only the early part of ξ; this is
why (Sound-request) is stated with that part.

