Proof. Join the committer, one query ann(rt0 ), the prover and V ◦ into one algorithm, with at most q + 1 + qV queries.
Modify it so that it makes the queries of the seed-first completion. This changes no output and yields the trace tr, with
at most four further queries for every query: at most Q in all. If V ◦ accepts while Ext◦ (k, trC ) is ⊥ or is not related to
ξ, then tr ∈ Bad by Lemma A.10. Lemma A.8, applied to the modified algorithm, bounds this probability.
Lemma A.12 (One hash function). [machine-checked] Let a bijection between bit strings and triples (oracle, query,
number of an answer block) be given, under which an answer of a Merkle oracle over oracle strings is one block. If an
argument over the separate oracles is index-binding knowledge sound with the extractor Ext◦ , then so is the argument
that queries H through the bijection, with the same error and the extractor that translates the trace and applies Ext◦ .
Proof. A uniform H induces independent uniform oracles, and a query to H is one block of one query to a separate
oracle. The extractor reads only entries at oracles with one block.
Lemma A.13 (Public readings). [machine-checked] Let Pub be a deterministic oracle algorithm that repeats no
query, and let S contain every string that Pub queries under some hash function. Let Pub and S be fixed before H is
sampled. Suppose that, for every pv in the codomain of Pub:
1. the conditions of Definition A.7 hold at pv;
2. (Spare) under the bijection at pv, every string of S codes a query to the spare oracle;
3. (Same-word) the extractor of Lemma A.12 at pv depends on pv only through dgZ .
Consider the argument over H with commitments (dg, k) whose verifier computes pv = PubH , rejects unless dg is the
digest in pv, and then runs V ◦ at pv through the bijection at pv. It is index-binding knowledge sound for ReqZ with
error εsep . Its extractor, at (dg, k), is that of Lemma A.12 at any pv with the digest dg, and ⊥ if there is none.
Proof. By induction on the queries of Pub. Its first query str ∈ S is fixed before H is sampled, and H can be sampled
by first sampling val = H(str). Fix val, and give (str, val) to the committer and the prover as a constant. By (Spare),
V ◦ does not query str, and deleting the entries at str from a trace changes no word under a root. When no query is
left, pv is a constant. The hypotheses hold at every pv, hence at this one, and Lemma A.11 and Lemma A.12 apply
with the error εsep .
This step requires that Z is fixed before H is sampled: the induction resamples H at strings that no adversary
chooses.
Lemma A.14 (The commitment as the hash of the key). [machine-checked] Let Π be an argument over H whose
commitments are keys and whose verifier is deterministic. Let Π be index-binding knowledge sound with extractor
Ext and error ε′ , and let code be an injective partial map from keys to bit strings. Consider the argument in which a
commitment is c = H(code(k)), a proof contains k, and the verifier rejects unless H(code(k)) = c and then decides as
the verifier of Π does. It is index-binding knowledge sound with error
