Proof. Let k∗ be the first key whose code the committer’s trace maps to c, and let the verifier accept a proof with the
key k. If k = k∗ , the outcome is that of the experiment for Π with the committer that outputs k∗ ; this gives ε′ (q).
Otherwise consider, in this order, the committer’s queries, one query at c read as a bit string, the prover’s queries and
the verifier’s query at code(k). The query at c is made in the analysis alone: it makes c a string that has occurred, for
the case that the trace holds no k∗ . Since code is injective, at some new entry of this list the answer equals a preceding
answer or, read asPa bit string, a preceding query or the entry’s own query. After i entries this has probability at most
(2i + 1)/2λ , and
