Lemma A.15 (The compilation lemma). [machine-checked] Let Z be fixed before H is sampled. Assume the
hypotheses of Lemma A.13, with Pub the public readings of VZ , and let code be injective on pairs (dg, k). Let the public
readings and the parser together make at most qpre queries. Let Rel be an indexed relation such that, for all A, x, y
and fill,
(A, build(x, y, fill)) ∈ ReqZ =⇒ wt(A) is defined and (wt(A), x, y) ∈ Rel.
Then every argument whose verifier accepts only where the public readings, the parser and the compiled steps of VZ
accept is index-binding knowledge sound for Rel, with error
εsep (q + qpre ) +

(q + qpre + 2)2
.
2λ

Its extractor is that of Lemma A.14, formed from that of Lemma A.13, followed by wt.
Proof. Lemma A.14 replaces the commitment (dg, k) of Lemma A.13 by c. An instance (x, y), with fill from the proof,
is mapped to ξ = build(x, y, fill); by the hypothesis, an instance outside Rel at wt(A) gives an encoded request outside
ReqZ at A. The queries of the public readings and the parser are charged to the prover: q becomes q + qpre .
Remark A.16 (Which form of the lemma). Two forms of the compilation lemma are machine-checked. One takes the
public readings as constants and has sixteen hypotheses: the fifteen of Definition A.7 and the implication for greedy
outputs. It does not apply to VZ , and the proof of Theorem 5.1 does not go through it: the strings that VZ hashes
hold digests that it obtains from H. The other is Lemma A.15. It requires the fifteen at every value of the public
readings, the implication for Rel in the place of the sixteenth, and further conditions; for FlashProveZ each of these is a
theorem, so that Theorem 5.1 has none of them as a hypothesis. Which form Lean’s proof uses is known by a reading
of the Lean code and by a program that follows the theorem’s dependencies; no theorem states it. Limits of both
forms: lean/LIMITS.md.
Lemma A.17 (The side conditions hold). [machine-checked] The tag is for parts 2 and 3 alone: no Lean statement
has the form of part 1 (the outline below). Let Z be a configuration and εrd = 2−128 .
1. If (Sound-key), (Sound-link proof) and (Sound-request) hold for FlashProveZ , then all fifteen conditions of
Definition A.7 hold at every value pv of the public readings.
2. The public readings of VZ are deterministic, repeat no query and make at most eight queries. They satisfy (Spare)
and (Same-word) of Lemma A.13.
3. The map code is injective on pairs (dg, k).
Outline. No single Lean statement has the form of part 1: besides the three conditions, the Lean lemma behind it
takes nine numeric premises, the numeric tests of (Layout) below. Further Lean lemmas prove these at every Z; they
are joined with it only inside the proof of another Lean statement.
(No-decoding-at-a-collision) is step 1 of Definition 4.5. (Rare-collisions) is Lemma A.25: for every configuration,
Lean’s construction forms committed tables at the sizes of Z0 . (Hashed-key) is Lemma A.14. (Local), (Coherent),
(Layout), (Answers-code) and parts 2 and 3 are properties of the verifiers and codings of Construction D.9, Construction D.11, and Construction 4.15, which Lean proves by unfolding them; (Layout) rests also on numeric tests, which
Lean proves at every Z. Only four of the fifteen depend on pv. Within (Layout), the coding of a start query of IOPreq
Z
is injective by the encoded request’s window and token positions alone, whatever the values of H ′ . (Spare) is domain
separation between the strings of the public readings and the coded queries.

A.3

Round-by-round soundness of the three IOPs

For sub-protocols, Lean states round-by-round soundness without a state function. The proofs of Lemma A.28 and
Lemma A.30 are outlines: for the arithmetization and for two of the three IOPs the printed text is a guide to Lean’s
proof of Proposition 5.14, not an argument.

60

Definition A.18 (Doomed, reduction, leads). Consider k rounds, a set B of complete transcripts, and counts
cnt = (cnt1 , . . . , cntk ) ∈ Nk . The prover is doomed towards B with these counts if
• k = 0 and the empty transcript lies in B; or
• k > 0 and, for every first message msg, at most cnt1 challenges ch1 are bad: after (msg, ch1 ) the prover is not
doomed, with counts (cnt2 , . . . , cntk ), towards {trs : (msg, ch1 , trs) ∈ B}.
The error of round m is cntm divided by the number of its challenges.
A reduction consists of rounds and a partial map out from an input statement and a complete transcript to an
output statement. It leads, with counts cnt, from a condition C on inputs to a relation R between inputs and outputs
if, from every input stm with C(stm), the prover is doomed, with these counts, towards the set of all trs such that
out(stm, trs) is undefined or R(stm, out(stm, trs)) holds.
Lemma A.19 (State functions; composition). [machine-checked]
1. If, outside L, the prover of an IOP is doomed towards rejection with counts cnt, then the IOP is round-by-round
sound for L (Definition A.1) with the errors of these counts.
2. Let Red1 lead from C1 to R1 with counts cnt, and Red2 from C2 to R2 with counts cnt′ . If C1 (stm) and
R1 (stm, stm′ ) imply C2 (stm′ ), then Red1 followed by Red2 leads from C1 to the composition of R1 and R2 , with
counts (cnt, cnt′ ); likewise for a finite list.
Proof. For part 1, let the state of a partial transcript be 0 if and only if the prover is doomed after it. Part 2 is by
induction on the rounds of Red1 .
Lemma A.20 (Proof of work; challenges from hash blocks). [machine-checked]
1. Let Ch be a finite set and Ch′ ⊆ Ch. The number of pairs (a, z) ∈ Ch × {0, 1}κ with z = 0 and a ∈ Ch′ is |Ch′ |.
2. Let a sub-protocol be stated with challenges in sets Chm , and let the prover be doomed towards B with at most a
share shrm of Chm bad in round m. In FlashProveZ the challenge of round m is a tuple of blkm hash blocks, from
which an element of Chm and κm bits of proof of work are computed. Over these rounds the prover is doomed
towards the transcripts whose image lies in B or in which a proof-of-work test fails, with round errors at most
1 + 2−128

blkm

shrm / 2κm .

Proof. For part 1, a 7→ (a, 0) is a bijection onto the pairs in question. For part 2, at most fibm tuples of hash blocks
are mapped to one element of Chm , where fibm |Chm | is at most (1 + 2−128 )blkm times the number of tuples. By part 1,
proof of work leaves the count unchanged, while the number of challenges grows by the factor 2κm .
The conditions of Definition A.6 do not mention proof of work: they bound the bad challenges among all challenges
of a round. A further nonce is a further query, which counts in q.
In the next lemma the implications between claims run backwards: if the claim after a part holds, so does the
claim before it. Informally, a false claim therefore remains false, except at bad challenges, until the last part refutes it.
Lemma A.21 (From three parts to a batch IOP). [machine-checked] Let a batch IOP for at most R IOP instances
consist of reductions Red1 , Red2 , Red3 : its first, shared and last part. Let Cfirst , Cshared , Clast be conditions, Pfirst a
relation between inputs and outputs of Red1 , and Pshared , Plast properties of the outputs of Red1 , Red2 . Suppose:
1. Red1 leads from Cfirst to Cshared ∧ (Pshared ⇒ Pfirst );
2. Red2 leads from Cshared to Clast ∧ (Plast ⇒ Pshared ), with counts cntkey + R cntown , where the challenges counted
in cntkey depend only on the external oracle string and its OOD sample;
3. Red3 leads from Clast to Plast ;
4. every accepting transcript is a run of the three reductions on the early part of the IOP instance;
5. on an accepting transcript, Cfirst ⇒ Pfirst implies membership in the IOP’s set;
6. every round error is at most εrd .
Then the batch IOP is round-by-round knowledge sound in the form of (Sound-request) (Definition A.6).
Proof. By Lemma A.19, from every input with Cfirst the prover is doomed towards Pfirst : the last part gives Plast ,
hence Pshared , hence Pfirst . By hypotheses 4 and 5 it is doomed towards the event that the verifier rejects or the IOP
instance lies in the set. The three reductions run on the early part; hence the state function of Lemma A.19 is one at
the early part, as Definition A.2 requires. In a batch, the challenges counted in cntkey form one set for all encoded
requests, which share the copy and its OOD sample.

