Step 2: folds. Folding 2v rows with eq(γ, ·) is a sequence of v steps. Step k maps two half tables g0 , g1 to
(1 − γk ) g0 + γk g1 = g0 + γk (g1 − g0 ), the line of Step 1 for the tables g0 and g1 − g0 ; these agree with the code on a
set of columns if and only if g0 and g1 do. At most v ⌊J⌋ |E|v−1 points γ are exceptional in some step.
Step 3: other points. If Enc(u) is within e′ positions of the folded word, then T∗ agrees with a table of codewords
on the same columns, and u is the fold of that table. At most ls(|D|, dmin , e′ ) tables are within e′ columns of T∗
(Lemma A.23); since e′ ≤ e, each lies in Λ(T∗ ).
Step 4: the list. Since there is no collision, such a table with the OOD answers a is the decoded table, which
condition 3 excludes. For one with other answers, condition 2 is a nonzero multilinear equation in γ, with at most
v |E|v−1 solutions.
Steps 2 to 4 run as the second of the error bounds in the proof of [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Lemma 6.5],
and for each variable the bound has the form of that lemma’s second error. That work, in its Section 2.5, likens its
analysis to those of [ACFY24b] and [BCFW25] and takes mutual correlated agreement from prior work; its own care,
shared here, is to count the lists on the interleaved code.
Lemma A.25 (Collisions are rare). [machine-checked] Let the sizes of a committed table, the radius e and the
number s of OOD answers be those of Z0 . Then, for every word T∗ , the OOD seed that is read off uniform hash blocks
is a collision of T∗ (Definition 4.5) with probability at most 2−128 .
Proof. A collision requires two of the at most ls members of Λ(T∗ ) with different messages and equal answers. The
word T∗ is over F, and e is below the minimum distance. A codeword over E within e positions of a word over F agrees
with it at ℓ + t or more positions of D ⊆ F, and is therefore over F. The difference of two members thus has its pad
entries in F, where the map from three entries to a pad is injective. Such a nonzero
message has all OOD answers 0
