(Definition A.27). The count of each round m, with challenge set Hm , is ⌊|Hm |/2128 ⌋.
Outline. Lean fixes, for each point between two sub-protocols, the set of claims that are open there. It divides the
sub-protocols into thirteen kinds: Send; Code switch; Lookup; the own rounds of layers, of attention and of the head;
the round that sends y + ; the matrix of the Range test; Batching; Batching with Fold; the merging of the head’s claims
on the table; the Proximity test; and the reduction to one claim on the table.
For each kind it proves that a sub-protocol leads, from every state that the first part can reach and that meets
the condition before it, to the condition after it and to the implication that, if the claims open after it hold, so do
those open before it; in Lean an explicit map sends values that satisfy the former to values that satisfy the latter. By
Lemma A.19 these compose, since what is open after one sub-protocol is open before the next.
The counts have the forms of Table 10, at the sizes that Z fixes; in the round that sends y + no challenge is bad.
They are transferred to challenges from hash blocks by Lemma A.20 and compared with |Hm |/2128 , as natural numbers;
for the attention segments this comparison is itself a parameter check. Since a larger count gives a weaker statement,
every round is bounded by the common count. What the lemmas require of Z is tested by Adm and Chk.
