Proof. Let Dec(T∗j , σj ) ̸= ⊥. Then Cfirst holds, by (No-decoding-at-a-collision), and Pfirst provides cells. By step 4 for
IOPreq
Z in Construction D.9, the stored token is unchanged after each sub-protocol other than the one that receives it;
hence the head’s claims concern the token received. By step 3, that token is y + .
Remark A.32 (Stored token). The test of step 4 concerns the verifier’s own state, not a prover message, and the proof
above relies on it. That it changes no decision is known in part by a Lean theorem, by which the decision is the same
for the written IOP and a second text of it, and in part by a reading, that the second text is the IOP without the test;
at worst it costs completeness, not soundness.
Lemma A.33 (From the request relation to the policy). [machine-checked] Let Z be an admissible configuration.
Let A be in range, and let x, y, fill be lists of tokens such that ξ = build(x, y, fill) is defined and (A, ξ) ∈ ReqZ . Then
wt(A) is defined, and y meets polZ on x under the weights wt(A) (Definition 3.4), in the integer model at µZ .
Proof. The relation is stated modulo p, for cells below 218 in absolute value whose lookup keys are entries of public
tables. The model parameters µZ are valid, since Z is admissible, and A is in range. Under these bounds the two sides
of each identity are integers that differ by less than 261 − 1 < p in absolute value; a multiple of p of that size is 0, and
the identity holds over the integers. By induction on the groups of layers, the cells are the values of the integer model
on the window, at the weights wt(A) and for every fill, and the model’s range conditions hold. The head’s identities
and lookups then state the conditions of Definition 3.4. The bounds for the single identities are Lean’s and are not
printed.
Lemma A.34 (The extractor). [machine-checked] Let Z be an admissible configuration. Then ExtZ (Construction 4.15) is the extractor of Lemma A.15.
Proof. By unfolding the two definitions.

A.5

Adding up the error

Lemma A.35 (Numbers of queries). [machine-checked] Let Z be any configuration.
1. At every value of the public readings, V ◦ makes at most qV ≤ 220 queries to the separate oracles.
2. The public readings of VZ make at most 8 queries to H.
3. The parser, step 2 of VZ , makes at most 1 query to H (Construction D.11).
A query to a separate oracle costs one query to H for each block of its answer; hence qV does not bound the
queries of VZ to H (Remark B.13). That εrd denotes 2−128 is a definition; that the round errors and the probability of
a collision are at most this number is Proposition 5.14 and Lemma A.25.
