gapi ≤ 256 nct .

2. If y meets pol, then for every integer g ≤ 25600,
g · #{ i < nct : gapi ≥ g } ≤ 256 nct .
3. If nct ≥ 100 and all counted tokens but at most one are greedy tokens, then y meets pol.
Remark B.4 (Reading the theorem: particulars). To the items of Remark 5.3:
3. No form of the theorem with a witness is stated, and ExtZ reads nothing of the prover. It is straight-line, and
no running time is stated.
4. The weights that ExtZ reads off a commitment and the committer’s queries need not be the same at all
configurations.
5. Configurations with other policies are admissible.
Remark B.5 (Bits per query). The bound ε(q) of Theorem 5.1 is at most about 16 (q + 223 )/2128 : 124 bits per query. It
is never below about 2−101 , it is about 2−60 at q = 264 , and it says nothing from about q = 2124 on. A query returns
32 bytes. A call of BLAKE2b that returns 64 bytes, as each call for a Fiat–Shamir challenge does, an attempt at a
proof of work among them, counts as two queries, and every other call as one. So 124 bits per query are at least 123
bits per call; this conversion is a reading, not a theorem, and it counts an attempt at a proof of work as a whole call,
whereas in the released programs one attempt can cost less than one hash call, so that κ counted bits mean fewer than
2κ calls; by how much is not known.

66

Remark B.6 (Acceptance). What is known beyond Limitation 8.9 (Completeness on served requests only): the runs in
which the executable form accepted honest proofs are at Z0 and Z1 , at the format’s hash function (Section 7.3); no run
concerns another hash function, and, besides the reduction of Remark 6.49, the one theorem on acceptance is over a
random oracle and on the Lean prover’s proofs (Section 6.5). Beyond Proposition 5.6, the parameter checks have been
evaluated, and pass, at seven admissible configurations, over Lean files that differ from the released ones, by a report
alone (lean/GAPS.md, gap 52; lean/THEOREM.md); at all others nothing is known. VZ can reject on Z alone at further
places, hundreds by a rough count, not traced one by one; lemmas settle two of them at Z0 , and no statement says that
the list is complete. Where Z is admissible, Chk(Z) = 1 and the policy is exact match, as at Z0 , Section 6.5 gives more:
some request is served there, at constructed weights, and VZ rejects the Lean prover’s proof for it with probability at
most 2−40 over the random oracle and the honest coins. So no test whose outcome is a function of Z and H ′ alone
makes VZ reject every input, and a test that also reads H at strings formed from Z does so with probability at most
2−40 over H; at the format’s hash function nothing follows for such a test, and no lemma settles a test that only other
requests meet. This is an inference from these statements; no Lean theorem states it. At other Z the question is open.
Remark B.7 (Which compiler). Two compilers occur: the Lean library’s own (Construction D.11) and the generic
compilation of Definition 2.4. Steps 4 to 9 of VZ (Construction 4.15) are, by definition, the output of the first at a
hand-written input (the three IOPs, the assignment of their rounds to hash queries, and the codings) that depends on
Z and the digests of step 3. Lemma A.15 is machine-checked for this compiler, and Theorem 5.1 rests on it. That
the first is an instance of the second is not machine-checked; Theorem 5.1 does not need it. In the Lean term for
ΠZ the model, the committer and the prover are fields, the last two constants (Remark 4.16, Honest parties). A
further Lean function, compile (Appendix F at 6.15; its output is the argument of Definition 6.15), which the release’s
page lean/STATEMENTS.html shows, is a layer around the first: it returns VZ (by a proved equation); soundness,
completeness and zero knowledge are all stated of its output; the soundness theorem is proved not through it but
through the compilation of Construction D.11 (Lemma A.15). The library also has a second theorem with the statement
of Theorem 5.1 to the letter (sound_of_verify_at_through_the_IOP), which draws the three protocols’ soundness
from the one round-by-round statement of Remark 6.21, item 1, and hands it to the same compilation lemma: it is not
an independent proof.

B.1

The executable form

Construction B.8 (Algorithm of the executable form). Coding. A bit string is plain if its length is a multiple of
8 and its first eight bits do not spell the byte 4. For a plain bs, byt(bs) is the byte string with the bits of bs. Otherwise
byt(bs) consists of the bytes 4 and |bs| mod 8 and of bs, extended by zeros to whole bytes. Bytes are read from the
lowest bit, and a byte string of fewer than 32 bytes is extended by zeros.
Algorithm. On input Z, O, c, x, y and π = (fill, π1 , . . . , π5 ):
1. If Adm(Z) = 0, return 0.
2. Let o0 = O(byt(code(Z))), and let o1 , . . . , ok , k ≤ 7, be the values of O at the codings of the further public
strings of step 3 of Construction 4.15, formed with o0 as the digest of Z.
3. If some oi does not have 32 bytes, return ⊥. If Chk(Z) = 0, return 0.
4. Unpack (π1 , . . . , π5 ), with o0 and without a call to O, into k, j, rtj , σj and the data of three proof strings. If
this fails, return 0.
5. Evaluate steps 4 to 9 of Construction 4.15 as tests, with O in the place of the hash function: build(x, y, fill) is
defined; k has a code; the hash value of that code is c; each of πkey , πlink , πreq can be formed from its data; the
plain part of πreq holds j; ς0 and ςj are the seeds of rt0 and rtj ; the three compiled verifiers accept. Return 1 if
all hold, and 0 otherwise.
