claim followed by E O .
Remark B.10 (Claim from files). Z, c, x and y must come from the client. That claim reads the client’s files as the
format’s specification intends is not proved, and an error there would make VZ decide another claim than the client’s.
The proof needs no trust, nor does the program outside Lean that makes π from the release’s proof files: Theorem 5.1
holds for every proof string.
A run also trusts the part of the command that reads files and prints, and Lean’s evaluator, compiler and runtime.
That none of the library’s definitions beneath the executable form is replaced by other code when compiled rests on a
walk in Lean over what the command runs, repeated whenever the library is compiled, which lists every constant there
that carries replaced or external code (none is this library’s). The command is part of the release’s source tree, not a
stand-alone program. In Corollary B.11 the functions OH are defined with the axiom of choice, have no code and are
not O∗ .
Corollary B.11 (Soundness of the executable form). [machine-checked] Let Z be as in Theorem 5.1. For each
hash function H let OH be a function from byte strings to 32-byte strings that induces H (Definition 5.4); such a
family exists. Then the bound of Theorem 5.1 holds, with the same error ε(q), for the experiment of Definition 2.7 in
OH
which the decision on (c, x, y, π) is EZ
(c, x, y, π).
Proof. By Theorem 5.5 the two experiments have the same event at every H. A family exists because byt is injective:
let OH (byt(bs)) be H(bs), written as 32 bytes, and let OH take any 32 bytes elsewhere.
Remark B.12 (Malleable proofs). That an accepted proof cannot be altered into another accepted proof of the same
instance is proved nowhere, and altered proofs were accepted in runs (Section 7.3). A false instance gains nothing by
this: Theorem 5.1 bounds its acceptance whatever a proof holds.

B.2

Many proofs

Proof of Corollary 5.10. With the extractor of Theorem 5.1, for i < N let AP,i run the given prover and output entry
i of its list, or a fixed triple; it makes the same queries. At every H and all random choices, the event of the corollary
implies that of Definition 2.7 for some AP,i , which has probability at most ε(qC + qP ).
Remark B.13 (Lists sent at once). In Corollary 5.10 the prover sees no decision of the verifier and no honest proof
before it sends its list. For proofs that are sent one after another, each after the decision on the one before, no bound
is stated. For lists sent at once, the bound ε(q + N qH ), without the factor N , is machine-checked if every run of VZ
makes at most qH queries to H. Such a qH is not known (Open problem 8.15): the bound qV of Lemma A.35 counts
queries to the separate oracles, each of which costs one query to H for each digest of its answer.
Corollary B.14 (Evaluation binding). [machine-checked] Let Z and ε be as in Theorem 5.1, and let N , qC , qP , the
committer and the prover be as in Corollary 5.10. With probability at most N · ε(qC + qP ), VZ accepts two entries i, j
of the prover’s list with xi = xj , |yi | = |yj | and yi ̸= yj .
Proof. For fixed weights, prompt and length determine a greedy output. Of two accepted entries with equal prompts
and lengths and different outputs, at most one lies in Lang(M ∗ ), so the event of Corollary 5.10 occurs.
Remark B.15 (Evaluation binding). Corollary B.14 has the form of evaluation binding for functional commitments [BNO21], for the function that maps a prompt and a length to an output and for two proofs in one list. It is
neither extractability of the commitment nor the absence of two openings (Definition 2.10).

