Proof of Proposition 5.6. Admissibility and the policy of Z0 follow from tests on its byte strings that Lean’s kernel
evaluates. Chk(Z0 ) = 1 is unfolded into closed facts, many of them proved by evaluation in the kernel (Remark 5.7,
How the proposition is checked). Among admissible configurations Chk does not depend on the policy.
Remark B.17 (Checkers). Lean’s kernel accepts the proofs of the Lean theorems that the machine-checked statements
of Section 5 and this appendix render or join, in a build of the released Lean files with no error and no sorry; for
those named in lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md Lean prints no axiom beyond its three standard ones. No lemma about a
step of the protocol and no conjecture on codes is assumed, and the random oracle and H ′ belong to the statements.
An independent proof checker has accepted the soundness theorem on verify, sound_of_verify_at (the form of
Theorem 5.1 that leaves the extractor open: Section 5, head), with every declaration beneath it, and nothing that
does not lie beneath it, in a pass on that theorem’s export alone: the pass was made over a copy of the compiled
files of the released Lean files’ build. Its passes by name on sound, repliesFit and refusedHandsOutNoProof were
made over that build too; that the compiled files that these passes read were that build’s is known from a report
alone; Lean’s own files were not compared with a list of checksums. The checker’s other passes were made over Lean
files that differ from the released ones. It has accepted neither Proposition 5.6 (Remark 5.7, How the proposition is
checked) nor Theorem 5.17 nor Theorem 5.19. The Lean verifier’s reference run (the 109 calls of Appendix H.3) has
been made over the released Lean files; what these are, and what has run: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md. One whole run
of the library’s full check (lean/scripts/check.py) over all but one of the released Lean files has the last line “All
steps passed: tree, words, imports, build (kernel replay, audit). . . . ”. That line also says that no theorem got a verdict
on binders or on Classical.choice: every line of lean/scripts/top-theorems.txt says “general” (the whole line,
and what the check does not cover: lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md). It is one run, on one machine. In its kernel replay
Mathlib and the other packages are taken as they were compiled beforehand, and are not replayed (lean/GAPS.md,
81 (g)); which modules go by another call than leanchecker: Remark 5.7. One Lean file, with one definition and four
theorems, which nothing else imports, was added after the runs reported here and checked on its own. Three of the
check script’s pinned values were changed and three of its stored lists were extended for it; nothing else in the script or
the lists changed. The full check’s run builds the three libraries from nothing in one call of Lake; the other results told
here stand on a second build of the same files, from nothing, in several calls (lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md). The checker
judges a text that this project’s own exporter writes from the compiled library; the exporter is not in the repository, so
a reader cannot repeat the pass; and no run shows the checker rejecting a wrong proof. The pinned version of Lean,
4.29.1, is older than the published fixes (from version 4.32.1 on) of soundness bugs in Lean’s kernel and runtime and in
the big-number library (GMP) that its Linux builds link. No proof in this library is known to use such a fault; the
proofs have not been searched for one, and the check script’s word list and audit would not detect one. This paper
tells no check of the library with a later version of Lean (lean/LIMITS.md, L45).
Remark B.18 (Typed statement). Lemma 5.13 is the statement as typed in the Lean library. That it follows from
the two-word case of the published theorem [BCHKS25, Theorem 4.6] rests on a reading; no theorem says it, and the
lemma does not make that theorem, in its own form, formally verified. The Lean proof derives it from two theorems in
files adapted from ArkLib [ArkLib] and Mathlib [Mathlib].

