• Extractability of the commitment with error εop implies no two openings with error 2εop .
• If Rnd makes no query and outputs a function of one uniform number, and Commit makes at most q0 queries on
every input, it also implies correctness of extraction for the same extractor with error εop (q0 ).
No two openings does not exclude two openings to one index with different randomness.
Remark B.22 (Proof outlines for the commitment). The outlines follow the Lean proofs.
• Part 2 of Theorem 5.17 with γ (Proposition 5.18). Where Chk(Z) = 1, at every hash function the explicit
committer outputs the commitment of a second committer, which is stated over the separate oracles of
Construction D.11. This rests on five equations on single queries and two on the encoder and the OOD answers;
the equation on leaves needs that both committers’ leaf salts have one length, which Chk(Z) = 1 gives. For the
second committer, take the trace of AC and then the traces of the opener and of the test that (M, r) opens c.
With probability at most γ(qC + qO ), a hash event occurs on these traces, or the word under rt0 changes when
the other two traces are added. Otherwise c fixes the key, and ExtZ reads off the trace of AC the table that the
test forms. The decoder returns the digit matrix of every honestly formed table (Definition 4.5).
• Theorem 5.17. Part 2 follows because γ ≤ β wherever β < 1. Parts 1 and 3 follow from part 2 by Remark B.21,
with q0 = 235 + 2.
• Theorem 5.19. Where Chk(Z) = 0, VZ rejects every input. Where Chk(Z) = 1, part 1 is the implication of
Remark B.20, applied to Theorem 5.11 with the extractor ExtZ . Its other two inputs are part 1 of Theorem 5.17
and q0 = 235 + 2. Part 2 follows by Proposition 3.5.
Remark B.23 (Constants of the errors).
• The bound 235 + 2 on the explicit committer’s queries is machine-checked, on every input. It is the argument of
β in part 1 of Theorem 5.17 and a summand of q in Theorem 5.19: the honest committer’s queries are counted
among the adversary’s.
• In β, the summand 223 is the allowance for the verifier’s queries in Theorem 5.1, although no verifier runs here.
• With γ(235 + 2) (Proposition 5.18) in the place of β(235 + 2), part 1 of Theorem 5.19 is likewise a single Lean
theorem, joined with the theorem on admissibility as in Remark 5.20.
Proposition B.24 (Two degenerate committers). [machine-checked] Let Z and Γ be as in Theorem 5.19.
1. If the explicit committer replaces every digit matrix by the all-zero matrix, none of the three properties of
Theorem 5.17 holds, for ExtZ and the errors stated there.
2. The constant committer always outputs 0λ , without randomness or queries. With it in the place of the explicit
committer, and any predicate on (M, x, y) in the place of “y meets polZ on x under M ”, part 1 of Theorem 5.19
holds with q = qP and without the term β(235 + 2).
Remark B.25 (What the two committers show). All of the following is machine-checked.
• Part 2 of Proposition B.24: soundness after an honest commitment (Definition B.19) does not separate the
explicit committer from the constant one. For the predicate that never holds, it states that VZ accepts after the
constant commitment with probability at most the error of Theorem 5.1.
• Part 1 holds in a stronger form: with the all-zero matrix, correctness of extraction fails for every extractor at
every error below 1/2, and no two openings for every error function whose value at 235 + 2 is below 1.
• With completeness, the property excludes that committer. If ΠΓ
Z with the all-zero matrix and a q-query prover
is complete on the instances that can be served (Definition 2.12 and Definition 3.11) with error α, and sound
