70

C.1

The definition of index privacy

Remark C.1 (Environment and simulator). Definition 2.14 is adapted from [CFWpc]. Its reach and its limits are these.
• The environment sees the whole view of V ∗ and may decline, but it is a function of that view and has no coins
of its own: no machine-checked statement extends a bound to environments with coins.
• Batches, with their revealed and hidden instances, are this paper’s addition to the definition. The addition has
had no independent check.
The simulator’s running time is not bounded.

C.2

Committed tables

Definition C.2 (Disclosed view, honest-verifier simulator). Let P be a prover for an IOP (Definition 2.3) in
which each oracle part is marked masked or not. Let ch denote the challenges of a run. The abort set of P at an input
is a set of values of ch, the union of an input-independent public part and a private part.
The disclosed view at ch consists of the plain parts of P , its oracle parts that are not masked, and the symbols
that the verifier reads in the masked ones and in the external oracle strings. The output of P at ch is ⊥ if ch lies in
the abort set, else the disclosed view.
An honest-verifier simulator is a randomized algorithm given the IOP instance, ch, and the symbols that the
verifier reads in the external oracle strings. It is exact at an input and ch if its output is distributed as that of P .
The core of this notion, a view of the verifier and a simulator with limited access to the oracle strings, goes back
to [BCFGRS17], as [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Definition 2.1] attributes it; here a prover may abort, and
a simulator is exact at single values of ch.
Definition C.3 (Table system). A table system consists of a set of indices; an encoder from an index and randomness,
uniform in a finite set, to a table, a string of columns; a finite set of OOD seeds with a map from index, randomness
and seed to OOD answers; and three IOPs (Definition 2.3). Their external oracle strings are one table, two tables,
and one table; their IOP instances are the table’s OOD sample, both samples, and, in a batch IOP, pairs of a request
and the sample. FlashProveZ (Construction 4.13) is a table system whose indices are the matrices over F shaped as in
Definition 4.1, encoded as in Definition 4.2 and Definition 4.3.
Definition C.4 (Four conditions on the tables). Let a table system (Definition C.3), a set U of OOD seeds and
εU ∈ [0, ∞] be given.
(Bounded reads) The first verifier reads at most νoff columns of its table, the second at most νfirst of its first table
and νcopy of its second, the third at most νon for a batch. For every seed in U and every set Q of at most t
positions, the OOD answers with the columns at Q have the same distribution for all indices.
(First-table budget) νoff + Kνfirst ≤ t.
(Copy budget) νcopy + Nc νon ≤ t.
(Good seeds) A seed derived from uniform digests lies outside U with probability at most εU .
Definition C.5 (Table experiment). Let a table system (Definition C.3), provers P for its IOPs and an index A be
given. In TabP (A, A) an unbounded randomized algorithm A names an OOD seed ς0 ; then T0 is sampled, and A
receives its OOD answers. Next A adaptively asks for:
• copy: A names ςj ; then Tj is sampled with fresh randomness, and A receives its answers;
• column: A receives Tj [ϕ] for j and ϕ of its choice;
• run: the first IOP on Tj , the second on (T0 , Tj ) with j ≥ 1, or the third on Tj and a batch of its choice is run
with the honest verifier; A receives ch and the output of P∗ (Definition C.2), in the third IOP for the revealed
requests.
The output is that of A. TabZ is the case of FlashProveZ with constant-message provers.
Definition C.6 (Within the opening budget). Let νoff , νfirst , νcopy , νon be as in (Bounded reads) of Definition C.4;
for FlashProveZ they are 62, 62, 62, 64. An algorithm A in the table experiment (Definition C.5) is within the opening
budget t if there are natural numbers K and Njkey , Njlink , Njreq , Njcol , for 0 ≤ j ≤ K, such that, for every j,
νoff Njkey + [ j = 0 ] νfirst

