Lemma C.8 (Columns are uniform). [machine-checked] Let fr ∈ Fℓ for r < 2v be arbitrary messages, and let
Q ⊆ D with |Q| ≤ t. Over independent uniform τr ∈ Ft , the values Enc(fr ; τr )(ϕ), for r < 2v and ϕ ∈ Q, are
independent and uniform in F.
Proof. By Definition 4.2, Enc(f ; τ )(ϕ) = τ (ϕ) + ϕt f (ϕ), where τ (X) = j<t τj X j . The linear map τ 7→ (τ (ϕ))ϕ∈Q
from Ft to FQ is given by a Vandermonde matrix on |Q| ≤ t distinct points, so it is surjective. Hence (τ (ϕ))ϕ∈Q is
uniform, also after the fixed vector (ϕt f (ϕ))ϕ∈Q is added. Rows have independent coefficients.

P

Lemma C.9 (Pads mask the answers). [machine-checked] Let s = 1. For every OOD seed (z0 , β0 ) and all plain
entries of a row, the map from the row’s three pads to its OOD answer (Definition 4.3) is a bijection from F3 onto E.
Hence, over uniform pads, the OOD answers of a table are independent and uniform in E, for every matrix.
Proof. By Definition 4.3, ar,0 is the sum of β0 padr,0 and a term that depends on the plain entries and on z0 alone.
The three pads are the coordinates of padr,0 in the basis 1, ϑ, ϑ2 of E over F, and β0 ̸= 0. Hence pads map to ar,0
bijectively. Rows have independent pads.
Masking an OOD answer (Definition 4.3) with fresh random entries of the message follows the private zero-evaders
of [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Section 9.2]: Lemma C.9 is in substance the privacy part of its Lemma 9.3,
with its Definition 9.2, at one answer in E with three pads in F.
Lemma C.10 (One table). [machine-checked] Let t = 44,040,191 and s = 1, at the table sizes of the default
configuration. For every OOD seed and every Q ⊆ D with |Q| ≤ t, the distribution of the pair of the OOD answers and
the columns (T[ϕ])ϕ∈Q of a committed table T for A is the same for all matrices A over F.
Proof. Fix the pads, and with them all messages fr and all answers. By Lemma C.8 the columns at Q are uniform,
whatever the pads are. Hence the columns are independent of the pads, and so of the answers. By Lemma C.9 the
answers are uniform; so is the pair, for every A.
