Proof of Theorem 6.3. By Lemma C.11, (Bounded reads) holds at all seeds. The simulator that outputs the fixed
messages and the symbols that it is given is exact at every ch. Let Y hold all pairs and A0 = A. By Lemma C.12,
TabZ (A, A) and TabZ (A′ , A) both have the distribution of TabSim (A0 , A).
Proof of Proposition 6.5. Suppose that the distributions agree for some Q. The value that A′ produces with all pads
and random coefficients 0 then has positive probability for A = 0, at some pads and (τr )r . At ς ◦ the entry f0 [0] has
coefficient eq((1, . . . , 1), 0) = 0, and the other plain entries of f0 vanish for both matrices. Hence both answers a0,0 are
images of the pads alone, and by Lemma C.9 the pads of f0 are 0 for A too. The codeword of f0 is then the evaluation
of X t for A′ and of τ0 (X) for A. They agree on Q, so the nonzero polynomial X t − τ0 (X) of degree t has t + 1 roots,
a contradiction.
Remark C.15 (What tightness shows). Proposition 6.5 negates Lemma C.10 at t + 1 columns. It follows, though not
machine-checked, that the conclusion of Theorem 6.3 fails for the algorithm that names ς ◦ , asks for the columns at Q
and outputs what it receives. It bounds no distance and concerns one seed, at which eq((1, . . . , 1), j) = 0 for every
j < ℓ0 . It is not shown that wt(A) ̸= wt(A′ ).
