C.3

After compilation

Theorem C.25 reduces index privacy of a whole-key argument (Definition C.20) to twenty conditions: four on the tables
(Definition C.4), eight on nonces and positions (Definition C.22), three on formats (Definition C.23) and five on the
provers (Definition C.21). Proposition C.26 gives the first fifteen for FlashProveZ at admissible Z and bounded K and
Nc , Lemma C.28 the five for constant-message provers, and Lemma C.27 bounds the error where ζ∗sim = ζ∗ab = 0. The
idea of this step, from honest-verifier zero knowledge of a public-coin IOP to zero knowledge of the argument compiled
from it, is public: compare [CF24, Lemma 2.20 (informal) and Lemma 9.9] and [CO25, Section 2.6, Corollary 1
(informal), with Theorem 7.1 for interactive proofs].
Definition C.18 (Compiled run of a prover). Let P∗ be a prover for an IOP of a table system, with a search limit
for each round with proof of work. A compiled run of P∗ computes a proof string, over the oracles of Construction D.11.
• The chain salt is sampled; challenges are derived as the compiled verifier recomputes them.
• In a round with proof of work the nonces 0, 1, 2, . . . up to the search limit are tried, one query each, until the
challenge passes.
• Oracle parts are committed with the leaf salts of Definition C.19.
The result is ⊥ if a search exhausts its limit or the challenges lie in the abort set.
Definition C.19 (Coins of a compiled run). Let ℓs ≥ 0. In a compiled run of P∗ (Definition C.18):
• a leaf salt over an oracle part is a public prefix, a function of a value of the coins of P∗ , the round, the component
and the chain salt, followed by ℓs uniform bits;
• a leaf without a position has a fixed public body;
• the leaf number in the batch tree is fixed and public, and the path’s digests are uniform.
A leaf salt of a committed table is a fixed public prefix followed by ℓs uniform bits.
Definition C.20 (Whole-key argument). Let Z be a configuration, and fix values (dg, par) in place of the two
public readings of VZ (Construction 4.15). Let P = (Pkey , Plink , Preq ) be provers for the three IOPs of FlashProveZ ,
with search limits and ℓs (Definition C.18 and Definition C.19), and K ≥ 1. The whole-key argument Πwk
Z [P] has the
matrices over F in the place of indices (Definition C.3), encoded requests ξ in the place of instances, and the separate
oracles of Construction D.11, independent and random.
• The committer samples T0 , . . . , TK with independent randomness, hashes each into a salted Merkle tree with
root rtj , computes ςj = Seed(rtj ) and
 σj , and makes one compiled run of Pkey and K of Plink . The commitment
(j)
is rt0 , σ0 , πkey , (rtj , σj , πlink )1≤j≤K .
• The stateless prover samples j uniformly, makes a compiled run of Preq on Tj and one encoded request, and
outputs (j, πreq ). On larger batches it outputs ⊥.
• The verifier consists of steps 6 to 9 of VZ at (dg, par).
Definition C.21 (Five conditions on the provers). For P as in Definition C.20, let Sim∗ be honest-verifier
simulators (Definition C.2), J a set of matrices, Y a set of pairs (A, ξ), and ζ∗sim , ζ∗ab ∈ [0, ∞]. Probabilities are over
uniform ch with proof-of-work bits 0. The conditions are required for all table randomness, OOD seeds and copy
numbers.
(Encoded tables) For all matrices: the external oracle strings of P∗ are the committed tables of its input.
(Exact simulation) For A ∈ J , (A, ξ) ∈ Y: except with probability ζ∗sim , ch lies in the private part of the abort set
or Sim∗ is exact.
(Rare private aborts) For the same A, ξ: ch lies in the private part with probability at most ζ∗ab .
(Readable prefixes) For A ∈ J and all ξ: unless P∗ aborts, its plain parts determine the value of its coins in the
public prefixes (Definition C.19).
(Uniform coins) All coins are uniform and independent, as in Definition C.19; salts have the compiled verifier’s
lengths.

74

Definition C.22 (Eight conditions on nonces and positions). Let a table system and data for the compiled
verifiers of its three IOPs (Construction D.11) be given. A condition stated for each IOP counts three times.
(Nonce-free positions) For each IOP: the positions read are independent of the nonces.
(Nonces readable) For each IOP: a plain part determines its nonce, up to the search limit.
(Plain seeds) The two queries of Seed have the nonce 0.
(Local verifiers) Each decision depends on the oracle strings only at the positions read.
Definition C.23 (Three conditions on formats). In the setting of Definition C.22:
(Round formats) The rounds are own rounds, then shared rounds, then own rounds, beginning with an own round.
In an own round the shared component is empty; in a shared round other than the first, the own components are.
(Coherent rounds) The plain part of a message is the pair of its components’ plain parts, and each oracle position
is answered from one component alone.
(Consistent layout) A uniform oracle answer gives a uniform challenge, of which the proof-of-work bits are a
separate coordinate. Each position of an oracle part is one leaf of one tree of its component. A table fits its
Merkle tree, position ϕ at leaf ϕ, and a batch its tree. Symbols in leaves can be read back. Openings and tree
depths are within declared bounds. The code of the OOD answers into digests is injective and of fixed length.
Definition C.24 (Hashing error). In the setting of Definition C.20, the queries of one run of the committer and N
of the prover are counted at names o: the oracles of Construction D.11 and, for each IOP and i ≥ 2, a further name
for attempt number i of all proof-of-work searches of that IOP.
• mo is the number of queries at o that contain a secret string: all leaves of Merkle trees over oracle strings;
attempt number 1 of each round’s query to fs; the further attempts, up to the search limits; each compiled run’s
query to g, leaf and inner nodes in the batch tree.
• eo is 2(K + 1) at the oracle of Seed and 0 elsewhere.
• bo is ℓs at such a leaf, the minimum of λ and the length of the chain salt at a query to fs, and λ elsewhere.
The hashing error is
