over names with mo ̸= 0.
Theorem C.25 (Reduction of index privacy to twenty conditions). [machine-checked] Let a table system
(Definition C.3), data for its compiled verifiers, provers P, simulators Sim∗ , and J , Y, U, K, Nc , ζ∗sim , ζ∗ab , εU , ℓs be
given. Suppose that the fifteen conditions of Definition C.4, Definition C.22, and Definition C.23 hold, and the five of
Definition C.21, of which (Exact simulation) and (Rare private aborts) are required at the OOD seeds of U alone. Let S
assign to each index of J a set of instances with which it forms a pair of Y. Then the whole-key argument formed from
these data as in Definition C.20, with constant pp, has index privacy on S for batches of at most 1 (Definition 2.14),
with error
ζ(q, N ) + (K + 1) εU ,
where ζ is the error of Theorem 6.6.
Proof. Fix A ∈ J , a valid environment and V ∗ .
Step 1: lazy sampling. Committer and prover answer their queries that contain a secret string with fresh uniform
values, without consulting the table of oracle answers. Such an answer is entered into that table only where a proof
string discloses the query: at opened leaves and at queries that are not leaves, never at an unopened leaf or after ⊥.
The distribution changes only if such a query coincides with another query. A query at the name o contains bo uniform
secret bits, and two attempts of one search differ in the nonce. A union bound over the q queries of V ∗ gives the first
term of ζhash (q, N ) (Definition C.24), one over the pairs of queries of committer and prover at one name the second.
Only (Uniform coins), (Consistent layout), (Plain seeds) and (Nonces readable) are used.
Step 2: a reduction to the table game. After Step 1 a table’s leaf digests are fresh and independent of it. Root and
OOD seed are therefore computed from sampled digests before the table is asked for, which is the order of the table
experiment. Hence the experiment is obtained by a reduction from the table game (Definition C.13) with provers P,
one offline proof, one link proof for each copy and Nc online proofs on each copy. They differ if some copy is sampled
more than Nc times, with probability at most K · Pr[Bin(N, 1/K) > Nc ], or a seed lies outside U, with probability at
most (K + 1) εU by (Good seeds).
Step 3: simulating the game. By the two budget conditions the game’s numbers satisfy the inequality of
Definition C.6. Challenges are derived from fresh answers; by (Consistent layout), the event that no search of a

75

compiled run fails and ch lies in a given set has at most that set’s probability under uniform ch with proof-of-work
bits 0. By (Rare private aborts) and (Exact simulation), a run differs from its simulation with probability at most
ζ∗ab + ζ∗sim . By Lemma C.14, the game at A with P is replaced by the game at a fixed A0 with Sim∗ , at the sum of
these terms over its 1 + K + N runs.
Step 4: the ideal experiment. The reduction around the simulated game depends on A0 and the revealed instances
alone; it is the simulator, and never answers one query in two ways.
