Proof of Corollary 6.7. Part 1. Where Chk(Z) = 1 a leaf salt has 576 bits, so Lemma C.28 admits ℓs = 320.
Theorem 6.6 applies with ζ∗sim = ζ∗ab = 0, and Lemma C.27 gives ζ ≤ ζmax . For K = N = 1 and q ≤ 264 the numerator
is at most 2121 .
Part 2. A search fails only if 240 + 1 fresh answers miss a test of at most 32 bits, with probability at most
40
(1 − 2−32 )2 ≤ 2−256 . The three IOPs have at most 19, 6 and 46 rounds with proof of work. The committer runs
∗
before V , and its 19 + 6K searches fail with probability below the stated bound. The event that some πreq = ⊥ is
a set of views, so its probability exceeds that in the ideal experiment by at most ζmax (q, N ); there the Fiat–Shamir
queries of a simulated run receive fresh answers.
On the scope of Corollary 6.7:
• Beyond one copy and one request the bound is a formula, which no theorem evaluates at the default configuration
(Section 6.3 has a value evaluated by hand).
• That the simulated view holds the roots, OOD answers and opened columns is read off the definitions, not
proved.
• That each proof opens a column of a committed table is proved for the run of committer and prover alone and
for one verifier that makes no query, not for every verifier.
• For two matrices of J and an environment valid at both, the real views are within 2 ζ(q, N ), which is below 1
only if ζ(q, N ) < 1/2.

