C.4

The link proof

Proof of Theorem 6.9, outline. Part 1. The abort set of Plink is empty by definition. Fix the input and ch. Prover
and simulator have the same finite set of coins, vectors co of field elements; let disP (co) and disSim (co) be what each
discloses at the coins co. There are maps sh, sh′ on coins such that, for all co,
disP (co) = disSim co + sh(co) ,



sh′ co + sh(co) = sh(co).



For intuition, consider a masked value: a secret plus a uniform coin is distributed as the coin alone; here sh plays
the role of the secret. By the second equation, two coins with the same image under co 7→ co + sh(co) have the same
sh, so they are equal. The map is injective, so a bijection of the finite set of coins, and by the first equation the two
outputs are identically distributed under uniform coins. The equations use that no oracle part is opened at more
positions than its rows have random coefficients, and that the claim left at the last sub-protocol is true in the honest
run.
Part 2. The verifier accepts Plink whenever the OOD answers are those of the row polynomials, the tables are their
evaluations, both encode the same plain entries, and the symbols have the format’s form, as for two committed tables
of one matrix. The other conditions of acceptance hold at the challenges that pass the proof-of-work checks.
For its own constructions, [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026, Theorem 10.2] proves honest-verifier zero
knowledge of the proximity test that IOPlink
runs with changes (Section 4.5), resting in substance on its Lemmas 6.4,
Z
7.3 and 9.8, through its Theorems 9.10 and 7.1, by its composition theorem (its Theorem 4.5); Part 1 is proved for the
whole of IOPlink
at once, by a bijection on coins.
