77

Remark D.2 (Written copy). The release ships Z0 and Z1 with its two stored proofs for the integer model at synthetic
weights. No theorem relates the shipped files to the Lean terms; the release contains a program that compares them.
The digests in Definition D.1 are the terms’, computed outside Lean. The value of ϵ1 = ϵ2 = ϵF in µ0 stands in the
shipped examples/full-size/config/policy.txt.
Construction D.3 (The integer model). Put
(dmodel , nh , ng , dh , f, fe , NE ) = (5120, 40, 8, 128, 16384, 8192, 128).
The weights M (Definition 3.1) are the following integer matrices: an embedding matrix (|V| × dmodel ), an output
matrix (dmodel × |V|) and, in each layer,
Wq : dmodel × nh dh ,

Wk , Wv : dmodel × ng dh ,

Wg , Wu : dmodel × dhid ,

Wo : nh dh × dmodel ,

Wd : dhid × dmodel ,

with dhid = f in an even layer; an odd layer has dhid = fe , a router matrix (dmodel × NE ) and NE further triples of
the shapes of Wg , Wu , Wd , the routed experts. Entries of the embedding matrix lie in [−2155905152, 2139062143], all
others in [−212 , 212 ).
Let µ be valid (Definition 3.6) and x̄ a context (Definition 3.1). At each token position of x̄ the model starts from
the token’s row of the embedding matrix; each layer adds the output of a causal attention block and then of a gated
feed-forward block, each applied to a normalized input; an odd layer evaluates Wg , Wu , Wd and the routed expert
that the router ranks first. The logits logitM (x̄) are the normalized vector at the last token position times the output
matrix. All steps are exact integer operations. Eight kinds of intermediate value have prescribed intervals; the model
is defined on x̄ if these hold at every token position.
Remark D.4 (Model specification). Construction D.3 is an outline of the Lean term that defines the integer model
(Section 3.1). The release’s docs/model.md gives every step, the eight intervals and the five constant lookup tables; cosine
and sine share one of the four digests of tables in the parameter file. In Lean the model holds its five tables as constants:
no theorem describes their content, and none ties them to these digests. On synthetic weights at small sizes the Lean
function agrees with the release’s reference program in every value that was compared, over Lean files and programs
that the integer-model package’s pages name (integrations/vllm/integer_model/tests/lean_vectors/README.md);
no theorem relates the two.
Remark D.5 (No real weights shown). On trained weights (Remark 3.3, Trained weights):
1. Counted outside Lean. A reference program like the release’s, not the released one, under a parameter file that
the release does not contain, found no range condition failing on 544 of 544 windows, cut from 96 prompts (what
the count does not show: lean/LIMITS.md, “At real weights: a count, no theorem”), at a public model’s 4-bit
weights.
2. Run. The C++ programs have proved requests on these weights; the timed runs of Section 7.2 do so, at a
configuration fitted to the weights (Section 7.1). The release ships the fitting tools and no fitted configuration. A
fitted configuration of timed exact-match runs on these weights passes the scope test Adm (Definition 3.8) by one
evaluation (whether it is these runs’ own files, byte for byte, is not shown), with Lean sources as in Section 7.1
(no theorem); every security line of its parameter file is the default’s. The parameter checks (Definition 4.14)
were not evaluated at it; where they fail, VZ rejects every input. The configuration of the clipped-gap rows was
not put to the scope test.
3. Not tried. The executable form of VZ has been run on proofs for synthetic weights (Section 7.3), not on proofs
for a trained model’s weights.
The sources of the sub-protocols are in Table 11; the Proximity test’s Fold, Code switch and Base case are, with
changes (Section 4.5), Constructions 6.3, 9.7 and 7.2 of [CFW26, version of February 25, 2026]. Send has the masking
of the answers from its Section 9.2, here with the pads in the message itself (in its Construction 9.7, in a mask
oracle); the main course of Masked sumcheck, with committed masks whose values are not revealed but join the
output claim, has the form of steps 1 to 4 of its Construction 6.3 and, for a polynomial summand, of steps 4 to 6
of its Construction 11.4. Steps 2 and 4 of Construction D.7 have the form of steps 1 to 4 of its Construction 9.7,
which carries over to zero-knowledge encodings a known template for code switching, here with the table’s random
coefficients in the new message.
Construction D.6 (Sub-protocols). This construction gives the course of each sub-protocol (Remark D.10, Given
in outline). An oracle message is a list of rows, each sent as a masked encoding (Definition 4.2) under a code that
Z fixes; its mask rows are uniform. A group is a set of these under one code. A linear constraint is an equation
⟨co, f ⟩ = tg on the entries f of a group, with co, tg computed by V . A challenge is a uniform tuple of blocks of λ bits,
from which fixed maps compute what V samples (Lemma A.20).

