(d) the segment’s own rounds: sampled points, Batching and masked sumchecks reduce its identities to claims
on cells and on the table;
(e) Range test on the cells that the segment reads;
(f) in the head alone, Batching and a masked sumcheck on its claim on the table;
(g) Batching of the segment’s claims into one constraint on its group; Batching of the group’s constraints; Fold.
Then Code switch and Proximity test on the last group; Batching and a masked sumcheck, as in IOPkey
Z , leave
one claim (h, ψ, η, b); Send w.
2. Shared and last part. Construction D.7 with ν = νon , with κ = κbat in the shared part and κreq in the last.
3. V rejects unless the token of step (a) is the y + of ξ.
4. After every sub-protocol of the first part other than step (a), V rejects if its stored value of that token has
changed.
Each verifier also rejects if a group is left untested.
Remark D.10 (Given in outline). This paper gives VZ in outline. Its Lean code defines, and the paper does not print:

80

• the placement rule and wt (Definition 4.1);
• the arithmetization (Definition 4.8), with the rounds of step (d) of Construction D.9; Lemma 4.9 states its
meaning; that the lemma applies at an admissible configuration joins Lean theorems by hand, a step that
Lean’s proof of Theorem 5.1 does not use, since that the three byte strings of Z decode without error, which
admissibility implies, is one of that theorem’s hypotheses in Lean;
• the closed list of the parameter checks (Definition 4.14), which are computed with a constant in the place of
dgZ ; among them are a second derivation of the rounds, from the sizes alone, with one decoding radius for each
shape of oracle message, and, in attention segments, a count of each round’s bad challenges;
• the sub-protocols in detail (Construction D.6);
• the maps code, the strings of the queries and the bijection (Construction D.11).
The release’s format specification states what is hashed and sent.
Construction D.11 (Oracles, seeds, and the compiled verifier of one IOP). Oracles. The algorithms of ΠZ
query separate oracles: for leaves and inner nodes of Merkle trees over oracle strings; for those of the batch tree, with
one leaf for each request of a batch; and, for each IOP, a Fiat–Shamir oracle fs and an oracle g. A bijection between bit
strings and triples (oracle, query, number of an answer block) implements them by H. The public readings determine
it.
Public readings. The pair (dgZ , par), where par consists of the values of H at up to seven strings, formed from
Z, dgZ and H ′ , that state the parameters of the three IOPs. ExtZ chooses public readings whose digest is dg, and
translates every query of tr by their bijection.
Unpacking. Step 2 of Construction 4.15 unpacks the byte strings of π at dgZ , by a query of its own; step 3 queries
code(Z) again. If π cannot be unpacked, VZ continues with a fixed tuple whose fill is not a list of tokens of V; step 4
then rejects. A byte string that is unpacked but gives no proof string of its IOP’s shape gives ⊥, and step 6 rejects.
Commitment to an oracle string. A Merkle root; the leaf at a position hashes the symbol there and a leaf salt.
Seed of a root. Seed(rt) is read off the second of two answers of one Fiat–Shamir oracle: the first query holds rt,
dgZ and the table’s sizes, the second the first answer’s chaining value.
Proof string. A chain salt; the roots of the prover’s oracle parts; its plain parts (Definition 2.3); openings; the
batch tree’s root, a leaf number, a path and a leaf salt.
Compiled verifier, given a list cms of digests, an IOP instance and a proof string. For IOPreq
Z the list holds the
copy’s root twice.
1. Recompute the challenges. Every round’s query to fs holds the round’s number, roots and plain part, nonce
+
included. The first also holds cms, the chain salt and, for IOPreq
replaced
Z , the early part of ξ, which is ξ with y
by 0; each further one the chaining value of the preceding answer. An answer gives the challenge, bits of proof of
work included, and a chaining value.
2. At the first shared round, g maps the request’s chaining value and own component to its batch value, and the
query to fs holds cms, the batch tree’s root and the shared component. The last part continues from the shared
part’s chaining value and the batch value.
3. Reject unless the salts have their fixed lengths and the openings are exactly those that the IOP’s verifier reads.
4. Verify every opening against its root and, where the IOP has shared rounds, the batch value, with its leaf salt,
against the batch tree’s root.
5. Decide as the IOP’s verifier does on the challenges, the plain parts and the opened symbols.
Construction D.12 (Committer and prover of ΠZ ). RndZ outputs the masks and salts. CommitH
Z (M, r) forms
T0 , . . . , TK (Definition 4.6) for a digit matrix determined by M , computes rtj , ςj = Seed(rtj ) and aj , makes πkey and
each πlink as the honest provers of Construction D.9 do at the recomputed challenges, and returns H(code(k, dgZ )).
PZH (M, r, x, y) makes πreq likewise, with empty fill; for a batch it chooses a copy within the opening budget (Definition C.6) and places the requests at distinct random leaves of the batch tree.
Remark D.13 (How the two hash functions differ). O∗ (Assumption 4.10) and H ′ (Assumption 4.11) both iterate the
compression function of BLAKE2b, which takes a counter of 128 bits. O∗ evaluates BLAKE2b as specified, on labelled
strings of fewer than 232 bytes; the upper 64 bits of the counter are 0 in each of its calls. H ′ starts from the state of
BLAKE2b with 64-byte output and sets these 64 bits to num + 232 k in every call, where k is the number of 32-bit
words of its input and num is one of the four numbers in use, none of them 0. The two functions hence differ in that
half of the counter in every call. That this keeps them apart is part of Assumption 4.11: no formal proof states it, and
the published analysis of BLAKE2b does not cover H ′ .
VZ evaluates H ′ on public tables and on parts of the statement, x and y among them. Its digest of the encoded
request is part of the first string that an online proof hashes (Construction D.11). The digest is redundant there: the

81

same string holds the window, with x and y, and the two token positions; the digest is compared with nothing; and
the coding of that string is injective by the encoded request alone, whatever the values of H ′ (proof of Lemma A.17).
Hence a collision of H ′ merges no two queries.
Remark D.14 (How the parameters are obtained). In Table 2 the parameter file states the sizes of the tables and the
bits of proof of work. Every other value is calculated from that file and taken from no run. A verifier program calculates
these values without checking a proof; δ and the bound on K are calculated from its values, and no program outputs
them. Rates are rounded up, proximity parameters down. The reduced configurations of Section 7 are synthetic models
far smaller than the default one.
• Range test. V samples a matrix Θ with 128 columns whose entries are 0 with probability 1/2 and ±1 with
probability 1/4 each. P sends each entry of the product as two digits; an entry that two digits can express lies
in [−217 , 217 ).
• Copies and batches. A copy serves at most 688,127 batches of up to 64 requests, and a commitment has at most
710,324 copies; both numbers are calculated. The committer fixes K when it commits. Every started batch
counts towards Nj , whatever its size. These 688,127 batches are about 4.81 years (1,759 days) of batches of
24 requests back to back on one GPU that never pauses, calculated from the measured time of such a batch
(Section 7), and less with smaller ones. The programs choose a batch’s copy at random (from code review).

