E.2

The forward pass, other systems, batches and policies

The forward pass. Softmax, attention, and rounding by a lookup of the remainder follow [SLZ24, QSL25, CWS24].

84

zkComposer [SGO26] cuts the computation into parts, by layers or by input positions, and proves each part on its
own. What passes from one part to the next is committed to, and that ties the parts’ proofs together. They can be
made in parallel or, to save memory, one at a time. DeepProve [GHL26, Section 4.5] likewise partitions the model into
chunks of consecutive layers, for several machines to prove. NanoZK [Wan26] proves each layer apart and links the
proofs by a chain of hash commitments.
Our segments are not proofs of their own: one proof runs through all of them in order, and a closed segment’s folded
row and the rows that a later segment still needs are sent on, encoded anew, with the claims on them (Construction D.9).

Batches. Our batches rest on the observation of SLP [LY26] that the weights, not the tokens, dominate the cost.
In SLP, one proof covers many requests; here, each request gets a separate proof. A Merkle tree over several verifiers’
transcripts, whose root serves as the challenge for all of them, appears in [YLFKM21]; [GGQZ25, Section 4.2] uses one
for proofs of neural networks and credits [ZXHSZ22].

Many proofs. The hiding theorems of [SLZ24, GGQZ25] cover one prompt or one query. For many proofs under

one commitment, [GGQZ25, Section 4.3], whose own aim is to hide the architecture too, notes that a one-time mask
fails, and masks the committed polynomial afresh for each proof through the commitment’s homomorphism.
As we understand it, a design like ours, in which each proof reveals positions of a table committed once, needs an
opening budget; a homomorphic commitment can be masked afresh for each proof and needs none. A hash-based
commitment needs no number-theoretic assumption, where zkLLM’s Hyrax commitment [WTSTW18] rests on the
discrete logarithm problem; neither needs a trusted setup.

Policies. At temperature 0, the logit margin of Token-DiFR [DiFR, Section 4.2] equals our logit gap up to one unit

of the integer logits, which our rule for ties can add (Definition 3.4). The equality is one of formulas on the same logits:
Token-DiFR’s are a reference model’s floating-point logits, and ours are the committed integer model’s integer logits.

