The Lean library

Codes. The proof of Theorem 5.1 calls theorems on Reed–Solomon codes, above all mutual correlated agreement

below the Johnson radius (Lemma 5.13, the statement that Lean’s theorems use in the place of [BCHKS25, Theorem
4.6]). As typed in the library they are proved there, not assumed, from files adapted from ArkLib and Mathlib
[ArkLib, Mathlib] (lean/ThirdParty/).
A public Lean library, ArkLib [ArkLib] (Apache-2.0), has a theorem of this kind: mutual correlated agreement of
Reed–Solomon codes on affine lines, for every radius below the Johnson radius (the probability form, which we have not
checked); the source’s own form of the bound is not proved there. The proof runs through adapted proofs of two theorems
of ArkLib’s: a counting form of such a statement and a bound on its count. Files of our own (lean/Cb/Bridge/) prove
that, under the statement’s premises, the estimate in the second equals the bound in the statement, and derive the

85

statement from the two. Two of these files (CodeListRSFromLines.lean, CodeListRSProved.lean) write out ArkLib’s
estimate and restate what its two theorems give together. Their heads say that the two cannot count as independent
of one more file of ArkLib’s, Johnson/Probability.lean, the file of the probability form, which the next paragraph
names.
The commit of [ArkLib] is cited as named in the copy that we used; we have not looked that commit up on ArkLib’s
public repository. ArkLib’s declarations in question are ReedSolomon.exists_johnson_line_exactCorrelatedPair
and ReedSolomon.HiddenDerivative.johnsonExceptionCount_le_comparisonEstimate, and ReedSolomon.mcaError_
affineLine_johnson_le (in ArkLib/Data/CodingTheory/ReedSolomon/MutualCorrelatedAgreement/Johnson/
Probability.lean), the probability form, which we have not checked; two files of our own do not count as written
independently of that file (the paragraph above). Files adapted from it come with this repository’s Lean library
(lean/ThirdParty/). Comments in six of these files credit further sources, among them the formalization kz99/rs-ld-mca
(“adapted, with permission”, in ArkLib’s words; that work, its licence and the permission were not seen) and the
public project proximity-prize; lean/ThirdParty/README.md lists them.

