Further limitations

These limitations continue Section 8.1. The judgement and an honest run are defined in Definition H.1.

The implementation.
Limitation G.1 (Implementation unverified). The C++ and CUDA programs and the Python check script are tested,
not formally verified, as is usual: no theorem concerns them, for soundness, weight hiding or completeness, and
no security level is claimed for them. They are only partly tested, with bad results among the tests. The released
programs run a scored output head under every policy; that it is the head of Definition 3.4 is tested, not proved
(Limitation 8.2, Protocol match unproved), and at one setting that no admissible configuration has its cost differs
(docs/limits-all-rows.tsv, SO-6.3.1.1). Between Theorem 5.1 and the C++ verifier’s answer on a real file lie several
steps, none closed by a theorem (lean/GAPS.md, docs/limits.md).
Remark G.2 (Stricter counts). For the IOPs of Construction 4.13, at every admissible Z with Chk(Z) = 1, the error
2−128 of the Range test’s round is machine-checked (Proposition 5.14). The figures that the C++ verifier prints for
single rounds follow another accounting, which no theorem relates to these IOPs (Limitation 8.2, Protocol match
unproved).
In that accounting the Range test’s round, which has no proof of work, and the positions in a link proof use a
smaller error than a stricter count would give, and a batch’s shared rounds are counted once. That the smaller errors
suffice there is not proved; which count of an own round applies to a single request is not known. By a union bound
over all columns of the matrix of cells that is tested, not of Θ, the Range test’s round would have 113.44 bits at the
default configuration, calculated and taken from no run, and so miss the target of 128 (Table 15); with a factor for the
number of requests, some shared rounds miss it too.

94

Table 15: Figures in bits. Programs: the programs’ own accounting at the default configuration, calculated and
taken from no run, and no security level. 113.44 and “6 fewer” are printed by the released verifier’s planning
call. “About 122.5”, with the rounds’ proof of work counted, is reckoned by hand from a list of rounds stored
in the repository, which the released verifier’s planning call prints again line for line (the repository’s test
verifier.kit_rounds). No figure is a level of the released programs.
