The relation.
Limitation G.3 (Greedy outputs and a fixed window only). Sampled outputs are not covered. By design a proof covers
token IDs, not text, in a window whose length the configuration fixes, 1,024 tokens at the default configuration: every
request fills a whole window and costs a full window of proving. An output cut short verifies as a shorter output, and
under a clipped-gap policy a provider can still end one early (docs/verifying.md).
Limitation G.4 (Prefixes count). Nothing is stated about where the model would stop: any prefix of its output is a
greedy output, so the client must check that the output is as long as expected, since Theorem 5.1 states nothing on
length. The empty prefix is one too. By step 4 of Construction 4.15, VZ rejects a request whose claimed output is
empty, since build is undefined there (Definition 4.7); no theorem states this by itself; Servµ leaves such requests out
(Definition 3.11). Every non-empty prefix of the model’s own output meets every admissible policy.

Security model.
Limitation G.5 (Fiat–Shamir instantiation). That BLAKE2b, on which the programs build the hash function that
Theorem 5.1 samples, may be treated as a random oracle, sampled after the configuration is fixed, is assumed; no proof
can close this, as is usual. The bound states nothing of the concrete functions. One constant beneath the Lean verifier
is a value of the programs’ own hash function; that nothing beneath the verifier reads it rests on a reading of the code,
not on a theorem (lean/THEOREM.md). Fiat–Shamir attacks on protocols of this kind have not been assessed, among
them attacks on GKR-style protocols that use a concrete hash function [KRS25] and on protocols whose instances a
program generates [Fen26] (docs/limits.md, lean/LIMITS.md).
Limitation G.6 (Classical adversaries). As is usual, in all machine-checked statements the committer and the prover
query the oracle classically, so nothing is claimed against a quantum adversary.

Evidence.
Limitation G.7 (No independent review). The tests, runs, comparisons and readings were made and reviewed within
this project, so reviews are not independent, and no written proof is verified or independently reviewed. The technical
report’s written proofs for soundness and for zero knowledge are not settled, and readings of them within this project
have found errors (Appendix E.1; docs/limits.md). Each count, outcome, figure or condition of Section 7 and
Appendix H is its producers’ account unless another checker is named. To our knowledge no person has checked the
written proofs, the code, or the logs of any run; of the Lean statements, six definitions of properties were shaped with
a person, which is not a check of them (Section “Who wrote and checked this”); the Lean statements apart from the
six top theorems and the final statement’s six fields no person has checked, and the definitions that lie beneath these
other statements alone no person has read in full; Lean’s kernel checked the proofs.
Limitation G.8 (Few runs). The figures of Table 3 rest on three runs each, each row’s made close together in time,
whose rates differ, row by row, by 1.5 to 1.6 % in the three rows at exact match and by 1.5 to 4.7 % over all six (the
most over the least), with no cause shown. The first row’s ± 0.92 is the standard deviation of three runs made side by
side: three runs of the same kind on other programs, on another day on other machines, had a standard deviation of
3.9; whether the programs, the machines, the day or the rule of the steady rate made that difference is not shown.
Each row’s spread reflects the machine and the seed, that of the like three runs also the order of the kinds of run on

95

a machine; it does not cover other prompts, days, kinds of machine or programs. Many measurements not on the
released programs, and most counts of tests, are single runs (docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.9 (Quality unmeasured). The model of the theorems is the integer model (Definition 3.1), a program in
integer arithmetic with the shape of Llama 4 Maverick, not the floating-point model; how far the two differ is stated by
no theorem. That the released programs prove this same program is tested where compared, not proved (Remark D.4,
Model specification); the model is defined at some inputs, weights and policies for which they make no proof. Its task
quality has not been measured: no task benchmark was run (docs/limits.md).

Visible by design.
Remark G.10 (What a client of the programs learns). By design, clients and the public still see some things. From an
honest, fault-free run of the implementation, with the provider’s operating conditions kept and a randomly sampled
secret seed, a client learns at least:
1. whether each request was served or failed;
2. the architecture, shape, ranges and fitted values;
3. each next token;
4. batch membership and timing;
5. failures and stops;
6. roughly how much was served;
7. the OOD answers.
The leakage (Definition 2.13) holds items 3 and 6, item 1 for served requests, and which revealed requests share a
batch. Item 2 is part of Z. Failed requests, timing and item 5 are outside the experiments. Item 7 is for the simulator
to produce: an OOD answer is computed from the weights and a pad, and with a fresh uniform pad, at a seed chosen
before the table is sampled, its distribution does not depend on the weights (Theorem 6.3). No statement covers the
OOD answers as the programs make them (Remark 6.4, No prover message is covered).
That nothing else is visible is not established: the release’s longer list (its page “What is not hidden”) is not shown
to be complete, or to match the released programs.
Limitation G.11 (Outcome leakage). By design a client learns whether each request is served or fails, and nothing
bounds what many answers reveal together. Whether an instance is served, that is, whether (M, x, y) lies in Servµ
(Definition 3.11), depends on the weights and is not hidden: in the results on the Lean provers the simulator is given
it, one bit per request, before compilation and in the statement at every request after it (Theorem 6.37; in a corollary,
within one label per request: served, failed or other tokens), and where a request is not served the prover shows
nothing before compilation and returns the empty proof after it; in the statement on served requests (Theorem 6.35) it
is given the requests and beside these nothing of the weights, and only served requests are asked (Section 6.5). A
bit says whether the claimed output is the model’s result on that request, so over many requests the bits tell what
the model answers. That a request is refused is unchecked, as the label is: an empty proof proves nothing, and a
prover can return it at a request that it could serve; that a service says “failed” rightly is not proved either. For the
implementation one bit is not a cap: no statement bounds how much is revealed, and rarer causes of a failure can also
depend on hidden values (docs/what-is-not-hidden.md).

Operating the released programs: eleven limitations.
Limitation G.12 (Further commitments). The judgement of Section 7.5 takes in a further commitment to the same
weights, which renewal or a second GPU requires, on thin ground. Among its five conditions: each commitment must
sample its own secret seed, on a machine that was not cloned or restored, take nothing from another, and keep the
same configuration files (all five: Appendix H.5). No test has searched two commitments together for leaks. With
hidden weights, nothing that a client can check shows that two commitments fix the same weights (docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.13 (Mask faults unnoticed). The first of the two main risks to weight hiding in the programs is a mask
silently lost to a bug or a hardware fault; the second is Limitation 8.4 (Opening budget). A lost mask can go unnoticed:
a file with an unmasked value can still verify, and no verifier can see it. A hardware fault or a compiler fault lies
outside an honest run (Definition H.1), and so outside the judgement of Section 7.5; a bug in the sources lies inside
both. Four compiler faults are known, at least three of which can act silently. One of them was seen to give wrong
values in the test of the prover’s own product routine, never in a mask or a file, though no build looks for a wrong
value there. It showed under an older version of the GPU compiler than the build requires, which built the GPU

96

programs of many of the tests and runs that the release’s pages report as not on the released programs; whether the
required version has it is not known (docs/how-hiding-is-checked.md, docs/limits.md). No one has looked for a
way for a client to cause such a fault.
Limitation G.14 (Published values are permanent). One lost mask can give away at least one exact equation in hidden
values, in a published file, to everyone and for good. Without its mask an OOD answer gives one exact linear equation
on a whole row of weights, permanently public, and so does every OOD answer if the secret seed gets out at any time.
Remark G.15 (Pseudorandom masks). All machine-checked statements of Section 6 suppose fresh randomness. The
released programs derive their masks and other random values, the pads of the OOD answers among them, from a
secret seed by a keyed function, the cipher ChaCha20 [RFC8439], which no machine-checked statement covers. For
them index privacy is computational at best (Limitation G.14, Published values are permanent).
Limitation G.16 (Operating conditions). Weight hiding in the programs rests on operating conditions of the provider,
most of which no program can check. Meeting them does not by itself show that the weights are hidden, and breaking
one can leak weights permanently and silently. All of them apply: the release’s thirteen conditions and their further
requirements. One of them is a check that the provider must make on every batch. The prover checks none of its
proofs with the verifier, and a proof spoiled by a bug or a hardware fault can fail to verify and hold unmasked values,
by readings of the code; so no response may leave the provider before the C++ verifier has accepted every proof
of its batch. Of what ships, by readings of the code, the middleware of the vLLM integration, from which clients
fetch their responses, hands on no response that has a proof before the C++ verifier has accepted every proof of
its batch; driver/measure_rate.py reports no rate, and an example script shows no answer of the model’s, before
the C++ verifier has accepted the proofs; a front end of a provider’s own must make that check too. At a rejection
the middleware overwrites the batch’s proofs, answers its requests as failed, and stops handing out proofs under
that batch’s acceptance policy until the operator lifts this hold; where a batch cannot be judged, it stops likewise,
at the second try in a row or at the tenth at the latest, and deletes nothing. That check misses a lost mask in a
proof that verifies (Limitation G.13). Narrow exceptions to what the middleware holds back and to what it does at a
rejection, none open to a client, are told in integrations/LIMITS.md. Nothing is hidden from anyone who can read
the provider’s disks, memory or GPU, and the shipped vLLM integration breaks at least one condition; in it vLLM
and the prover run under one account, which can read and change the weight files, the secret key directory and the
request directory (docs/limits.md). By a reading of the code, whoever can write the secret key directory, or name a
directory of their own as the directory of weights, may learn about the weights from how the prover’s starts end; the
commitment covers no line of the text file in the secret key directory, the line on the weights among them.
Limitation G.17 (Tensors of eight or sixteen bits). Nothing stated here on weight hiding in the programs, the judgement
of Section 7.5 included, covers a weight file in which any tensor is stored in 8 or 16 bits. The programs accept such
files without a warning; it is for the provider to know how each tensor is stored. The examples’ public 4-bit conversion
has no such tensor (docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.18 (One trial with such a file). No test of hiding on the released programs has used a weight file
with a tensor of 8 or 16 bits (Limitation G.17, Tensors of eight or sixteen bits); in the one trial not on the released
programs, a party that could write such a file but not read it learned the magnitudes of two weights of their choice
(docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.19 (Overlapping batches). By default, by a reading of the code, the prover takes a batch while one
other, which it has proved, has not been released. In the vLLM integration’s middleware every stored response waits
until somebody asks for its batch, up to 1,000 responses by default; a rejection there withholds one batch, and a hold
(Limitation G.16) keeps back all of them and deletes none. No proved statement covers batches that overlap in time,
so none reaches a request that is sent again to the service, at once or later. No attack through the overlap is known;
the only search for one was a brief reading of the code, with nothing run (docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.20 (Other requests in a batch). For the programs, hiding a batch’s other requests is a design goal: not
proved, never compared in a run of the prover, and outside the judgement of Section 7.5, which concerns the weights.
Any client can submit a request that cannot be proved; for the implementation no statement on hiding then covers the
batch’s other proofs (docs/what-is-not-hidden.md, docs/limits.md).
Limitation G.21 (Clipped gap reveals logits). Under a clipped-gap policy, which ordinary serving uses, each answer also
reveals by design something about the model’s logits, and so about its weights, even if the masks hold; no statement
bounds how much. Many requests can measure hidden logit gaps, up to the clip: outputs cut one token apart at a
limit are such questions. Nothing bounds what many answers, time or a stop reveal (docs/what-is-not-hidden.md).

97

Limitation G.22 (Unmarked public-seed mode). A commitment does not say in which mode it was made: the
release’s mode for a public seed in place of the secret seed, in which nothing is hidden, neither the weights nor
a batch’s other requests, leaves no mark in what is published, so neither a client nor the verifier can detect it
(docs/what-is-not-hidden.md).
Limitation G.23 (Second verifier text). The Lean library holds the text of a second verifier, written after the C++
verifier’s source text; the release does not build it as a program. It is not VZ . Definitions from that text also stand in
VZ ’s own term (lean/GAPS.md, gap 58); the second verifier as a whole does not. Some runs that this paper tells set it,
built as a program, beside the C++ verifier (Appendix H.1): the two are not independent, so their agreement adds no
evidence, and they are known to differ (lean/OnBytes/LIMITS.md, docs/limits.md).

