The artifact and the evaluation in detail

Under the name of each table of Section 7, this appendix gives the conditions, the provenance and the limits of its
results.

98

H.1

Artifact and programs

In short. The runs that this subsection reports as runs on the released programs were made on programs built

from sources that differ from these in comments, names and the wording of messages alone; of the programs that these
sources themselves build it reports three small runs and one at the default configuration’s size (“Which programs ran”,
below); the sources of cp_verify do not differ. In the test runs that cover all 173 public tests, most of which start
none of the three programs, 170 passed, one passed in part, two were not run and none failed.
On synthetic weights, the released cp_verify and the Lean verifier were given the same sets of files at the default
configuration’s size, as reported: both accepted all 47 honest proofs, and neither accepted one of the 57 proof files
from malicious provers; at 3 of the 57 the Lean command’s parser refused the file, which is not a verdict. The Lean
verifier also accepted 41 further proofs, on synthetic weights, as reported of the timed runs. One proof made at the
default configuration’s size, on synthetic weights, by the programs that these sources build, as reported, was accepted
by cp_verify and by the Lean verifier.
examples/maverick/run.sh ran at the default configuration’s size on the public weights, without building, downloading or installing: its five stored values came out the same, byte for byte, and cp_verify ended with YES. In the
timed runs on those weights, at the default configuration as fitted to them, all 1,851 requests were served, and all 1,851
proofs, each of the one token after the prompt, were accepted by the runs’ own calls of cp_verify. The first and the
fifth of the five tests of weight hiding ended as written down beforehand, the first also at the default configuration,
once on synthetic weights and once, as fitted to them, on the public weights.

What is left. The 57 proof files from malicious provers were made beforehand by test programs that this repository

cannot build, all at one commitment and for three requests: no malicious prover was run against the released programs,
and no rejection at the lookup checks themselves has been seen. At the reduced configuration cp_verify accepted
228 replayed calls on altered stored tables: that is right if each table still decodes to the honest weights alone, which
rests on counts of the changed columns: no decoder was run. Neither test of weight hiding shows that the masks hide,
and the other three of the five were made on other programs; no run shows what the programs leave in host or GPU
memory, and no audit with the provider’s secrets has run on a commitment to a real model’s weight files.
Two runs at 64 requests on synthetic weights failed, and no cause is shown. The tests and scripts of those test runs
differ from this repository’s, a few in code; of the public tests that need a GPU or the CUDA compiler only two have
been run on this repository’s own files. The programs that ran and the runs’ records are not in this repository, so
a reader cannot check how they are tied to these sources. Nothing in this subsection is a proof about the compiled
programs.

Which programs ran. The version of the release is the one line of the file VERSION at the repository’s top; this

PDF ships with it.
The release contains no model weights and no proof about the public weights.
The released programs are cp_commit, cp_prove and cp_verify (the committer, the prover and the C++ verifier)
as the release’s sources build them (the production build). The programs that made the runs that this paper tells as
runs on the released programs, or on a test program built from these sources, were built from sources that differ from
these in comments, in the names of identifiers and files, in seven quoted texts that hold such a name and in the wording
of eight messages (seven that cp_commit or cp_prove prints, one that a build script prints), and in nothing else.
This paper tells a program built from those as built from these sources, a run on it as a run on the released
programs or on a test program built from these sources, and a reading of those as a reading of these sources; how far
that tie is shown, and where it ends, is told under “What a reader can check”, below. This rule speaks of cp_commit,
cp_prove and cp_verify and of their test programs. How the tests’ own files, the serving scripts and the other scripts
of those runs differ from this repository’s (they differ in code too) is told under “What these runs do not show”, at
“The public tests”.
Of the cp_commit and cp_prove that this repository’s sources build, this paper tells four runs, three of them here
alone: the first and the fifth test of weight hiding, once each, at the reduced configuration, on synthetic weights, each
ending as written down beforehand; and a comparison with the middle build told under “What a reader can check”,
on synthetic weights: under one test seed the test programs of both sides gave the same files, byte for byte, at the
reduced configuration, and so did both production builds of cp_commit, called with -public-weights, at the reduced
configuration. The fourth made one commitment and one proof at the default configuration’s size, on synthetic weights:
its item under the calls of the Lean verifier, below, tells it. The counts of runs elsewhere in this paper leave these four
out.
This paper tells of them no timed run, no other run at the default configuration’s size, no run of
examples/maverick/run.sh and no public test that starts one of the two. The times, speeds and peaks of memory
that this paper gives as the released programs’ were measured on the programs of the runs, and are told of the released
programs on the comparison alone.

99

No program file is in this repository: a reader builds them, and no run told in this paper was made on a reader’s
or a provider’s own build. The runs told in this paper are of these kinds, told below in turn: runs on the released
programs (the timed runs are among these); runs on other programs, test programs among them; and, told apart, the
calls of the Lean verifier. Rows are those of docs/limits-all-rows.tsv.
On the released programs is said only of a run in the list below; in the timed runs, of those of them that are told
under that heading; not on the released programs († ), or on other programs, of every other run. Test programs are
programs in which a fault, a test switch or a test seed is built in (those of the second and the third test of hiding
bear ‡ ); a test program may or may not be built from the released sources. Where this paper says test programs, the
reduced configuration and the default configuration, the release’s pages say “on test builds”, “small size” and “full size”;
of a run on the public weights this paper says the default configuration’s size, or that configuration as fitted to them.

What ran on the released programs. The programs that ran were built from the sources told above, and are

tied to those by two digests: the verifier’s build word, and the digest in each prover’s build record (cp_commit.build,
cp_prove.build). The verifier’s build word is this repository’s too; the provers’ digest is not: comments, names and
the wording of messages turn it. Those records are not in this repository, so a reader cannot check that tie. Where a
run’s record holds no digest, its programs’ checksums are those of the build whose record holds both. Pages lie outside
both digests, and so do nearly all tests and most scripts. Of the 469 files under the provers’ digest 33 are not sources,
six scripts and a Makefile among them (what a reader can check: below).
A test program leaves no build record. That the test programs of these runs came from these sources rests only on
the logs of the test runs that made them, which are not in this repository; each checked a checksum of the sources
before it built.
Weights are synthetic, and each run was made once, unless a line says otherwise. The list tells what ran on them.
Of the public tests only some rows start one of the three: how many, and how many run test programs instead, is told
under “What these runs do not show”. The Lean verifier is none of them: its calls have a head of their own, below. A
run that neither the list nor “The public tests, in full” names is to be taken as not made on the released programs, but
for one test that is not in this repository, which by a reading of its commands ran them and passed (row RES-39), and
the runs of the check by the vLLM integration’s middleware told under the timed runs, and the four runs told under
“Which programs ran”.
• The public tests (SUITE.md: 173 rows), on one H100 a test run, or a CPU machine; reduced configurations,
synthetic weights, but for ten rows that give cp_verify a parameter file or a stored example of the default
configuration’s size, also on synthetic weights, and a few that run a single kernel, sub-proof or matrix at a
shape of the default configuration, also on synthetic data (among them gpu.base_kernels_test); no row runs
cp_commit or cp_prove at the default configuration’s size.
On programs that each test run built from these sources, 170 of the 173 rows passed, one passed in part, two
were not run and none failed. Apart from those test runs, on this repository’s files alone (without lean/; some
pages, tables, paper/paper.pdf, one list and licence and notice files differ), 90 of the 105 rows of the kind cpu
passed and two passed in part; the other 13 need a set-up row that needs a GPU, and the runner’s cpu mode
leaves such rows out. Two of the rows that need a GPU or the CUDA compiler have been run on this repository’s
own files, once, as far as their files were compared, and passed. In full: below the list.
• cp_verify , on the four stored examples, two at the reduced configuration and two at the default configuration’s
size; 20 altered sets of the two at the reduced configuration, ten of each; 57 proof files from malicious provers at
the default configuration’s size, made by test programs that this repository cannot build, and replayed on the
released cp_verify, beside 47 honest proofs at the default configuration’s size and 5 controls; 15 bad parameter
files; 13 small proofs on synthetic weights, from a test program that cheats, which are not in this repository:
2 of true claims and 11 false proofs; 2,720 kept calls (a kept call is a command line and its files, stored with
the ending that other programs gave it, and replayed), among them 228 calls, at the small example’s setting,
on altered stored tables; planning calls at 15 configurations, of which one was read against the figures of the
release’s pages (12 of the 15 are in this repository), and three more planning calls told here; and 21 made-up
settings, each a set of files in which one file is changed.
All four stored examples accepted (two with YES, two with WITHIN LIMITS), and all 20 altered sets rejected.
All 47 honest proofs accepted. All 57 proof files from malicious provers rejected, and all 5 controls (4
NO: reply-differs, 1 NO: model-statement). All 15 bad parameter files refused (10 NO: parameter-range
(policy), 4 NO: parameter-group-shape (policy), 1 NO: parameter-table-shape (policy)). Of the 13
small proofs the 2 of true claims were accepted (YES) and the 11 false proofs rejected (NO: proof-refused), as
expected.
Of the 2,720 kept calls 2,672 ended as recorded, in exit code and last line, the 228 among them, which cp_verify
accepted. Each of the 228 is on an altered table that, by separate programs’ counts of the changed columns, lies
far inside the distance at which it decodes to the honest weights alone, so that the accepted request and output

100

are the honest run’s own. That rests on those counts: no decoder was run, nothing of it is machine-checked, and
BLAKE2b-256 is taken as collision-free.
The counts measure from the table of the run with nothing altered; that this table holds the honest codewords
was checked at all positions under one of the four test seeds only, under which 24 of the 57 sets of files behind
the 228 stand (these 57 are other files than the 57 proof files from malicious provers). In 72 others of these calls
cp_verify passed a check of one part on a key file or a copy’s file whose own table was altered; a passed check
of one part is not an acceptance (row SO-15.7).
The other 48 read parameter files that the released programs refuse, and end with NO: parameter-range
(policy): they show nothing beyond that refusal. No call was more accepting than recorded. All 21 made-up
settings were refused, the one with answers 2 with NO: parameter-range (policy). The planning calls at the
15 configurations all ended with exit code 4 (PLAN ONLY): a planning call judges no proof. The three others told
here ended with exit codes 1, 1 and 2, as written down beforehand; on which files they were made this paper
does not tell. The one that was read against the release’s pages printed the figures that they give as its own.
• Tests of weight hiding (Section 7.5 and Appendix H.5). The first and the fifth of the five tests (row LIM-63.2,
lines 1 and 5), under exact match, on all three of the released programs, each commitment under a seed that
cp_commit drew. Both at reduced configurations, the first three times and the fifth once. The first also at the
default configuration, once on synthetic weights and once, as fitted to them, on the public weights. Both audits
also under a clipped-gap policy, at the reduced configuration, on two proofs. One of the two audits, the audit of
a commitment, also at the default configuration’s size, in the timed runs.
Each ended as written down beforehand. The first: all published OOD answers were the row’s weighted sum
plus the public factor times the row’s own pad; no pad came twice, and none was 0. The fifth: both audits
(checks, with the provider’s secrets, of a commitment and of a kept batch) accepted the honest files, and with a
false file in turn for each of three kept files the answer was no each time. The figures: row LIM-63.2, lines 1 and
5. Under the clipped-gap policy both audits accepted the honest files, and cp_verify accepted both proofs as
within the policy’s limits.
• The example script, examples/maverick/run.sh, at the default configuration’s size, on the public weights;
all the stages of run.sh all, in their order, on one H100; the build stage was skipped: the programs were
built beforehand from these sources; the download stage fetched nothing: the five weight files were laid there
beforehand at their checksums; and the toolkit was installed beforehand by that page’s typed lines, so building,
downloading and installing the tools were not exercised.
Exit code 0. The five values in examples/maverick/expected.txt came out the same, byte for byte, and the
commitment ID was the stored one; cp_verify ended with YES. By the run’s own record, which two readers
read apart, it changed no file of the repository.
• The timed runs, told under their own heading, below.

The public tests, in full. This part gives in full what the list’s item on the public tests gives in short.

The four test runs. For 172 of the 173 rows, on programs that each test run built from these sources: 169 passed, one
passed in part (tools.confine: of its check “a device node” the part on the character device 0:0 was not run on that
machine), two were not run (verifier.clang_build: no clang++ on the machine; tools.lean_verify.full_size,
the one row that would give a proof to a Lean verifier: the Lean library was not built), none failed; all on synthetic
weights, in four test runs, which ran the tests by name.
The 173rd row, verifier.rejected_proofs, ran in a test run of its own on a verifier built from these sources, on
a CPU machine, and passed. Of the 172 rows nine start cp_commit or cp_prove in the production build and 20 others
start the production cp_verify alone; the rest run test programs, copies, the tests’ own programs or scripts, only
build, or only read files. The four test runs ran in a tree that holds files that this repository does not hold, and that
differs from it in some tests and scripts (described under “What these runs do not show”, at “The public tests”).
The rows that need a GPU or the CUDA compiler. Nearly every public row that needs a GPU or the CUDA
compiler compiles the provers’ sources, and so reads files that differ as “Which programs ran” describes; beyond
that: gpu.forest_lookup_test and build.forest_lookup_test (their test, in code), gpu.coins_own_gpu_test and
build.coins_own_gpu_test (one printed message of their test), gpu.quick_start (the page that it reads), and
gpu.serving_first_run and gpu.coin_contexts.services.not_full (they run driver/spool.py, in which comments and docstrings alone differ); each of them ran, in the four test runs, under a runner that differs as described
under “What these runs do not show”, at “The public tests”.
The tests’ own files that differ in comments, names and printed texts alone are described there too.
build.forest_lookup_test and gpu.forest_lookup_test have been run once on this repository’s own files, as far as
their files were compared, by their rows’ own commands, apart from the four test runs, and passed; from which build
the checking program of gpu.forest_lookup_test came is not shown. No other row that needs a GPU or the CUDA

101

compiler has been run on this repository’s own files. The builds for the comparison described under “What a reader
can check” were made apart from these rows.
This repository’s files alone. Apart from the four test runs, most of the rows to which SUITE.md gives the kind
cpu or long (107 of the 173: the rows that themselves need neither a GPU nor the CUDA compiler) were run on
a machine with no GPU, each row confined by the runner. On this repository’s files alone, without lean/, the
runner was asked in one round for all 105 rows of the kind cpu: 92 were run to their end, and 90 of them passed.
That round ran on files that differ from this repository’s in 47 paths: 38 pages, 4 tables, paper/paper.pdf, the list
tools/lean-verify/native-modules.txt and 3 licence and notice files. No source, test or script is among them.
The 90 are all the rows of the kind cpu except weight_sums, tools.confine and the 13 described below;
verifier.clang_build, which the four test runs could not run, is one of them. weight_sums and tools.confine
passed in part. Of weight_sums 13 steps were skipped without two Python packages; run with both installed it passed,
with no step skipped. Of tools.confine one check was not run, “as root, another account’s file in /dev/shm”, since
the test already ran inside a row that the runner had confined as root. Sixteen rows were also run on files that differ
from this repository’s in 30 paths, of the same kinds, and all passed; integer_model.cpu (below) is one of them.
Which rows read a path that differs has not been traced. Neither the round nor these sixteen ran on this repository’s
files as they are.
Rows left out or not run. On this repository’s files alone the runner left out 13 rows of the kind cpu whose name
begins verifier. (those that need setup.target.other_parameters_worlds or its .work): each needs a set-up row
that needs the CUDA compiler and a GPU. The rows that were run to their end and these 13 make up all 105 rows
of the kind cpu. Of the two rows of the kind long, integer_model.cpu passed on this repository’s files alone, in the
environment that SUITE.md names for it, and tools.lean_verify.full_size was not run: that tree held no lean/.
Its command, run once apart from the test runner, is described below, with the calls of the Lean verifier.
A row that can fail on a busy machine. driver.measure_rate has failed twice, once on this repository’s files alone
and once in another tree with the same driver/, each time on a machine under load: one of its timing tests found no
sample from its stand-in for nvidia-smi. Run again it passed both times, and in the round described above it passed
at once. That the load is the cause is not shown.
What no row runs. No row runs tools/accepts/accepts.sh, the real
tools/lean-verify/LeanVerifyWalk.lean (tools.lean_verify.script runs a stand-in for it),
integrations/vllm/integer_model/tests/lean_vectors/run.py, or make_vectors.py and CompareVectors.lean
beside it, whose runs the integer-model package’s pages describe.

What these runs do not show.
• The public tests. Most rows run the tests’ own programs or scripts, or only build, and start none of the three
released programs. By a reading of the commands of the 172 rows, not a trace, nine rows start cp_commit or
cp_prove in the production build, and 32 others start cp_verify: ten of these a copy of it that the folder of a
test program’s build holds, and two a sanitizer build of it.
Of the public tests eight rows run cp_commit or cp_prove as test programs built from these sources. Those are
not the released programs, and such rows are told as of test programs. Two of the eight are also among the nine.
Thirteen further rows read or judge files that two of these eight made. In each GPU test run the two digests
were found among the records of its production and verifier builds; test programs are tied to no digest. One
production build is tied to none: gpu.quick_start builds its own programs in a copy of the tree.
Each test run ran named rows (--only), so the commands that SUITE.md prints were not used. The tree that
the rows ran in holds 473 files that this repository does not hold, nearly all of them tests, and no lean/. Of this
repository’s 1,218 files outside lean/, paper/paper.pdf apart, 880 stand the same, byte for byte, in that tree.
The others are 97 pages and tables, three of them tables that tests read; nine files that stand at another path; 129
files of the provers’ sources, which differ as “Which programs ran” tells; 12 files of the provers’ tests, in comments,
names and printed texts as reported, and one of them, prover/tests/forest_lookup_test.cu, in code; 52
other tests; the runner, run_suite.py, in a comment; eight files of the serving scripts and their tests; one file
of tools/lean-verify/, in which a comment alone differs; one list, tools/lean-verify/native-modules.txt,
which names the Lean modules that the command runs compiled: it was drawn anew; of the rows that
were run none takes a module’s name from it (tools.lean_verify.script lays a stand-in list of its own);
one script each of tools/accepts/ and examples/rejected-proofs/; eight tests and four sources of the
integer-model package; four files under docs/format/ and tools/lean-verify/, in comments, docstrings and
two printed headings; three licence and notice files, one of which that tree lacks; and seven others: three
figures.txt, examples/maverick/run.sh, examples/quickstart-gpu.sh, scales/make_calibration_set.py
and toy/mask_rules.py.
By a comparison of tokens, of the 52 other tests 35 differ in comments and docstrings alone, one in a name too, 11 in
string literals too, two are text or data, and three differ in code (integrations/tests/test_ask_and_check.py,

102

test_end_to_end.py and test_spool.py beside it); of the eight files of the serving scripts and their tests two
differ in comments and docstrings alone and six in code (integrations/proof_middleware.py and five tests or
helpers; the runs of the middleware’s check, told under the timed runs, ran another text of that script, and in
what it differs is told there); the scripts of tools/accepts/ and examples/rejected-proofs/ differ in code; of
the integer-model package’s files and the seven others none differs in code.
A string literal is not always only printed: some are values that a test compares, or lines that are written into
a file. Where a test or a script differs in code, what passed in those test runs is the text that ran there, not
this repository’s. prover/tests/forest_lookup_test.cu is the test of the rows build.forest_lookup_test
and gpu.forest_lookup_test. Apart from those test runs, tools/accepts/, the released script and its table of
calls, by their checksums, ran at the reduced configuration alone over the released Lean files: all 4 calls of Lean
ended as expected, and all 17 controls were refused. It runs single parts’ checks, not the Lean verifier.
A row that reads pages read that tree’s. A row that reads the stored examples read them as they lie in this
repository. Three rows ran a second time in one test run, and passed both times. Two rows passed with some
of their cases skipped, as their entries in SUITE.md allow (integrations.unit, integer_model.cpu). The two
rows setup.target.other_parameters_worlds and setup.target.other_parameters_worlds.work made 17
sets of files under other parameters (the tests call them worlds); the set-up leaves 3 out. Of each row its verdict
line was read, not all that it printed.
The row gpu.frame_readings, one of the 169, compares the prover’s and the verifier’s readings of the public
files. In the four test runs it ran over more folders than SUITE.md names; over the eight that it names it passed,
at reduced configurations, in a test run of its own, on another machine, on the tree that the rows ran in. At the
default configuration’s size, on four folders (two different configurations), on synthetic weights, it ran in a test
run of its own, in a form that SUITE.md does not have, on one H100, and ended as expected. What failed in
other runs of these tests: row T45.
• cp_verify. The kept calls say nothing of a byte that is hashed on a path that none of them takes: a change
there would not show. At the kept refusals the replay is blind: those calls end just as a changed digest would
end them. Of the runs on the 57 files, each run’s exit status, the number of its lines on standard output and its
last two lines, cut at 120 characters, were kept; a refused run prints no build word.
Twenty-two of the 57 proof files from malicious provers were tried once each, and all of the twenty-two claim the
true output. Of the 57, 24 claim a false output and 33 the true output with a lie inside, as reported. All 57
proof files from malicious provers stand at one commitment and at exact match, for three requests. The stored
files were checked, not made, and which programs made them is not traced. The altered sets are honest files
with a few bytes changed, not the work of a malicious prover.
How narrow the tests with proof files from malicious provers are, and that the falsifications of those twenty-two
were not aimed at the lookup checks: guide/known-limits.md. Of the other 35, at least three, by their makers’
labels (the 24 made for false outputs have records that name no kind), aimed at a lookup (a key in no row;
a count at another row) or at a digit’s range; these three were rejected at the check that the proof’s masked
message fits the linear claim, before any check of a lookup or a range was reached, so no rejection at the lookup
checks themselves has been seen (as reported; row T1.1).
• Tests of weight hiding. Neither test shows that the masks hide (each test’s bounds: row LIM-63.2, lines 1 and 5).
No run or comparison told in this subsection shows what the programs leave in host or GPU memory (rows H39.1
and HW-748). On the released programs, of the audits with the provider’s secrets, the audit of a commitment
alone ran at the default configuration’s size: in the timed runs, behind each of four honest commitments, one of
them under a clipped-gap policy: by the runs’ own account it accepted all four. At the default configuration’s
size the fifth’s two audits have run together only on other programs (below). An audit’s yes to honest files is no
negative test. At the reduced configuration the false files stood for kept files: no altered public file was put to
the audits.
With the provider’s secrets no audit has run on a commitment to a real model’s weight files: by a reading of the
code, these programs do not let it run there (row HW-747). Under the clipped-gap policy the audits at the
reduced configuration show that the masks are there as derived: that is no search for a leak; in that run the
batch’s two requests have one and the same output, and no false proof was put to cp_verify (row H13.8). Of
the five tests behind the judgement on weight hiding these two ran on the released programs (docs/limits.md).
The other three, and most of the readings behind that judgement, were made on other programs, so it reaches
the released programs on less evidence, and it does not reach code that differs from the released programs’.
• The example script. One run, of the one example request of examples/maverick/README.md, on one card, with
no batch. It neither built the programs nor downloaded the weights. The commitment is the published one,
made with -public-weights and this repository’s public seed: nothing under a secret seed was tried. A program
typed that page’s lines, and a reader’s machine may differ in CPUs, memory and disk. The script that ran
differs from this repository’s in comments, in its usage text and in some messages, and in nothing else.

103

The calls of the Lean verifier. The command lean-verify, which evaluates the Lean verifier, is not one of the

released programs (Appendix H.3; lean/THEOREM.md, “What has been run”). It ran over the released Lean files. It was
called on proofs at the default configuration’s size, which are inputs made beforehand; 109 calls, on synthetic weights:
47 honest proofs (42, as reported, made by programs built from other sources; the 2 stored examples at the default
configuration’s size, whose maker is not traced; 3 by test programs), 57 proof files from malicious provers, which test
programs made, and 5 controls, each an honest proof beside a claim that is not its own.
All 47 honest proofs accepted, 45 at exact match and 2 under the stored clipped-gap policy, where an accept says
less. None of the 57 proof files from malicious provers accepted: 54 rejected, and at 3 the command’s parser refused
the file before Lean was called, which is no verdict and no rejection. All 5 controls rejected.
• lean-verify, 41 calls more: proofs at the default configuration’s size; 14 requests, 7 of them also among those
of the 47, at 3 commitments; 38 proofs for one next token, 3 for 1,001 tokens.
41 proofs, as reported of the timed runs, all on synthetic weights and none of them among the 42 told above,
have been put to the Lean verifier, over the released Lean files; it accepted each (lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md).
• lean-verify, nine calls more: a small stored example beside altered parameter files.
Each ended at the parser with no verdict, as written down beforehand.
• The command of the public test tools.lean_verify.full_size, run once apart from the test runner, beside the
109 calls: the proof of the stored example examples/full-size/ (on synthetic weights), which is one of the 2
stored examples among the 47; over the released Lean files.
Exit code 0 and “VERDICT: ACCEPT (exact match)” (lean/CHECK-RESULTS.md). Through the runner that
row has never reached Lean in a run that this paper tells; whether it can end in an accept there, under the
runner’s own HOME and confinement, is untested (SUITE.md names the obstacles).
• cp_commit, cp_prove, cp_verify and lean-verify, one behind the other: one commitment, under a seed that
cp_commit drew, and one proof at the default configuration’s size (the second call of cp_prove; the first was
refused for a fault in the testers’ command), for 1,001 tokens, on synthetic weights, made, as reported, by the
cp_commit and cp_prove that this repository’s sources build; judged over the released Lean files.
cp_verify ended with YES, and the Lean verifier accepted the proof: exit code 0 and “VERDICT: ACCEPT
(exact match)”, as written down beforehand. It is one proof of one request: it says nothing of other requests, of
proof files from malicious provers or of a real model’s weights.
What these calls do not show. These calls were made over the released Lean files. This part speaks of the 109 calls,
the 41, the nine and the run of the public test’s command; the call on the one proof at the default configuration’s size
in the item on cp_commit, cp_prove, cp_verify and lean-verify above is described at its end. The command and
the parser were held by checksums before and after the calls; every file of the command and of the parser outside
lean/ that the traced calls beside them opened has this repository’s checksum (none of the run’s own calls was traced:
lean/GAPS.md, 14, “Which programs (k)”).
The outcomes were known beforehand, from other calls, over other Lean files, and those written down beforehand
were these. The program cp_verify was not called in these 109 calls: the script that made the calls calls the command
alone, and traces of one small call and of one call at the default configuration’s size, neither of them among the 109,
show no start of that program. Its source file is compiled into the parser’s unit. The same 109 sets of files were also put
to the released cp_verify, as reported: it accepted the 47 honest proofs and rejected the 57 proof files from malicious
provers and the 5 controls.
The sets were paired afterwards, by the label, the policy and the first 16 hex digits of the proof file’s checksum; the
other files of a set were not paired. The 42 told above are, as reported, 41 of the 363 at exact match and the one under
a clipped-gap policy that guide/what-is-proved.md tells. They were taken by rules fixed beforehand and have little
variety: as reported, the 42 are of 38 requests and 2 shapes (38 proofs for one next token, 4 for 1,001 tokens), at 4
commitments.
All proofs of these calls at the default configuration’s size are at one model size: each set’s three configuration files
are those of one of the two stored examples at that size, which part in three lines of the parameter file alone. Who
made a file, of the 109 or of the 41, on which weights, by which rules it was taken, and that it is honest or from a
malicious prover, is as reported: it rests only on the word of those who made and chose them. None of the 57 proof
files from malicious provers and none of the 5 controls was put to the command under the clipped-gap policy.
All calls ran on one machine, beside other work. By the command’s text the natively compiled part and the
parser’s unit are each compiled by one compiler; what these two compilers print as their versions was read off that
machine after the calls. That the command and the parser read the files that the traced calls opened, and no others, is
not shown. At those of the 109 calls and of the 41 more that reached Lean the command loaded the compiled files of
the released Lean library.
By the word of those who made them and of those who keep them, a build of these Lean files made them from
nothing, in several calls of Lake; that they were compiled from these files is Lake’s own finding, not the run’s. The

104

checksums of the library’s own compiled files, taken before and after the 109 calls, the 41 more and the nine more, are
the same. The compiled files of the packages that the library imports, which that build did not make, were not held by
checksum at the calls.
This paper tells no comparison of two builds of these Lean files. The natively compiled part the command compiled
itself, in a call of its own before the 109, but for three objects of a package that lay in the tree before that call. The
natively compiled objects’ checksums, taken before and after the calls, are the same; each call links the library anew
from them, and that library’s checksum was printed only at the call that compiled them. In full: lean/GAPS.md, 14.
lean-verify evaluates the Lean verifier, natively compiled in part: that is a run, not a proof. Lean reads each
file’s parsed form, made by the command’s parser, which no theorem covers. The parser’s compiled code had the same
checksums at each of the 109 calls that reached it and at a control compiled apart; at the run of the public test’s
command (its item above), at another path, its checksum is another, and the same once names are struck.
The call on the one proof in the item on cp_commit, cp_prove, cp_verify and lean-verify above was held less
closely. As reported, the compiled Lean files of its folder had the checksum of the build described above before and after
that folder’s first call, which was another call; no checksum was taken after this one. That the Lean files, the command
and the parser of that folder are the released ones rests on the testers’ word alone. It ran on another machine.

The timed runs. These are the runs behind the figures of Section 7.2 and Appendix H.2 that carry no mark,

made on the released programs. They were made within three hours of one day, on H100 GPUs: six kinds of run at
the default configuration’s size, made 3 times each, each kind’s three on three machines (at the default configuration,
on the public weights as fitted to them: on the public weights (1) a commitment with one request, and services (2) at
24, (3) at 64, and (4) at 8 and 1 requests a batch; on synthetic weights (5) a commitment, its audit and services at 24;
(6) at that configuration with a clipped-gap policy: on the public weights, sessions with every output token checked
and like sessions that check none).
Each of the nine machines that served on the public files at the default configuration as fitted to them made its
own commitment first; three of the nine commitments were named beforehand as the runs behind the figures. The
audit in these runs is a check of a commitment with the provider’s secrets. One run each was made of the making of an
example under a clipped-gap policy, with an audit of its commitment, and of one service and one call at 64 on synthetic
weights, both on one commitment made for them on that machine, which was not audited; those last two failed.
On synthetic weights every proof was accepted, and every altered copy and call rejected. On the public weights, in
the four kinds of run at the default configuration as fitted to them, all 1,851 requests were served, and all 1,851 proofs
were accepted by the runs’ own calls of cp_verify behind the timed stretches (1,845 in the runs behind the figures;
six more, one on each of six further commitments); in the three runs of the services at 24 requests a batch, the only
runs on these weights with that test, all 249 altered copies and calls were rejected.
cp_verify rejected every one (exit code 1, a last line NO); six of the 240 fell at the test on a file’s first eight
bytes, three of the calls fell at the comparison of the proof’s next token with the caller’s and three at that of the
commitment’s ID, and for 234 copies and three calls the records keep only a name that covers tests of form and of
substance alike (for two of the 234 a name that places the fall in a chain inside a sub-proof, and no nearer), so at
which test each fell is not known.
Each of these 1,851 proofs is of the one token after the prompt, under exact match. So are the 720 proofs of the
services on synthetic weights; four more there, one on each of four commitments, cover 1,001 positions each: 1,000
tokens of the window are checked and the token behind it is stated; so does the clipped-gap example’s one proof, under
that policy.
In the sixth kind of run all 720 requests were served and all 720 proofs accepted by the runs’ own calls of cp_verify
behind the timed stretches: the 360 of the sessions with whole outputs checked check every output token of their
request, and passed within the clipped-gap policy’s limits, not by exact match; their control’s 360 check the last
position alone, and passed by exact match. The requests of both were 48 stored windows of 1,024 tokens, some sent
more than once.
The requests were, on the public weights, in the four kinds of run at the default configuration as fitted to them, by
each run’s own line, a set of 96 windows of 1,024 tokens, and at 24 and 64 a batch some were sent more than once; on
synthetic weights 96 windows of made-up tokens (row RES-6). The altered copies are honest files with a few bytes
changed, not the work of a malicious prover. The audits that were part of these runs are told under the tests of weight
hiding (Appendix H.5).
Whether a difference from a figure of another run comes from the programs is not shown: such runs were on
other machines. The scripts driver/spool.py and integrations/spool.py that ran differ from this repository’s in
comments and docstrings alone. The check by the vLLM integration’s middleware also ran in runs of its own, in
none of the timed runs, with the released cp_verify, on stored proofs at the default configuration’s size on synthetic
weights, as reported, called directly, with no inference engine and no HTTP server around it.
By itself, on a batch of 24, all 24 were released, which is no negative test: no false proof was put to it. Beside
a serving cp_prove, in one run, it read stored proofs, not that prover’s own (row RES-32). By the checksums that

105

these runs’ records print, the scripts that ran differ from this repository’s in comments, docstrings and the texts of
messages of integrations/proof_middleware.py alone; for one run the constant CHECK_THREADS was also set to 4
(row RES-32; a run behind none of this paper’s figures).

What came out badly, or otherwise than expected.
• On the released programs: 48 of the 2,720 kept calls of cp_verify end otherwise than recorded, all with exit
code 1 and NO: parameter-range (policy): they read parameter files that the released programs refuse.
• In the timed runs:
– Two runs at 64 requests failed, both on synthetic weights: a service of five batches of 64 in its first batch,
and one call of the prover on 64 requests at once. None of their 320 and 64 requests got a proof, the GPU’s
memory was nearly full in both (81,053 of 81,559 MiB at the largest sample, which is a reading of the
samples, not a line of the program’s), and no cause is shown; one run each, so no failure rate comes of it.
No batch of 64 has been proved on synthetic weights: every attempt on record failed, eight in all, six of
them on other programs. On the public weights such services ended well (rows T50 and RES-11).
– On the public weights the services at 64 requests a batch came to their end with 930 MiB of the card’s
81,559 MiB free at the fullest (80,629 MiB in use); in like runs on other programs: 866 MiB (row RES-11).
– With the start-up counted, one of the three sessions with whole outputs checked, at 24 requests a batch
under a clipped-gap policy, stays under 100 tokens per second (99.33; the others 103.94 and 103.79), and so
does one of the three sessions of their control (98.12; 102.47 and 102.54): both on one machine, the slower
in both sets. No cause is shown. Both ran at the default configuration as fitted to the public weights, but
for the three numbers of the clipped-gap policy in the parameter file, on stored outputs with no inference
engine; the rates with whole outputs checked, accepted under that weaker policy, cannot be set against the
project’s target of 100 tokens per second or the rates at exact match (row RES-12).
• On other programs† , in another set of runs that were timed, three of each of five kinds of run at the default
configuration (on the public weights: as fitted to them), no run reaches 100 tokens per second with the start-up
counted: 96.32, 97.21 and 96.43 on the public 4-bit weights (start-ups of 78.2 to 81.0 seconds), 97.90 ± 0.74 on
synthetic weights (97.10 to 98.56); in one of the runs on the public weights other work of the project stood on
the host through one service’s whole start-up, and the CPU pressure rose to 15.8 % of a session’s length (row
RES-33).
• On other programs† , in the first test’s run at the default configuration’s size on the public weights, which was
not made to be timed, cp_commit took 3,143.5 seconds, about 38 % longer than a like run on other programs
(2,277.0 seconds): a test script that is not in this repository bound the run to CPUs of the other NUMA node
than its GPU’s. In the same test on the released programs, not made to be timed either, bound to CPUs of the
GPU’s own node, on another machine of the same kind, it took 2,328.2 seconds; programs and machine differ
too, so whether the binding is the cause has not been tried.
Another run like the one of 3,143.5 seconds, also on other programs, took 3,169.9 seconds, against 2,369.76 to
2,374.11 in three runs of the same programs on the public weights, on other machines (row RES-46); on which
NUMA node that run’s GPU sat is not known. In the run on synthetic weights, also on other programs, on
CPUs of the GPU’s node, it took 2,248.7 seconds (a like run on other programs: 2,261.5) (row RES-9).
• On other programs no run reached 100 tokens per second with the start-up counted, at the default configuration
(on the public weights: as fitted to them) (row RES-33), two commitments took far longer than their like (row
RES-9), and one proof was longer than any of the timed runs’, with no cause shown (row RES-6).
• In the timed runs, and in one set of three runs on other programs, the audit with the provider’s secrets took
longer in each run than in any of another set of three on other programs, on other machines: a small shift
upward is neither shown nor ruled out, and no cause is named (the times in the timed runs, and the range of the
others: row RES-15).
• On other programs: some tests notice little of a fault in HasherT, the class that feeds and finishes BLAKE2b
(its source: docs/sources-notes.tsv; what that costs the self-checks: row E2.4). Faults were planted in a like
class. Of three, 12, 10 and 12 of 50 CPU rows noticed each in turn. Of three more, planted for the GPU’s code
alone, the comparison of host and GPU noticed all three, three tests noticed one, and two tests noticed none.
• On other programs, in other runs of public tests or of tests not in this repository: a GPU test that is not in this
repository failed on a wrong count in the test itself, with all its cases agreeing, and a runner marked two such
tests as failed, against wrong counts in its own table (row H1.4); one CPU row, integrations.unit, failed for a
cause in the test machine, and passed with that cause removed.

106

• Among the public tests: driver.measure_rate has failed on a busy machine, and weight_sums and
tools.confine passed in part (above).

What this paper tells of other programs. Other programs are programs that are not the released ones: built

from sources that differ from this repository’s in more than “Which programs ran” tells, and are not part of it, or
from these sources with other options (test programs). What a run found on them does not carry over to the released
programs; what a reading found carries over only as far as the differences were read (row HW-679). A fault found on
them may be in the released programs too.
The kinds of result below were got on other programs. Most bear the mark “not on the released programs” († ) or,
in Table 6, that of test programs (‡ ); a run told with no mark, and named neither in the list, nor in “The public tests,
in full”, nor among the calls of the Lean verifier, nor among the timed runs, is to be taken as one of them, but for the
test of row RES-39, the runs of the middleware’s check and the four runs told under “Which programs ran”.
A leak of weights found there is to be taken as holding of the released programs unless a page says otherwise. The
calls of the Lean verifier, and any run of tools/accepts/, that this subsection tells outside its list are told apart: over
which Lean files each ran stands at its sentence or at the head of the Lean verifier’s bullet. The judgement on weight
hiding is given of the released programs: two of its five tests have run on them, the fifth at the reduced configuration
alone (Section 7.5). It takes results on other programs as evidence, not as shown for the released programs; other code,
changed or built otherwise, it does not reach.
Many of the tests and readings that this paper reports were not made on the released programs, but on programs
whose sources differ from the released ones; some, the tests with malicious provers among them, on programs that also
held test-only code; and some on programs that differ from these in more code. The compiled programs differ too; the
machine code that differs is not shown equivalent and has not been read.
• Runs with malicious or faulty programs, and searches for attacks. No malicious prover was built or run against
the released programs. The 57 proof files from malicious provers of the list are files that test programs made
beforehand; here cp_verify alone ran on them. This repository cannot build such a prover: the code is not in it.
It does hold the functions that such a prover would use, which, by a reading, do nothing here (that their calls
leave no instruction in the host code is not shown), and test code that can inject a fault in every build but the
production one (row E29.1).
No public test builds or runs a prover under a switch that makes it malicious; some run a prover that argues for
something false, with code of the test’s own. verifier.forest_twin_test has no malicious prover. Eleven of
the twelve stored false files of examples.protocol_toy_false_files were made by builds that this repository
cannot make.
• Tests of weight hiding, but for the list’s: the second and the third of the five, on test programs built from other
sources, which hold the code that injects a fault; the fourth, on programs of other sources that hold test-only
code; the fifth’s two audits together at the default configuration’s size; and the audits by value that stand beside
the five. What each found, and what went unnoticed: row LIM-63.2 and docs/limits.md.
• Other replays of altered, crossed or spliced honest files (what they found: row SO-15.7).
• Nine planning calls at reduced configurations, eight on parameter files made up for the call and one on the
unchanged files of a small example of this repository: four were admitted, three refused, and two aborted at an
internal check; none read a key, a copy’s file or a proof (row SO-6.3.2). The released programs refuse a file with
answers other than 1, with NO: parameter-range (policy) (read in the code; run at 2).
• Every figure marked † . A † time, rate or memory peak is an estimate at most.
• Every run of the Lean verifier that this paper tells and this subsection does not, and the runs of cp_verify side
by side with a stand-alone program built from the text of the second verifier (Limitation G.23, Second verifier
text) (row L14), which this repository does not build as one.
• The vLLM integration’s runs, but for the check told under the timed runs (Section 7.4; integrations/LIMITS.md).
• Most readings of the code: a reading is of other sources, unless its line says otherwise.
Which refusals no test in this repository shows: SUITE.md. Which faults of hiding no test in this repository shows:
docs/limits.md.

What a reader can check. cp_verify prints a build word (build=), the SHA-256 of a list of checksums of at
least every file of this repository that it includes. A reader can compute this repository’s word from the sources, and
build a cp_verify that prints it; the word stands in examples/README.md. It covers sources alone, tells neither the
compiler nor the flags, and does not cover the rest of the provers’ sources, and a printed word shows only what a
program says of itself.

