• Two figures of Section 1.3, from the timed runs on the public weights at the default configuration as fitted to
them (n = 3). Each commitment had one copy: its key file and its copy’s file had 6.24 MB together (6,235,160
to 6,237,760 bytes); by Definition 4.7 a further or renewed copy would have a file of its own; no run here made
one. The rate with start-up counted is the 122,880 prefill tokens of five batches of 24 over the time from the
program’s start to the last release. That time takes in a start-up of 74.8, 73.1 and 72.6 seconds, run by run,
and a first batch longer than a steady gap between releases, so the rate depends on the number of batches: the
longer the service, the nearer it comes to the steady rate; in the timed runs no service of more than five batches
of 24, or six of any size, was run. In the timed runs the start-up at 64 requests a batch took 76.5 to 78.9 seconds.
• By a reading of other sources, not by a run, each call on a proof also makes the full check of the key file and of
the copy’s file, besides the proof’s own check, so 21.14 ± 0.03 seconds called with its option for one thread, on
the prover’s machine (n = 3) takes both in, at every call.
• Whether the CPU cores were reserved to a run is not shown: by each run’s own record the job ran on a set of 24
of its host’s 96 CPUs, under a hard limit of 26 CPUs, and every program printed that it was pinned to 24 CPUs
of its GPU’s NUMA node; beyond that the host’s other seven GPUs were in use when the machine was assigned
