H.4

Runs by policy (Table 5)

Under neither policy has the prover run as a service beside the engine: it was started after the outputs were served.
Serving a trained model with an ordinary engine and proving its outputs in one service has never run end to end;
ordinary serving and proving have run end to end only in scripted demonstrations at a reduced configuration on a
synthetic model. The release’s integrations/LIMITS.md and docs/results.md report further runs.

H.5

Tests of weight hiding (Table 6)

Definition H.1 (Weight hiding, judgement, honest run). Weight hiding is the goal that clients and the public
learn nothing about the weights beyond a declared list. The judgement of Section 7.5 is a grade that we give to a goal
of the programs, from the design, what is proved in Lean for the protocol, tests and code review, on a scale of four:
proved; likely holds; cannot say; does not hold. Likely holds means that in honest runs, with the provider’s operating
conditions and the judgement’s own conditions kept, no way to a leak beyond the list is known, while nothing is
proved for the programs. A run is honest if nobody has changed the provider’s programs and no injected, compiler or
hardware fault has occurred; a bug in the published sources is not excluded.
Outside the judgement of Section 7.5 are a real model’s weights, and what a fit to them writes (a parameter file, a
file of scales): only one of that judgement’s five tests has used any, once on the released programs and three times on
other programs, and it does not ask what they reveal.
On the released programs two of the five tests have run (the first at the reduced and at the default configuration,
there once on synthetic weights and once, as fitted to them, on the public weights; the fifth once, at the reduced one),
and both audits once more under a clipped-gap policy (below): every published out-of-domain answer that the first
recomputed was the row’s sum plus its own mask, and the shipped audits accepted the honest files. A reading of the
committer’s and the prover’s sources, once and fast, with nothing built or run, found no leak beyond the declared list
(Remark G.10, What a client of the programs learns); how many of the files so read differ in the released sources is
told below. Recovering weights from published files has barely been tried, and not on the released programs. Every
“stopped” was observed in a test program or, in the fourth test, in a program that held test-only code: no self-check
on randomness (a program’s recomputation of masked values) has been observed to report a failure in the released
programs, and it is assumed that the test programs and the released programs apply the masks in the same way.
• The first and the fifth ran on the released programs, the first also at the default configuration. The second, the
third and the fourth ran on test programs or on other programs alone (Section 7.5), the third and the fourth on
more than one set of such programs, with the same outcomes, the bad ones too. For the released programs that
judgement has less evidence than there is for the programs on which most of its tests ran (Appendix H.1). A
provider’s own build from the released sources comes under it only as a correct build: every build tested here is
our own. The second, the third and the fourth each have one fixed set of losses; a repeat of a test is no new
witness. The test programs of the second and the third do not ship.
What was read is the sources of programs other than the released ones: those of the committer and the prover
were read whole, once and fast, in a text of 384 files, without the C++ verifier’s own files, which both programs
of that text include. The released sources differ in code in 68 of the 384, the file with the routine that feeds
BLAKE2b its blocks and finishes the hash among them, and in 136 more in comments, names and the wording
of messages alone; two more of the 384 are not in the released sources; in the 68 the differences in code were
read for hiding, by difference alone, apart from renamed names and reworded messages, and the files were not
read whole again; that routine’s released code has been read for hiding twice, by readers working apart. For
many single faults the one net that the whole reading found is a verifier’s run on a response before anyone else
holds it; the prover makes no such run, and that reading did not take in the serving scripts (Limitation G.16).

112

• At the default configuration’s size two of the five tests have run, the first and the fifth; of the fifth, both audits
there on other programs alone, and the audit of a commitment also in the timed runs. One test of hiding has run
on the public weights: the first, once on the released programs and three times on other programs† , at the default
configuration as fitted to them. In the run on the released programs, on a commitment that the committer
made under a sampled secret seed, all 4,096 OOD answers of its first table and its one copy (Definition 4.6) were
as prescribed, no pad occurred twice or was 0, and the separate program found all 8 faults that it planted for
itself as controls. The 8 faults show that the program’s count on the pads can ring, not that each of its three
counts (equal pads; equal single field values; pads that are 0) could have: no fault tells them apart, and that the
program counts all three is read, not tested. This does not show that the masks hide, or what becomes of a
response that does not verify (the run was honest); the test reads under 2 % of the bytes of the key file and of
the copy’s file, and of the five tests this one alone has run on such weights.
At that size the shipped audit accepted, in the timed runs, four honest commitments of the released programs’
to synthetic weights (with the provider’s secrets the audit tools accept commitments to synthetic weights alone),
one of them under a clipped-gap policy, which is no negative test. On other programs both audits ran at that
size too, on synthetic weights: in one run they accepted the honest files and, in trial calls, rejected four of four
false secrets. On the released programs the fifth has run once under exact match, at the reduced configuration,
and the first three times at reduced configurations, on synthetic weights (1,536 answers of two commitments; 512
of one; 512 of one to a small synthetic model in files of the public weights’ form, at a configuration of its own,
whose parameter file that judgement does not reach), and twice at the default configuration: on a commitment
that their committer made to the public weights, at that configuration as fitted to them, and on one to synthetic
weights. On the released programs, of the audits with the provider’s secrets, the audit of a commitment alone
ran at the default configuration’s size, in the timed runs (Appendix H.1). On the released programs, beside the
first test, in each of its runs at the default configuration (on the public weights: as fitted to them), the audit
tool’s program, run without the provider’s secrets over copies of what is published alone (the key file, the copy’s
file and four proofs), reported nothing on them and ended with an error at each of three copies with one bit
changed; a changed bit is not a lost mask, and this is not a test of hiding. At reduced configurations it read four
honest sets of such files and reported nothing; no altered copy was put to it there. Here and in Table 6 “as
prescribed” means by the rules that the test carries; these were typed mainly from the release’s pages, in a text
that is not shown to be the released one.
• None of the five tests shows what an OOD answer reveals or an unnoticed loss exposes; far more samples of the
kind of the nine are covered by no self-check; the audits cover no served proof and do not remove the risk of a
lost mask.
• That judgement reaches a clipped-gap policy (in a parameter file that differs from the default configuration’s,
or from that of a reduced configuration at which it is given, in that policy’s three numbers alone, and at the
reduced configuration at which both audits ran under such a policy: In sum, below), on less evidence: audits
apart, no test of hiding ran under such a policy. Both audits ran under one: once on the released programs, at
the reduced configuration, on synthetic weights, on two proofs that each cover 32 output tokens. Every random
value that they compare by value (in the batch’s audit just under a fifth of the bytes) was what the secret seed
gives, they rejected three false secrets, and the C++ verifier accepted both proofs as within the policy’s limits.
By the audits’ own table such a proof has no kind of record that a proof at exact match lacks, so this is the
exact-match check passed on such proofs, not a check of anything that only such a policy has. It shows that
the masks are there as derived, and it is not a search for a leak (docs/limits-all-rows.tsv). The audit of
a commitment ran once more on the released programs, at the same reduced configuration and under such a
policy, in the making of a small example, and accepted the honest commitment. The reach rests on the design
(the released programs run one scored output head under every policy: Limitation G.1), on one reading aimed at
that policy’s path, made in the code of other programs and not repeated in the released code, and on these
audits. Lean’s theorem on zero knowledge is for exact match: under a clipped-gap policy no Lean theorem
covers what a whole proof reveals (what the answers themselves tell: Limitation G.21). Also untested: batches
that overlap in time, the prover’s default; several clients’ proofs together; out-of-range prompts.
• One finding, in which no leak was shown, as far as its account goes: the number of events in a reply streamed by
vLLM (integrations/LIMITS.md).
In sum: that judgement is given at the default configuration and at the reduced configurations of its tests, but
for that of the small synthetic model in files of the public weights’ form (above). It reaches, on less evidence, a
configuration that differs from one of these in a clipped-gap policy’s three numbers alone, and the reduced configuration
at which both audits ran under such a policy, which also has a scale file of its own. It covers no other configuration, so
none fitted to a real model’s weights, no weight file with a tensor of 8 or 16 bits (Limitation G.17, Tensors of eight
or sixteen bits), and not the mode for a public seed, which hides nothing (Limitation G.22, Unmarked public-seed
mode). A provider that serves for long enough, or on a second GPU, needs a further commitment to the same weights

113

(Limitation G.12, Further commitments). The judgement of Section 7.5 takes that in, on thin ground, whether the
commitments follow one another or serve at once on several GPUs, on five conditions.
1. Each commitment comes from its own call of the release’s committing program, on a machine that was not
cloned or restored, and samples its own secret seed.
2. Each commitment takes nothing from another.
3. The configuration files stay the same.
4. Each commitment stays within its limit (Limitation 8.4, Opening budget).
5. Nothing hidden may choose which commitment serves a batch. One exception: the commitment may be picked
by load or queue length. That can depend on hidden values, and the pick gives away something of time and
load, which the release declares as visible.
Each commitment also meets that judgement’s other conditions. So two commitments to the same weights under
different configuration files, for instance under two acceptance policies, may each be inside alone, but not together.
By design, one commitment already has several tables of the same weights (Definition 4.6), each with its own
randomness and OOD answers. Brief readings of parts of the host code, all of them of the code of other programs,
found no way in which two commitments tell more than one; none of them covered the GPU kernels, except the
two kernels that add the pads to a table and to its columns. Not on the released programs, a count over two small
commitments found no derived random value twice, a check for gross faults only.
No test has searched two commitments together for leaks, and Lean has no statement on hiding for more than one
commitment. So the thin ground is the design, these readings and that count.
A further commitment resets only the count of opened columns: each proved request’s share of any bound on
hiding, and what the served-or-failed answers give, run on across commitments. That judgement on weight hiding does
not cover a commitment that is in doubt: one of which a count, a key or a directory may not be what the provider’s
record says (the list: docs/operators-duties.md). The one that replaces it may be inside alone, on conditions of its
own, but of these weights as a whole that judgement says nothing. It does not cover what the one before may already
have shown.
The release’s docs/what-is-not-hidden.md gives the declared list (Definition H.1), and docs/limits.md the
provider’s operating conditions (Limitation G.16, Operating conditions), that judgement’s own conditions and its
grounds; docs/how-hiding-is-checked.md and docs/limits.md give further tests and bad results.

H.6

Negative tests (Table 7)

No malicious program has been built from the released sources or run against the released prover and committer.
The malicious provers and committers of Table 7 are test programs, not the released ones, some of them edited for
a test; they do not ship, so the rows that use them cannot be repeated from the release. No reported test uses a
searching prover, or any malicious prover on real weights. Most tests are weak, at reduced sizes and on synthetic
weights: evidence, not a guarantee, resting on no Lean theorem about the programs.

H.7

The integer model

On 544 of 544 windows the range checks of the integer-model package (not on the released programs) found none
failing at the public weights. Besides the runs of Section 7.2 and Section 7.4, this count outside Lean is the only
evidence reported here that instances of a trained model can be served (Remark D.5, No real weights shown).

